    <div type="book" osisID="2Tim">
      <title>2 Timothy</title>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="2Tim.1">
        <title>2 Timothy 1 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre and occasion:</hi> This is a letter from Paul to Timothy, written from prison in Rome. The letter's circumstances are markedly different from those of 1 Timothy. In 1 Timothy, Paul had departed for Macedonia and expected to return to Ephesus relatively soon (1 Tim 3:14, "I hope to come to you soon"). The tone of 1 Timothy was official and institutional — a mandate letter authorizing Timothy to manage the Ephesian community during a crisis of false teaching. In 2 Timothy, the situation has deteriorated on every front. Paul is no longer merely absent; he is imprisoned (1:8, "me his prisoner"; 1:16, "my chain"; 2:9, "bound with chains as a criminal"). His imprisonment is not the relatively open house arrest described in Acts 28:30-31, where Paul received visitors freely and taught without hindrance; this confinement is severe enough that Onesiphorus had to search earnestly to find him in Rome (1:17). Paul's associates have largely abandoned him (1:15, "all who are in Asia turned away from me"; 4:10, "Demas, in love with this present world, has deserted me"; 4:16, "At my first defense no one came to stand by me, but all deserted me"). He is aware that his death may be near (4:6-8, "I am already being poured out as a drink offering, and the time of my departure has come"). The letter reads not as an administrative manual but as a personal, urgent appeal from a father who may be seeing his son for the last time.</p>
          <p>The genre of 2 Timothy shares features with what the first-century hearer would recognize as a testamentary exhortation — the farewell words of a dying figure to his successor. This genre has deep roots in Israel's scriptures. Jacob gathers his sons and blesses them before death (Genesis 49). Moses delivers his final addresses to Israel before dying on Mount Nebo (Deuteronomy 31-33). Joshua delivers a farewell charge (Joshua 23-24). David gives final instructions to Solomon (1 Kings 2:1-9; 1 Chronicles 28-29). In the Second Temple period, the genre was well-developed: the <hi type="italic">Testament of the Twelve Patriarchs</hi>, the <hi type="italic">Testament of Moses</hi>, and similar works present dying patriarchs reviewing their lives, warning their descendants, and committing their legacy to the next generation. The features of the genre include: the speaker faces death, reviews his life and service, gives final charges to successors, warns about future dangers, commends faithfulness, and expresses confidence about the outcome despite present danger. 2 Timothy displays all of these features. Yet it is also a genuine letter with personal details (Timothy's tears at their parting, 1:4; the request for cloak and books, 4:13; greetings to specific individuals, 4:19-21), practical instructions, and the epistolary framework of greeting and closing. It is a letter written in the shadow of death, and this shadow gives every word an urgency absent from 1 Timothy.</p>
          <p>The crisis that prompted 1 Timothy — false teaching in Ephesus — has not been resolved. It has, if anything, worsened. The desertion of "all who are in Asia" (1:15) suggests that the false teaching has won widespread adherence, or at minimum that the social cost of aligning with Paul has become too high for most to bear. The central question of the letter is no longer institutional management but personal courage: will Timothy stand firm, or will he join the many who have turned away?</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (2 Timothy 2):</hi> Chapter 2 opens with "You then, my child, be strengthened by the grace that is in Christ Jesus" (2:1). The conjunction <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("then, therefore") connects directly to the contrast that closed chapter 1: the many who turned away versus the one who remained faithful. In light of that contrast, Timothy is to be strengthened — not in his own resources but "by the grace that is in Christ Jesus." The instruction that follows develops the chapter 1 themes in three directions. First, the deposit of teaching must be transmitted: "what you have heard from me in the presence of many witnesses entrust to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also" (2:2). This extends the chain of custody from 1:13-14 — Paul gave the pattern to Timothy; Timothy must now pass it to reliable people who will pass it further. Second, suffering is the expected condition of faithful service, illustrated through three metaphors: the soldier who does not entangle himself in civilian affairs (2:3-4), the athlete who competes according to the rules (2:5), the farmer who works before he receives the crop (2:6). Third, Paul returns to the gospel itself: "Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, the offspring of David, as preached in my gospel, for which I am suffering, bound with chains as a criminal. But the word of God is not bound!" (2:8-9). The statement "the word of God is not bound" stands in pointed contrast to Paul's own chains — the messenger is imprisoned, but the message cannot be. Then comes a "trustworthy saying" that develops the shame-versus-faithfulness theme: "If we have died with him, we will also live with him; if we endure, we will also reign with him; if we deny him, he also will deny us; if we are faithless, he remains faithful — for he cannot deny himself" (2:11-13). The conditional structure makes faithfulness consequential: endurance leads to reigning, denial leads to being denied. This directly extends the choice set before Timothy in chapter 1.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 1 connects to what follows:</hi> Chapter 1 establishes the emotional, theological, and exemplary foundation for the entire letter. It begins with Paul's personal longing for Timothy and his affirmation of Timothy's genuine faith (vv. 3-5). It then moves to the central exhortation: do not be ashamed, but share in suffering for the gospel (vv. 6-8). The exhortation is grounded in a compressed statement of God's saving purpose and grace (vv. 9-10). Paul offers himself as a living example of unashamed suffering (vv. 11-12). The charge to guard the deposit (vv. 13-14) specifies what Timothy must protect. And the closing examples — mass desertion versus one man's faithfulness (vv. 15-18) — illustrate in concrete terms the choice before Timothy. Every subsequent section of the letter builds on this foundation. The three metaphors of chapter 2 (soldier, athlete, farmer) develop the suffering theme from 1:8. The warning against "quarreling about words" (2:14) and the description of Hymenaeus and Philetus (2:17-18) develop the false teaching crisis that is the backdrop to the desertion in 1:15. The character catalog of 3:1-9 ("in the last days there will come times of difficulty") develops the warning implicit in 1:15's mass desertion. The personal charge of 4:1-5 ("preach the word; be ready in season and out of season") develops the commissioning language of 1:6-8, 13-14. And the final testimony of 4:6-8 ("I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith") is the mature fruit of the confidence expressed in 1:12 ("I am not ashamed, for I know whom I have believed"). Chapter 1 is the seed from which the entire letter grows.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1.1-2Tim.1.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: The Greeting — Apostleship and the Promise of Life</title>
          <p>"Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God according to the promise of life that is in Christ Jesus, to Timothy, my beloved child: Grace, mercy, and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Lord."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Paulos apostolos Christou Iesou dia thelematos theou</hi>). As in 1 Timothy, Paul states his title immediately: <hi type="italic">apostolos</hi>, one sent with authority. But the grounding of the apostleship is expressed differently. In 1 Timothy 1:1, Paul was an apostle <hi type="italic">kat' epitagen</hi> — "by the command" — of God our Savior and Christ Jesus our hope. The word <hi type="italic">epitage</hi> ("command, order, decree") is the language of formal mandate, military commission, official dispatch. Here in 2 Timothy, Paul is an apostle <hi type="italic">dia thelematos theou</hi> — "through the will of God" or "by the will of God." The noun <hi type="italic">thelema</hi> ("will, desire, intention, purpose") is broader and more personal than <hi type="italic">epitage</hi>. It describes not merely an order issued but an intention held, a purpose determined. Paul's apostleship exists because God willed it — not because Paul chose it, not because a human council approved it, not because circumstances suggested it, but because the will of the living God stands behind it. The shift from "command" to "will" may reflect the letter's more personal and intimate register. In 1 Timothy, where the letter functioned as public authorization, the language of formal command was appropriate. Here, where Paul writes to his beloved child from prison, the warmer language of divine intention takes its place.</p>
          <p>The same phrase <hi type="italic">dia thelematos theou</hi> appears in Paul's greetings in 1 Corinthians 1:1, 2 Corinthians 1:1, Ephesians 1:1, and Colossians 1:1. It is Paul's most common way of grounding his apostleship. The consistency of the formula indicates that Paul understands his vocation as originating in and sustained by God's deliberate intention, not by accident, human approval, or personal ambition.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"According to the promise of life that is in Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kat' epangelian zoes tes en Christo Iesou</hi>). This prepositional phrase modifies either Paul's apostleship (he is an apostle oriented toward the promise of life) or the entire greeting (he writes in the context of the promise of life). The preposition <hi type="italic">kata</hi> with the accusative ("according to, in connection with, for the purpose of") indicates that the promise of life is the framework or aim within which Paul's apostleship operates.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">epangelia</hi> ("promise, pledge, undertaking") is one of Paul's most theologically loaded terms. In his usage, it almost always refers to God's promises to Israel — particularly the promise to Abraham. In Galatians 3:14, the blessing of Abraham comes to the Gentiles "so that we might receive the promised Spirit through faith." In Galatians 3:16-18, Paul argues that the promises were spoken to Abraham and his offspring (singular: Christ), and that the law, which came 430 years later, does not annul the promise. In Romans 4:13-16, the promise to Abraham and his offspring was received through faith, not through law. In Romans 9:4, the promises belong to Israel. In Romans 9:8-9, children of the promise are distinguished from children of the flesh. In Romans 15:8, Christ became a servant to the circumcised "to confirm the promises given to the patriarchs." In Ephesians 2:12, the Gentiles were formerly "strangers to the covenants of promise." The word <hi type="italic">epangelia</hi> thus carries the full weight of Israel's covenantal history: God's promise to Abraham, renewed through Isaac, Jacob, David, and the prophets, now coming to fruition.</p>
          <p>What is promised here is <hi type="italic">zoe</hi> — "life." The promise of life runs through Israel's scriptures as a persistent thread. In Deuteronomy 30:15-20, God sets before Israel "life and good, death and evil" and commands: "Choose life, that you and your offspring may live, loving the LORD your God, obeying his voice, and holding fast to him, for he is your life and length of days." In Psalm 16:11 (LXX 15:11): "You make known to me the path of life; in your presence there is fullness of joy." In Proverbs 3:18, wisdom "is a tree of life to those who lay hold of her." In Daniel 12:2, "many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life." In Ezekiel 37, the valley of dry bones receives life from God's breath — a promise of national resurrection. The "promise of life" is not a new concept introduced by Paul but the culmination of God's oldest and most persistent pledge to his people: I am the God of the living, and my purpose for you is life.</p>
          <p>This life is located <hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi> — "in Christ Jesus." The prepositional phrase defines the sphere in which the promise finds its fulfillment. The promise existed from of old, but its realization is in Christ. This connects directly to verse 10, where Christ is said to have "abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel." The promise of life announced in the greeting will receive its explanation in the body of the chapter.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To Timothy, my beloved child"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Timotheō agapetō teknō</hi>). In 1 Timothy 1:2, Timothy was addressed as <hi type="italic">gnesios teknon en pistei</hi> — "genuine/legitimate child in faith." The adjective <hi type="italic">gnesios</hi> emphasized legitimacy: Timothy was Paul's true spiritual offspring, not a pretender. Here, the adjective changes to <hi type="italic">agapetos</hi> — "beloved, dear, cherished." The shift from "legitimate" to "beloved" reflects the different register of this letter. Where 1 Timothy needed to establish Timothy's credentials before the Ephesian assembly, 2 Timothy speaks from the heart of a father to the heart of a son. The word <hi type="italic">agapetos</hi> carries the full weight of covenantal love: in the LXX, it translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">yahid</hi> ("only, beloved") and describes Isaac as Abraham's beloved son (Genesis 22:2 LXX, <hi type="italic">ton huion sou ton agapeton</hi>). At Jesus' baptism, the divine voice declares: "This is my beloved Son" (<hi type="italic">houtos estin ho huios mou ho agapetos</hi>, Matthew 3:17; Mark 1:11; Luke 3:22). The word is not casual affection but the deep, committed, covenantal love of a parent for a child.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Grace, mercy, and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Lord"</hi> (<hi type="italic">charis eleos eirene apo theou patros kai Christou Iesou tou kyriou hemon</hi>). The threefold greeting is identical to 1 Timothy 1:2. The addition of <hi type="italic">eleos</hi> ("mercy") to Paul's standard twofold greeting of "grace and peace" was noted in the analysis of 1 Timothy 1 as anticipating that letter's theme of mercy (1 Tim 1:13, 16). Here in 2 Timothy, mercy will again be prominent: Paul prays for mercy on the household of Onesiphorus (1:16) and for Onesiphorus himself on "that day" (1:18), and the theme of God's compassion toward the faithful amid suffering will pervade the letter. The greeting announces in compressed form what the letter will develop: grace (the unmerited gift of God's favor), mercy (God's covenant faithfulness shown to those in need), and peace (the restored relationship between God and his people) — all flowing from two sources, "God the Father" and "Christ Jesus our Lord."</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1.3-2Tim.1.5">
          <title>Verses 3-5: Thanksgiving — Ancestral Faith and Spiritual Heritage</title>
          <p>"I thank God whom I serve, as did my ancestors, with a clear conscience, as I remember you constantly in my prayers night and day, longing to see you, remembering your tears, so that I may be filled with joy. I am reminded of your sincere faith, a faith that dwelt first in your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice and now, I am persuaded, dwells in you as well."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I thank God whom I serve, as did my ancestors, with a clear conscience"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Charin echo to theo, ho latreuo apo progonon en kathara syneidēsei</hi>). The phrase <hi type="italic">charin echo</hi> ("I have thanks, I give thanks") parallels <hi type="italic">charin echo</hi> in 1 Timothy 1:12 ("I thank him who has given me strength"). The verb <hi type="italic">latreuein</hi> ("to serve, to worship, to render sacred service") in the LXX describes Israel's cultic service to God. It is the verb used in Exodus 3:12, where God tells Moses: "When you have brought the people out of Egypt, you shall worship (<hi type="italic">latreusete</hi>) God on this mountain." In Deuteronomy 6:13: "You shall fear the LORD your God and serve (<hi type="italic">latreuseis</hi>) him." In Joshua 24:15: "Choose this day whom you will serve (<hi type="italic">latreusete</hi>)." The verb carries the sense of devoted, exclusive religious service — the worship owed to God alone.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">apo progonon</hi> ("from ancestors, from forebears") is critical. Paul is asserting continuity: the God he now serves in the gospel of Christ is the same God his Jewish ancestors served. His faith in Christ is not an apostasy from ancestral religion but its fulfillment. This claim appears in Paul's defense speeches in Acts: "According to the Way, which they call a sect, I worship the God of our fathers, believing everything laid down by the Law and written in the Prophets" (Acts 24:14). "I stand here on trial because of my hope in the promise made by God to our fathers, to which our twelve tribes hope to attain" (Acts 26:6-7). The continuity claim is not incidental; it is foundational. For a first-century Jewish hearer, the charge that the Jesus movement had abandoned the God of Israel would be devastating. Paul answers it here in a single phrase: he serves the God of his <hi type="italic">progonoi</hi>, the ancestors, the ones who came before. The same God, the same service, the same line of faith.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en kathara syneidēsei</hi> ("with a clear/clean conscience") connects to one of 1 Timothy's central themes. In 1 Timothy 1:5, the aim of the charge was love from a pure heart and a "good conscience" (<hi type="italic">syneidēseōs agathēs</hi>). In 1 Timothy 1:19, Hymenaeus and Alexander rejected "good conscience" (<hi type="italic">agathēn syneidēsin</hi>) and made shipwreck of their faith. In 1 Timothy 3:9, deacons must hold the mystery of the faith "with a clear conscience" (<hi type="italic">en kathara syneidēsei</hi> — the identical phrase used here). Conscience in these letters is the inner moral witness, the capacity to evaluate one's own conduct before God. Paul's conscience is clean: he has not betrayed his calling, has not abandoned his commission, has not distorted the gospel. The man in chains has a clear conscience; the false teachers who walk free have rejected theirs.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"As I remember you constantly in my prayers night and day"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hōs adialeipton echō tēn peri sou mneian en tais deēsesin mou, nyktos kai hēmeras</hi>). The adjective <hi type="italic">adialeiptos</hi> ("unceasing, constant, without interruption") describes the frequency of Paul's remembrance of Timothy. The word appears also in Romans 1:9 ("without ceasing I mention you always in my prayers") and 1 Thessalonians 1:2 ("we give thanks to God always for all of you, constantly mentioning you in our prayers"). Paul's prayer life is characterized by persistent, unwavering intercession for those he loves. The phrase <hi type="italic">nyktos kai hēmeras</hi> ("night and day") uses the genitive of time to indicate the continuous span during which the praying occurs. In the confinement of prison, with activity restricted and freedom curtailed, prayer would be Paul's primary occupation.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Longing to see you, remembering your tears"</hi> (<hi type="italic">epipothōn se idein, memnēmenos sou tōn dakryōn</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">epipothein</hi> ("to long for, to yearn for, to desire earnestly") expresses intense personal desire. Paul uses the same verb in Romans 1:11 ("I long to see you"), Philippians 1:8 ("I yearn for you all with the affection of Christ Jesus"), and 1 Thessalonians 3:6 (where Timothy's report that the Thessalonians "long to see us" brings Paul comfort). The intensity of the word is not decorative; it conveys the ache of separation between two people who share the deepest bonds.</p>
          <p>The tears (<hi type="italic">dakrya</hi>) belong to Timothy. The perfect participle <hi type="italic">memnēmenos</hi> ("remembering, being mindful of") indicates that the memory of those tears is present and vivid in Paul's mind. Timothy wept — presumably at their parting, when Paul left him or was taken away. The mention of tears accomplishes several things at once. It establishes the depth of the personal relationship. It reminds Timothy (and the reader) that there was a time when the separation was unbearable, when the prospect of Paul's absence moved Timothy to open weeping. And it creates urgency: Paul remembers the tears and longs for the reunion.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that I may be filled with joy"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hina charas plērōthō</hi>). The <hi type="italic">hina</hi> clause states the purpose of Paul's longing: joy. The verb <hi type="italic">plēroun</hi> ("to fill, to make full, to bring to completion") in the passive describes joy that fills the whole person — not a partial or qualified happiness but a complete filling. Paul in prison wants one thing: to see Timothy and be filled with joy. The emotional texture of the letter is established in these verses: this is not an administrative communication but a cry from the heart.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I am reminded of your sincere faith"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hypomnēsin labōn tēs en soi anypokritou pisteōs</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">hypomnēsis</hi> ("reminder, remembrance, recollection") with the verb <hi type="italic">lambanein</hi> ("to receive, to take") means "having received a reminder" — something has caused Paul to remember afresh the quality of Timothy's faith. What triggered the reminder is not specified; perhaps a report, perhaps a memory, perhaps the Spirit's prompting.</p>
          <p>The adjective <hi type="italic">anypokritos</hi> ("sincere, unfeigned, without pretense") is the same word Paul used in 1 Timothy 1:5 to describe the faith that is the aim of sound teaching: <hi type="italic">pisteos anypokritou</hi> ("sincere faith"). The word is composed of the alpha-privative (<hi type="italic">an-</hi>) and <hi type="italic">hypokritos</hi> ("actor, pretender, one who wears a mask"). Faith that is <hi type="italic">anypokritos</hi> is faith without a mask — real, not performed, not adopted for social advantage. In the context of 2 Timothy, where many have turned away from Paul because of the cost of association, the emphasis on sincere faith is pointed. Those who abandoned Paul may have had faith that looked genuine when the cost was low; Timothy's faith is the kind that does not depend on favorable circumstances.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"A faith that dwelt first in your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hētis enōkēsen prōton en tē mammē sou Lōidi kai tē mētri sou Eunikē</hi>). The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">hētis</hi> ("which," qualitative: "which is of such a character that") introduces additional information about this sincere faith. The verb <hi type="italic">enoikein</hi> ("to dwell in, to inhabit, to make one's home in") personifies faith as a resident — something that takes up dwelling in a person and makes its home there. The same verb will appear in verse 14 for the Holy Spirit "who dwells in us" (<hi type="italic">tou enoikountos en hēmin</hi>). The parallel is deliberate: faith dwells in persons the way the Spirit dwells in persons — as an inhabitant, not a visitor.</p>
          <p>The adverb <hi type="italic">prōton</hi> ("first") establishes chronological priority: the faith dwelt first in Lois, then in Eunice, then (as Paul is about to state) in Timothy. This is three-generation transmission. The grandmother heard, believed, and the faith took up residence in her. The mother heard, believed, and the faith took up residence in her. The son heard, believed, and the faith took up residence in him. The pattern is not automatic — faith is not genetically inherited — but it is familial: the household was the primary context in which faith was transmitted in the first-century world.</p>
          <p>The names are Greek: <hi type="italic">Lōis</hi> is attested as a name among diaspora Jews, and <hi type="italic">Eunikē</hi> (from <hi type="italic">eu</hi> + <hi type="italic">nikē</hi>, "good victory") was common in the Greco-Roman world and used by Jewish families in the diaspora. Acts 16:1 confirms the family background: "He came also to Derbe and to Lystra. A disciple was there, named Timothy, the son of a Jewish woman who was a believer, but his father was a Greek." Timothy's mother was Jewish and a believer; his father was Greek (and, by implication, not a believer — the text does not call him one). The faith came to Timothy through the maternal line, through women who believed when the gospel first came to Lystra.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And now, I am persuaded, dwells in you as well"</hi> (<hi type="italic">pepeismai de hoti kai en soi</hi>). The perfect tense <hi type="italic">pepeismai</hi> ("I have been persuaded and remain persuaded") expresses a settled, confident conviction. Paul is not uncertain; he does not say "I hope" or "I suspect." He has been persuaded and stands in that persuasion. Yet the very need to state the conviction — combined with the exhortation to "fan into flame" that immediately follows in verse 6 — suggests that Timothy's faith, while genuine, may be under pressure. The same faith that was authentic and indwelling may be in danger of being dampened by fear, by the cost of association with a prisoner, by the mass desertion Paul is about to describe. Paul affirms what is true so that Timothy will have the courage to live it out.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1.6-2Tim.1.7">
          <title>Verses 6-7: The Gift Rekindled — Power Instead of Cowardice</title>
          <p>"For this reason I remind you to fan into flame the gift of God, which is in you through the laying on of my hands, for God gave us a spirit not of cowardice but of power and love and self-discipline."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For this reason I remind you to fan into flame the gift of God"</hi> (<hi type="italic">di' hēn aitian anamimnēskō se anazōpyrein to charisma tou theou</hi>). The phrase <hi type="italic">di' hēn aitian</hi> ("for which reason, on this account") connects back to the preceding affirmation of Timothy's sincere faith. Because Paul is persuaded that genuine faith dwells in Timothy, he now calls Timothy to act on it.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">anamimnēskein</hi> ("to remind, to call to remembrance, to bring back to mind") does not introduce new information but recalls what Timothy already knows. Paul is not teaching Timothy something he has never heard; he is reminding him of what he already possesses. The distinction matters: the problem is not ignorance but dormancy.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">anazōpyrein</hi> ("to fan into flame, to rekindle, to stir up the embers") is vivid. It is a compound of <hi type="italic">ana-</hi> ("up, again") + <hi type="italic">zōpyrein</hi> ("to kindle to life, to fan a fire"). The word <hi type="italic">zōpyron</hi> was used for the ember or coal kept alive to restart a fire. The metaphor is precise: the gift of God in Timothy is not extinguished — it still has life, like embers under ash — but it needs stirring, fanning, active attention to flame back to full intensity. The verb appears only here in the New Testament. In the LXX, the related concept appears in texts about fire that must be tended and not allowed to die out. Leviticus 6:12-13: "The fire on the altar shall be kept burning on it; it shall not go out... Fire shall be kept burning on the altar continually; it shall not go out." The priestly duty was to maintain the fire; letting it die was a failure of stewardship. Timothy has a similar duty: the gift of God requires active tending.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> ("gift, grace-gift, endowment") is composed of <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace") + the <hi type="italic">-ma</hi> suffix (indicating the result of an action). A <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> is a concrete expression of grace, a gift that embodies and conveys grace. In Romans 12:6, Paul lists the <hi type="italic">charismata</hi> ("gifts") given to the community: prophecy, service, teaching, exhortation, giving, leading, showing mercy. In 1 Corinthians 12:4, "there are varieties of gifts, but the same Spirit." The specific <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> Timothy received is not identified in this verse, but its context — the laying on of hands, the connection to ministry, the call to boldness in the gospel — suggests it is a gift related to his public commission for ministry.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which is in you through the laying on of my hands"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ho estin en soi dia tēs epitheseōs tōn cheirōn mou</hi>). The gift is located <hi type="italic">en soi</hi> ("in you") — it is not external to Timothy but indwells him. It came <hi type="italic">dia</hi> ("through, by means of") the laying on of Paul's hands (<hi type="italic">epitheseōs tōn cheirōn mou</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">epithesis</hi> ("laying on, placing upon") combined with <hi type="italic">tōn cheirōn</hi> ("of the hands") describes the ancient practice of commissioning through physical touch. The laying on of hands has a long history in Israel: Moses laid hands on Joshua to transfer authority and the spirit of wisdom (Numbers 27:18-23; Deuteronomy 34:9). In the early Jesus communities, hands were laid on people for various purposes: healing (Acts 9:17; 28:8), reception of the Spirit (Acts 8:17; 19:6), and commissioning for ministry (Acts 6:6; 13:3).</p>
          <p>A comparison with 1 Timothy 4:14 is necessary. There Paul wrote: "Do not neglect the gift you have, which was given you by prophecy when the council of elders laid their hands on you" (<hi type="italic">meta epitheseōs tōn cheirōn tou presbyteriou</hi>). In that passage, the gift came <hi type="italic">meta</hi> ("with, accompanied by") the laying on of hands by the <hi type="italic">presbyterion</hi> ("council of elders, body of elders"). Here in 2 Timothy, the gift came <hi type="italic">dia</hi> ("through") the laying on of Paul's hands (<hi type="italic">mou</hi>, "my hands"). The two descriptions are not contradictory; they describe the same event from different angles. Paul laid his hands on Timothy in the presence of the elders, who also participated. In 1 Timothy, where Paul was establishing Timothy's credentials before the Ephesian community, the corporate action of the elder body was emphasized. Here in 2 Timothy, where Paul writes personally to his beloved child, his own role in the commissioning is emphasized. The event was one; the perspectives are complementary.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For God gave us a spirit not of cowardice"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ou gar edōken hēmin ho theos pneuma deilias</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") introduces the ground of the exhortation: Timothy should fan the gift into flame because the spirit God gave is not characterized by cowardice. The verb <hi type="italic">edōken</hi> ("gave," aorist of <hi type="italic">didonai</hi>) points to a definite past event — a specific giving that occurred at a specific time, likely the same commissioning event just described.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> ("spirit, breath, wind") here could refer to the Holy Spirit whom God gives, or to the disposition or inner orientation that God produces. In the immediate context, where verse 14 will explicitly mention "the Holy Spirit who dwells in us" and where the gift is described as coming from God, the reference is most likely to the Holy Spirit — but not abstracted from the disposition he produces. The Spirit God gives is characterized by certain qualities, and these qualities become the believer's inner reality. The distinction between "the Holy Spirit" and "the spirit/disposition God gives" would not be sharp for a first-century hearer: the Spirit's indwelling is the source of the believer's new character.</p>
          <p>The genitive <hi type="italic">deilias</hi> ("of cowardice, of timidity, of fearfulness") describes the character that this spirit does not have. The noun <hi type="italic">deilia</hi> appears only here in the New Testament. In the LXX, the related adjective <hi type="italic">deilos</hi> ("cowardly, fearful, timid") appears in contexts of military failure. In Deuteronomy 20:8, before battle, the officers say: "Is there anyone who is fearful (<hi type="italic">deilos</hi>) and fainthearted? Let him go back to his house, lest he make the heart of his fellows melt like his own." The cowardly soldier is sent home because cowardice is contagious. In Judges 7:3, God tells Gideon: "Whoever is fearful (<hi type="italic">deilos</hi>) and trembling, let him return home." The military context is significant because the letter has already framed Timothy's task as warfare (1 Tim 1:18, "wage the good warfare"), and 2 Timothy 2:3-4 will develop the soldier metaphor explicitly. Timothy is not to be a warrior sent home for cowardice. He is to stand and fight.</p>
          <p>The word also carries weight in another direction. In the words attributed to Jesus in the Gospels, <hi type="italic">deilia</hi> or its cognates describe the failure of the disciples in moments of crisis. In Mark 4:40, after the storm on the sea, Jesus asks: "Why are you so cowardly (<hi type="italic">deiloi</hi>)? Have you still no faith?" Cowardice and faithlessness are linked: to be <hi type="italic">deilos</hi> is to fail in <hi type="italic">pistis</hi>. Paul's statement that God did not give a spirit of cowardice is therefore also a statement about faith: the Spirit God gives produces not faithless fear but faithful courage.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But of power and love and self-discipline"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla dynameōs kai agapēs kai sōphronismou</hi>). Three genitives describe the positive character of the spirit God gives:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Power</hi> (<hi type="italic">dynamis</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> means "power, ability, capacity, strength, might." In Paul's usage, <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> is characteristically the power of God at work in and through human agents. In Romans 1:16, "the gospel is the power of God for salvation." In 1 Corinthians 2:4-5, Paul's preaching was "in demonstration of the Spirit and of power, so that your faith might not rest in the wisdom of men but in the power of God." In 2 Corinthians 12:9, the Lord tells Paul: "My power is made perfect in weakness." The power that replaces cowardice is not human assertiveness or self-confidence but God's own operative energy working through a weak vessel. This connects directly to verse 8, where Timothy is to share in suffering for the gospel "according to the power of God" (<hi type="italic">kata dynamin theou</hi>) — the same power.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Love</hi> (<hi type="italic">agape</hi>). Love appears because the gospel demands action for the sake of others even when it is costly. Cowardice is self-protective; love is self-giving. The pairing of power with love prevents power from becoming mere force: power exercised in love serves others rather than dominating them. Love was the <hi type="italic">telos</hi> ("aim, goal") of sound teaching in 1 Timothy 1:5 ("the aim of our charge is love from a pure heart"). Here it is part of the character the Spirit produces in those who minister the gospel.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Self-discipline</hi> (<hi type="italic">sōphronismos</hi>). This noun appears only here in the New Testament. It is related to the <hi type="italic">sōphron-</hi>word group: <hi type="italic">sōphrosynē</hi> ("self-control, sound-mindedness, moderation," 1 Tim 2:9, 15), <hi type="italic">sōphronein</hi> ("to be of sound mind, to think clearly"), <hi type="italic">sōphronizein</hi> ("to bring to one's senses, to train in self-discipline," Titus 2:4). The <hi type="italic">-ismos</hi> suffix suggests the active process or exercise of <hi type="italic">sōphrosynē</hi> — the discipline of bringing one's mind to soundness, the act of self-correction that keeps a person from either panic on one side or recklessness on the other. Against cowardice, God gives not mere bravado but disciplined self-mastery — the capacity to assess a situation clearly and act rightly despite danger. The word captures the mental clarity that fear destroys: a coward cannot think straight, but the person with <hi type="italic">sōphronismos</hi> maintains sound judgment under pressure.</p>
          <p>The three qualities together compose the character required for ministry in dangerous circumstances. Power supplies the capacity to act. Love supplies the motivation to act for others. Self-discipline supplies the judgment to act rightly. Together they replace cowardice with courage that is both effective and wise.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1.8-2Tim.1.12">
          <title>Verses 8-12: The Call to Suffer Without Shame — Grounded in God's Saving Purpose</title>
          <p>"Therefore do not be ashamed of the testimony about our Lord, nor of me his prisoner, but share in suffering for the gospel by the power of God, who saved us and called us to a holy calling, not because of our works but because of his own purpose and grace, which he gave us in Christ Jesus before the ages began, and which now has been manifested through the appearing of our Savior Christ Jesus, who abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel, for which I was appointed a preacher and apostle and teacher, which is why I suffer as I do. But I am not ashamed, for I know whom I have believed, and I am convinced that he is able to guard until that day what has been entrusted to me."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Therefore do not be ashamed of the testimony about our Lord"</hi> (<hi type="italic">mē oun epaischynthēs to martyrion tou kyriou hēmōn</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("therefore, then, accordingly") connects this exhortation to the preceding statement about God's gift of power, love, and self-discipline. Because God gave these qualities rather than cowardice, Timothy should not be ashamed. The negative imperative <hi type="italic">mē epaischynthēs</hi> ("do not be ashamed") uses the aorist subjunctive, addressing a specific potential act of shame rather than an ongoing state. The verb <hi type="italic">epaischynesthai</hi> ("to be ashamed of, to be embarrassed by") will become the chapter's controlling word, appearing three times: here (v. 8), in Paul's statement about himself (v. 12, "I am not ashamed"), and in the description of Onesiphorus (v. 16, "he was not ashamed of my chain"). The threefold repetition creates the chapter's spine: shame is the temptation, and the letter presents three responses to it — Timothy is told not to be ashamed, Paul models not being ashamed, and Onesiphorus demonstrates what not being ashamed looks like in practice.</p>
          <p>The object of potential shame is <hi type="italic">to martyrion tou kyriou hēmōn</hi> — "the testimony about our Lord" or "the testimony of our Lord." The noun <hi type="italic">martyrion</hi> ("testimony, witness, evidence") describes the public declaration about Jesus Christ. In a Roman context, where the Lord the community confessed was a man executed by Roman authority, the "testimony" was socially dangerous. To testify about a crucified criminal as Lord was to invite ridicule, suspicion, and potential legal consequence. The temptation to shame was not merely emotional but practical: being associated with this testimony could result in arrest, imprisonment, or death.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Nor of me his prisoner"</hi> (<hi type="italic">mēde eme ton desmion autou</hi>). The negative <hi type="italic">mēde</hi> ("nor, and not") extends the prohibition: not only must Timothy not be ashamed of the testimony itself, he must not be ashamed of Paul, its imprisoned herald. The noun <hi type="italic">desmios</hi> ("prisoner, one in bonds") is modified by <hi type="italic">autou</hi> ("his") — Paul is the prisoner of Christ, not merely of Rome. The possessive genitive reframes the imprisonment: Paul is in chains not because Rome has captured him but because Christ's service has led him into chains. The one who put Paul there, in the deepest sense, is not the Roman magistrate but the Lord whose gospel Paul proclaims. The reframing matters because it transforms the social meaning of the imprisonment. A man in Roman chains was disgraced — a failure, a criminal, someone to be avoided. But if the chains are Christ's chains, then the disgrace belongs to those who imposed them, not to the one who wears them.</p>
          <p>The temptation to be ashamed of Paul was not hypothetical. Verse 15 will reveal that "all who are in Asia turned away from me." The social cost of association with a condemned prisoner was real, and many had already decided the cost was too high. Paul names this reality and tells Timothy to resist it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But share in suffering for the gospel by the power of God"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla synkakopathēson tō euangeliō kata dynamin theou</hi>). The strong adversative <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but, rather, on the contrary") introduces the positive alternative to shame. The verb <hi type="italic">synkakopathein</hi> ("to suffer together, to share in hardship, to endure affliction with") is a compound: <hi type="italic">syn</hi> ("together with") + <hi type="italic">kakos</hi> ("evil, bad, harmful") + <hi type="italic">pathein</hi> ("to suffer, to experience"). The word appears only here and in 2 Timothy 2:3 in the New Testament. Timothy is commanded not merely to endure his own suffering but to suffer with — to join Paul in the hardship that the gospel brings. The dative <hi type="italic">tō euangeliō</hi> ("for the gospel") identifies the cause: the suffering is not random or purposeless; it is for the sake of the good news about Christ.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kata dynamin theou</hi> ("by the power of God, according to the power of God") specifies the resource by which this shared suffering becomes possible. The preposition <hi type="italic">kata</hi> ("according to, in accordance with") indicates that the suffering is undergone within the sphere of divine power, enabled by it, sustained by it. Human capacity alone would not be sufficient to endure what Paul describes. But the power (<hi type="italic">dynamis</hi>) is God's — the same word that appeared in verse 7 as part of the spirit God gives. The connection is explicit: the spirit of power (v. 7) enables the sharing in suffering (v. 8). What God provides in the interior life (power rather than cowardice) is what makes the exterior life (suffering rather than retreat) possible.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Who saved us and called us to a holy calling"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tou sōsantos hēmas kai kalesantos klēsei hagia</hi>). The participial phrase identifies the God whose power sustains the suffering. Two aorist participles describe God's completed actions:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Saved</hi> (<hi type="italic">sōsantos</hi>, from <hi type="italic">sōzein</hi>, "to save, to rescue, to deliver"). The aorist tense treats the saving as a completed event — something that has already happened. The first-century hearer would understand this in terms drawn from Israel's history: God is the one who saves. The same God who rescued Israel from Egypt (Exodus 14-15), who delivered them from Babylon (Isaiah 43:1-3; 44:6; 45:17), who saved individuals from danger and death throughout the Psalms (Psalm 18:3 LXX [17:3]; 34:6 LXX [33:7]; 107:13 LXX [106:13]) — this God has now saved Paul and Timothy and all who are in Christ. The verb echoes the greeting's identification of God as the one from whom salvation flows, and connects to 1 Timothy 1:15 ("Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners") and the designation "God our Savior" (1 Tim 1:1; 2:3; 4:10).</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Called</hi> (<hi type="italic">kalesantos</hi>, from <hi type="italic">kalein</hi>, "to call, to summon, to invite, to name"). God has issued a summons. The calling is described as <hi type="italic">klēsis hagia</hi> — "a holy calling." The noun <hi type="italic">klēsis</hi> ("calling, summons, vocation") in Paul's usage describes God's effectual summons to faith and participation in his purposes. In Romans 11:29, "the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable." In 1 Corinthians 1:26, "Consider your calling, brothers." In Ephesians 4:1, "Walk in a manner worthy of the calling to which you have been called." In Philippians 3:14, "the upward call of God in Christ Jesus." The adjective <hi type="italic">hagia</hi> ("holy, set apart, sacred") characterizes the calling as belonging to God's sphere — a summons that separates the called ones from the common and brings them into God's own purposes.</p>
          <p>The pronoun <hi type="italic">hēmas</hi> ("us") is important. Who is the "us" God has saved and called? In the immediate context, Paul is addressing Timothy, so the most proximate referent is Paul and Timothy together. But the statement's scope is broader — it describes the fundamental experience of every person who has received the gospel. The "us" is not private but representative: Paul and Timothy are examples of the universal pattern of God's saving and calling action.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Not because of our works but because of his own purpose and grace"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ou kata ta erga hēmōn alla kata idian prothesin kai charin</hi>). This is a critical clarification. The saving and calling were not <hi type="italic">kata ta erga hēmōn</hi> — "according to our works, on the basis of our deeds." The preposition <hi type="italic">kata</hi> ("according to, on the basis of") identifies the ground or basis of the action. God's saving and calling did not respond to or originate from human achievement. The negative <hi type="italic">ou</hi> ("not") is emphatic: it was not works.</p>
          <p>The positive ground is stated with the same preposition: <hi type="italic">alla kata idian prothesin kai charin</hi> — "but according to his own purpose and grace." Two terms describe what motivated God's action:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Purpose</hi> (<hi type="italic">prothesis</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">prothesis</hi> ("setting forth, purpose, plan, resolve") is composed of <hi type="italic">pro</hi> ("before, ahead") + <hi type="italic">thesis</hi> ("placing, setting"). It describes something set in place beforehand, a plan established in advance. In Paul's usage elsewhere, the word consistently describes God's sovereign plan. In Romans 8:28, those who love God are "called according to his purpose" (<hi type="italic">kata prothesin</hi>). In Romans 9:11, "in order that God's purpose according to election might continue to stand" (<hi type="italic">hē kat' eklogēn prothesis tou theou</hi>) — here the purpose is what governs the election of Jacob over Esau, "not because of works but because of his calling." In Ephesians 1:11, God "works all things according to the counsel of his will" (<hi type="italic">kata tēn prothesin tou ta panta energountos kata tēn boulēn tou thelēmatos autou</hi>). In Ephesians 3:11, there is "the eternal purpose" (<hi type="italic">kata prothesin tōn aiōnōn</hi>) that God has realized in Christ Jesus. In every instance, <hi type="italic">prothesis</hi> refers to God's deliberate, premeditated plan — a purpose that originates in God's own initiative and governs the course of events.</p>
          <p>The adjective <hi type="italic">idian</hi> ("his own, personal, private") intensifies: this is God's own purpose, internal to God, not suggested or necessitated by anything outside God. The plan did not originate in human achievement, in cosmic necessity, or in the movement of history. It originated in God.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Grace</hi> (<hi type="italic">charis</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace, favor, gift, kindness") in Pauline usage describes God's free, unmerited favor toward those who do not deserve it. Grace is the mode of God's action: he acts not in response to human merit but out of his own generous purpose. The pairing of <hi type="italic">prothesis</hi> and <hi type="italic">charis</hi> is deliberate: God's plan (<hi type="italic">prothesis</hi>) is executed through grace (<hi type="italic">charis</hi>). The plan is the architect; grace is the mode of construction.</p>
          <p>The concept that Israel's God acts according to his own purpose, not in response to human works, has deep roots in Israel's own history. God chose Abraham before Abraham had done anything to merit it (Genesis 12:1-3). God chose Israel not because they were the greatest of peoples but because he loved them and kept the oath he swore to their fathers (Deuteronomy 7:7-8). God chose Jacob over Esau before they were born or had done anything good or bad (Genesis 25:23; Malachi 1:2-3). The pattern is consistent: God's initiative precedes human response. This text affirms that pattern and applies it to the experience of salvation and calling in Christ.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which he gave us in Christ Jesus before the ages began"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tēn dotheisan hēmin en Christō Iēsou pro chronōn aiōniōn</hi>). The grace described in the previous clause was "given" (<hi type="italic">dotheisan</hi>, aorist passive participle of <hi type="italic">didonai</hi>, "to give") to "us" (<hi type="italic">hēmin</hi>) at a time specified as <hi type="italic">pro chronōn aiōniōn</hi> — "before the ages of time" or "before eternal times." The phrase is extraordinary. The noun <hi type="italic">chronos</hi> ("time, period, age") in the plural, modified by <hi type="italic">aiōnios</hi> ("eternal, age-long"), describes the vast stretches of cosmic time. The preposition <hi type="italic">pro</hi> ("before") places the giving prior to these ages. Before time as we know it began, God's grace was already given.</p>
          <p>The idea that God's plans precede created time is not novel in Jewish thought. Proverbs 8:22-31 describes wisdom as present with God "before the beginning of the earth" and "before the depths" were made. Isaiah 46:10: God declares "the end from the beginning and from ancient times things not yet done, saying, 'My counsel shall stand, and I will accomplish all my purpose.'" Isaiah 25:1: "You have done wonderful things, plans formed of old, faithful and sure." The concept of God's pre-temporal planning was well-developed in Second Temple Judaism: God's purposes exist before creation and govern history toward their fulfillment.</p>
          <p>The grace was given <hi type="italic">en Christō Iēsou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus") — the sphere in which the giving occurs. Christ Jesus is the locus of God's grace. Before the ages began, God's grace was already operative, already given, already residing in the person and purpose of Christ. What was hidden in God's pre-temporal counsel has now become visible in history.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And which now has been manifested through the appearing of our Savior Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">phanerōtheisan de nyn dia tēs epiphaneias tou sōtēros hēmōn Christou Iēsou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">phaneroun</hi> ("to make visible, to reveal, to manifest, to bring to light") marks the transition from the hidden to the revealed. What was given before the ages has now (<hi type="italic">nyn</hi>, "now, at this present time") been made manifest. The adverb <hi type="italic">nyn</hi> is emphatic by position: now, at this decisive moment in history, the hidden has been uncovered.</p>
          <p>The means of manifestation is <hi type="italic">dia tēs epiphaneias</hi> — "through the appearing." The noun <hi type="italic">epiphaneia</hi> ("appearing, manifestation, coming into visible presence") was used in Greek literature for the visible manifestation of a deity or the arrival of a king or emperor. The word carried connotations of divine intervention becoming visible in the human sphere. In the Pastoral Epistles, <hi type="italic">epiphaneia</hi> is used both for Christ's first appearing — his incarnation, ministry, death, and resurrection (here, clearly, since the results described in the next clause are already accomplished) — and for his future appearing (1 Tim 6:14; 2 Tim 4:1, 8; Titus 2:13). Here the reference is to Christ's historical appearing in the world — the event through which God's pre-temporal purpose became visible.</p>
          <p>The title <hi type="italic">sōtēros hēmōn</hi> ("our Savior") applied to Christ Jesus echoes the greeting's attribution of salvation to God ("God the Father") and completes the theological circuit: God our Savior (1 Tim 1:1; 2:3) works through Christ Jesus our Savior. The title <hi type="italic">soter</hi> ("savior, deliverer, rescuer") applied to Christ identifies him as the agent through whom God's saving purpose is executed.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Who abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel"</hi> (<hi type="italic">katargēsantos men ton thanaton, phōtisantos de zōēn kai aphtharsian dia tou euangeliou</hi>). Two aorist participles describe what Christ accomplished through his appearing:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Abolished death</hi> (<hi type="italic">katargēsantos ton thanaton</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">katargein</hi> ("to render inoperative, to abolish, to nullify, to put out of action, to make of no effect") is one of Paul's most characteristic and forceful verbs. In Romans 6:6, "our old self was crucified with him in order that the body of sin might be rendered inoperative" (<hi type="italic">katargēthē</hi>). In 1 Corinthians 15:26, "the last enemy to be destroyed is death" (<hi type="italic">katargeitai</hi>). In 1 Corinthians 15:54-55, when the perishable puts on the imperishable, "then shall come to pass the saying that is written: 'Death is swallowed up in victory. O death, where is your victory? O death, where is your sting?'" The verb does not mean that death has ceased to exist as a physical phenomenon — people still die — but that death's power, finality, and dominion have been broken. Death has been rendered inoperative as the last word. Christ's resurrection is the event that accomplished this: by passing through death and emerging alive, Christ broke death's claim to be the end of the story.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Brought life and immortality to light</hi> (<hi type="italic">phōtisantos de zōēn kai aphtharsian</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">phōtizein</hi> ("to illuminate, to shed light on, to make visible, to bring to light") provides the positive complement. The <hi type="italic">men... de</hi> ("on the one hand... on the other hand") construction creates a balanced pair: death abolished on one side, life and immortality illuminated on the other. The verb <hi type="italic">phōtizein</hi> implies that life (<hi type="italic">zōē</hi>) and immortality (<hi type="italic">aphtharsia</hi>, "incorruptibility, imperishability") already existed — they were present in God's purpose "before the ages began" (v. 9) — but they were hidden, invisible, in the dark. Christ's appearing turned on the light. What was concealed in God's pre-temporal purpose has now been lit up for all to see.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">aphtharsia</hi> ("incorruptibility, imperishability, exemption from decay and death") is composed of the alpha-privative (<hi type="italic">a-</hi>) and <hi type="italic">phtharsis</hi> ("corruption, decay, destruction"). In 1 Corinthians 15:42, 50, 53-54, Paul uses the term for the resurrection body: "What is sown in corruption is raised in incorruption" (<hi type="italic">speiretai en phthora, egeiretai en aphtharsia</hi>). In Romans 2:7, God will give eternal life "to those who by patience in well-doing seek for glory and honor and immortality" (<hi type="italic">aphtharsian</hi>). The pairing of <hi type="italic">zōē</hi> and <hi type="italic">aphtharsia</hi> is a hendiadys that describes one reality from two angles: the life God gives is life that does not corrupt, does not decay, does not perish. It is imperishable life.</p>
          <p>The means of illumination is <hi type="italic">dia tou euangeliou</hi> — "through the gospel." The gospel is the lamp by which life and immortality become visible. Christ's appearing accomplished the reality; the gospel proclamation broadcasts it. The chain is: God's purpose (before time) -&gt; Christ's appearing (in time) -&gt; the gospel (to all who hear). The entire sweep of God's action, from pre-temporal purpose to present proclamation, is compressed into these two verses (9-10).</p>
          <p>These verses, taken as a unit, contain the chapter's theological core. They describe a movement from eternity to history to proclamation: grace given before the ages, revealed through Christ's appearing, and communicated through the gospel. The purpose originates entirely in God. It is not a response to human achievement. It results in the defeat of death and the disclosure of imperishable life. This is what Timothy is being told not to be ashamed of: a gospel grounded in God's eternal purpose, accomplished by Christ's conquest of death, and making visible the life that God has always intended for his people.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For which I was appointed a preacher and apostle and teacher"</hi> (<hi type="italic">eis ho etethēn egō kēryx kai apostolos kai didaskalos</hi>). The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">ho</hi> ("which") refers back to the gospel. Paul was appointed (<hi type="italic">etethēn</hi>, from <hi type="italic">tithenai</hi> in the passive, "to be placed, to be set, to be appointed") for this gospel. Three titles describe his role:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Preacher</hi> (<hi type="italic">kēryx</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">kēryx</hi> means "herald, crier, proclaimer" — one who announces a message on behalf of a superior authority. In the ancient world, the <hi type="italic">kēryx</hi> was the town crier, the royal herald, the one who stood in the public square and proclaimed the king's decree. The herald does not compose the message; he delivers it. Paul is the herald of the gospel, not its author.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Apostle</hi> (<hi type="italic">apostolos</hi>). One sent with authority, an authorized representative. This repeats the title from verse 1.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Teacher</hi> (<hi type="italic">didaskalos</hi>). One who instructs, who explains, who trains others in the content of the message. The herald announces; the teacher explains. Paul does both.</p>
          <p>These three roles correspond to three aspects of gospel ministry: public proclamation (<hi type="italic">kēryx</hi>), authorized representation (<hi type="italic">apostolos</hi>), and detailed instruction (<hi type="italic">didaskalos</hi>). Together they describe a comprehensive commission to make the gospel known in every dimension.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which is why I suffer as I do"</hi> (<hi type="italic">di' hēn aitian kai tauta paschō</hi>). The phrase <hi type="italic">di' hēn aitian</hi> ("for which reason, on account of which") identifies the cause of Paul's suffering: his appointment to proclaim this gospel. The suffering is not random misfortune; it is the direct consequence of his commission. The demonstrative <hi type="italic">tauta</hi> ("these things") points to the specific sufferings Paul is experiencing: imprisonment, abandonment, danger. The verb <hi type="italic">paschein</hi> ("to suffer, to experience") is stated plainly, without self-pity or elaboration. Paul suffers because he does what he was appointed to do.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But I am not ashamed"</hi> (<hi type="italic">all' ouk epaischynomai</hi>). The adversative <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but") introduces the contrast: suffering should logically produce shame — the social disgrace of a prisoner, the humiliation of chains, the abandonment by associates. But Paul is not ashamed. The verb <hi type="italic">epaischynesthai</hi> echoes verse 8 exactly: Paul told Timothy "do not be ashamed" (<hi type="italic">mē epaischynthēs</hi>), and now he models what he commands. He does not merely preach courage; he practices it. The present tense <hi type="italic">epaischynomai</hi> ("I am [not] ashamed") describes Paul's current, ongoing state: right now, in this prison, in these chains, with these desertions, I am not ashamed.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For I know whom I have believed"</hi> (<hi type="italic">oida gar hō pepisteuka</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">oida</hi> ("I know") denotes settled, certain knowledge — the kind of knowledge that is not shakeable by circumstances. The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">hō</hi> ("whom") is critical: Paul says "I know whom," not "I know what." The object of his confidence is a person, not a proposition. He has believed in someone, and he knows that someone. The perfect tense <hi type="italic">pepisteuka</hi> ("I have believed and continue to believe") describes a past act of faith whose results persist into the present. Paul committed himself to Christ at some point in the past, and that commitment stands unshaken.</p>
          <p>This is the chapter's answer to the problem of shame. What enables a person to suffer without shame is not philosophical resolution or psychological toughness but personal knowledge of the one in whom faith has been placed. Paul knows the character, the faithfulness, the power, and the purposes of Christ. Because he knows whom he has trusted, the external circumstances of suffering cannot produce inner shame.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And I am convinced that he is able to guard until that day what has been entrusted to me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai pepeismai hoti dynatos estin tēn parathēkēn mou phylaxai eis ekeinēn tēn hēmeran</hi>). The perfect tense <hi type="italic">pepeismai</hi> ("I have been convinced and remain convinced") matches the same verb in verse 5, where Paul was convinced of Timothy's sincere faith. The conviction is settled and enduring.</p>
          <p>The content of Paul's conviction is that Christ is <hi type="italic">dynatos</hi> ("able, capable, powerful") to guard (<hi type="italic">phylaxai</hi>, "to keep, to protect, to watch over") <hi type="italic">tēn parathēkēn mou</hi> ("my deposit") until <hi type="italic">ekeinēn tēn hēmeran</hi> ("that day"). Several elements require careful attention:</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">parathēkē</hi> ("deposit, something committed to another's care, what is entrusted") is a legal and banking term. In the ancient world, a person could deposit valuables with a trustworthy friend or institution for safekeeping. The depositor expected to receive the deposit back intact. The law protected deposits: Exodus 22:7-13 provides detailed regulations about items left in another's care. The <hi type="italic">parathēkē</hi> is valuable property placed in trusted hands.</p>
          <p>The possessive pronoun <hi type="italic">mou</hi> ("my") identifies this as Paul's deposit — what Paul has entrusted. This is distinct from the <hi type="italic">parathēkē</hi> in verse 14 ("guard the good deposit") and 1 Timothy 6:20 ("guard the deposit"), where the deposit is the gospel that God has entrusted to Timothy. Here the direction is reversed: Paul has placed something in God's keeping. What is Paul's deposit? In context, it is his life, his ministry, his faithfulness, his service — everything he has invested in the gospel. He has staked his existence on Christ and has entrusted the outcome to God's safekeeping. The confidence is that God is able to protect what Paul has committed to him.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">eis ekeinēn tēn hēmeran</hi> ("until that day") points to a specific, known future day. The demonstrative <hi type="italic">ekeinēn</hi> ("that") identifies it as a particular day both Paul and Timothy understand: the day of Christ's return and judgment. The phrase appears again in verse 18 and in 4:8 ("the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me on that day"). "That day" in the prophetic tradition is the Day of the Lord — the day of God's decisive intervention, judgment, and vindication. Isaiah 2:12: "The LORD of hosts has a day against all that is proud and lofty." Joel 2:31 (LXX 3:4): "The great and awesome day of the LORD." Malachi 4:5 (LXX 3:23): "before the great and awesome day of the LORD comes." Paul's confidence extends beyond his present suffering and beyond his death to that ultimate day when all things will be revealed and all accounts settled.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1.13-2Tim.1.14">
          <title>Verses 13-14: The Pattern and the Deposit — What Timothy Must Guard</title>
          <p>"Follow the pattern of the sound words that you have heard from me, in the faith and love that are in Christ Jesus. By the Holy Spirit who dwells within us, guard the good deposit entrusted to you."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Follow the pattern of the sound words that you have heard from me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hypotypōsin eche hygiainontōn logōn hōn par' emou ēkousas</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">hypotypōsis</hi> ("pattern, outline, model, sketch, prototype") appears elsewhere in the New Testament only in 1 Timothy 1:16, where Paul's experience of mercy was described as a <hi type="italic">hypotypōsis</hi> — a prototype — for future believers. The word denotes a model to be followed, a template that guides subsequent work. An artist's preliminary sketch, a builder's blueprint, an architect's plan — these are the analogies. Paul has given Timothy a set of "sound words" (<hi type="italic">hygiainontōn logōn</hi>), and these words function as a pattern that Timothy must have (<hi type="italic">eche</hi>, present imperative of <hi type="italic">echein</hi>, "to have, to hold, to possess") — not merely remember but hold onto, maintain, keep as a working guide.</p>
          <p>The adjective <hi type="italic">hygiainontōn</hi> (from <hi type="italic">hygiainein</hi>, "to be healthy, to be sound, to be in good condition") is the medical metaphor characteristic of the Pastoral Epistles. Sound words are healthy words — teaching that produces health in those who receive it. The metaphor is developed throughout the letters: 1 Timothy 1:10 ("whatever else is contrary to sound teaching"); 1 Timothy 6:3 ("the sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ"); 2 Timothy 4:3 ("the time is coming when people will not endure sound teaching"); Titus 1:9, 13; 2:1, 2. Unsound teaching is diseased, producing sickness in the community. Sound teaching is healthy, promoting the well-being of those who hear and follow it.</p>
          <p>The source of these words is specified: <hi type="italic">hōn par' emou ēkousas</hi> — "which you heard from me." The preposition <hi type="italic">para</hi> ("from, from beside, from the presence of") indicates direct personal transmission: Timothy heard these words from Paul's own mouth, in Paul's own presence. The chain of custody is important: the sound words did not arrive through multiple intermediaries but through direct apostolic teaching. Timothy is a first-generation recipient.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"In the faith and love that are in Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">en pistei kai agapē tē en Christō Iēsou</hi>). The prepositional phrase <hi type="italic">en pistei kai agapē</hi> ("in faith and love") modifies the holding of the pattern. Timothy is not to hold the pattern as a dead letter, a merely formal checklist, a set of propositions maintained without personal investment. He is to hold it in faith (<hi type="italic">pistei</hi>) — with trust in the God from whom the words come — and in love (<hi type="italic">agapē</hi>) — with the self-giving character that is the aim of all sound teaching (1 Tim 1:5). Faith and love are located <hi type="italic">en Christō Iēsou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus"): they are not free-floating virtues but realities that exist within the sphere of Christ.</p>
          <p>The instruction guards against two errors. On one side, Timothy might abandon the pattern under pressure — surrendering the content to fit the expectations of a hostile environment. On the other side, he might hold the pattern rigidly but without the animating qualities of faith and love — maintaining correct words while losing the life they are meant to carry. Both errors are rejected: he must hold the pattern (preserving the content) and hold it in faith and love (preserving its character).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"By the Holy Spirit who dwells within us, guard the good deposit entrusted to you"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tēn kalēn parathēkēn phylaxon dia pneumatos hagiou tou enoikountos en hēmin</hi>). The imperative <hi type="italic">phylaxon</hi> ("guard," aorist imperative of <hi type="italic">phylassein</hi>, "to guard, to watch over, to protect, to keep safe") is direct and urgent. The verb carries the sense of vigilant custody — the watchfulness of a guard over a treasure or a soldier over a strategic position. In 1 Timothy 6:20, the identical command appeared: <hi type="italic">tēn parathēkēn phylaxon</hi> ("guard the deposit"). The repetition underscores the gravity of the charge: this is not a new instruction but a renewed and intensified version of the charge already given.</p>
          <p>The adjective <hi type="italic">kalēn</hi> ("good, noble, beautiful, excellent") describes the deposit. It is not merely a deposit but a good deposit — <hi type="italic">kalos</hi>, the same adjective used throughout the Pastoral Epistles for things that are worthy and noble: the "good warfare" (1 Tim 1:18), the "good confession" (1 Tim 6:12, 13), the "good foundation" (1 Tim 6:19). The deposit is precious, valuable, worthy of the effort required to guard it.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">parathēkē</hi> here — unlike the "my deposit" of verse 12 — refers to the gospel itself, the body of apostolic teaching that Paul has transmitted to Timothy and that Timothy must now protect and pass on. The direction of entrustment is from God through Paul to Timothy: God entrusted the gospel to Paul (1 Tim 1:11), Paul entrusted it to Timothy (1:14; 2:2), and Timothy will entrust it to faithful men (2:2). The deposit is not Timothy's to modify, reinterpret, or adapt to the preferences of the age. It is to be guarded — kept intact, protected from corruption, preserved for transmission.</p>
          <p>The means of guarding is <hi type="italic">dia pneumatos hagiou tou enoikountos en hēmin</hi> — "through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us." The preposition <hi type="italic">dia</hi> ("through, by means of") identifies the Holy Spirit as the agent through whom the guarding is accomplished. Timothy is commanded to guard the deposit, but he is not left to do so in his own strength. The Spirit who dwells (<hi type="italic">enoikountos</hi>, from <hi type="italic">enoikein</hi>, "to dwell in, to inhabit, to make one's home in") within the community of believers is the one who makes the guarding possible.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">enoikein</hi> echoes verse 5, where the same verb described faith dwelling in Lois, Eunice, and Timothy. The parallel is pointed: faith dwells in persons, and the Holy Spirit dwells in persons. The indwelling of the Spirit is what sustains the indwelling of faith. The community is not left alone to protect the gospel through merely human vigilance; the Spirit who inhabits them is the guardian's guardian.</p>
          <p>The pronoun <hi type="italic">hēmin</hi> ("us") rather than <hi type="italic">soi</hi> ("you") is significant: the Spirit dwells not only in Timothy but in all who believe. The guarding of the deposit is Timothy's specific responsibility, but the resource for guarding — the indwelling Spirit — is a shared possession of the whole community of faith.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1.15-2Tim.1.18">
          <title>Verses 15-18: Two Responses to Suffering — Desertion and Faithfulness</title>
          <p>"You are aware that all who are in Asia turned away from me, among whom are Phygelus and Hermogenes. May the Lord grant mercy to the household of Onesiphorus, for he often refreshed me and was not ashamed of my chain, but when he arrived in Rome he searched for me earnestly and found me — may the Lord grant him to find mercy from the Lord on that day — and you well know all the service he rendered at Ephesus."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"You are aware that all who are in Asia turned away from me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">oidas touto, hoti apestraphēsan me pantes hoi en tē Asia</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">oidas</hi> ("you know") indicates that this information is not news to Timothy — he already knows what happened. The verb <hi type="italic">apostrephein</hi> ("to turn away from, to turn one's back on, to reject") in the passive (<hi type="italic">apestraphēsan</hi>) describes a deliberate reorientation: these people have turned their backs on Paul. The preposition <hi type="italic">apo</hi> (implicit in the compound verb) indicates separation: they have separated themselves from Paul, distanced themselves from him.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">pantes hoi en tē Asia</hi> ("all who are in Asia") is striking in its scope. "Asia" (<hi type="italic">hē Asia</hi>) refers to the Roman province of Asia, whose capital was Ephesus — the very city where Timothy is stationed. The word <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") is likely a generalization expressing the overwhelming scale of the desertion rather than an absolute count of every individual, since Onesiphorus is about to be named as an exception. But even as hyperbole, the statement is devastating. The mass of the Asian believers has abandoned Paul. The reasons are not specified but can be inferred from the letter's context: association with a condemned prisoner was dangerous. The cost of loyalty was real, and the community that Paul had built in Asia was not willing to pay it.</p>
          <p>The significance for Timothy is immediate and personal. He is in Ephesus, the capital of the province where "all" have turned away. The social pressure to join the desertion is enormous. Paul names the reality so that Timothy knows what he faces and can choose deliberately rather than drifting with the current.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Among whom are Phygelus and Hermogenes"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hōn estin Phygelos kai Hermogenēs</hi>). Two individuals are named as specific examples of the general desertion. The names appear nowhere else in the New Testament or in known first-century Christian literature. Naming them serves the same function as naming Hymenaeus and Alexander in 1 Timothy 1:20: it exposes them to the community's knowledge and judgment. They are not anonymous figures in a general trend; they are specific people who made a specific choice. Phygelus (possibly from <hi type="italic">phygē</hi>, "flight" — though the etymology may be coincidental) and Hermogenes ("born of Hermes" — a standard Greek name) are on record as having turned away from Paul. Timothy knows them; the letter ensures that the community will remember their choice.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"May the Lord grant mercy to the household of Onesiphorus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">dōē eleos ho kyrios tō Onēsiphorou oikō</hi>). The optative <hi type="italic">dōē</hi> ("may he grant, would that he might give") expresses a prayer or wish. Paul prays for mercy (<hi type="italic">eleos</hi>) upon the household (<hi type="italic">oikos</hi>) of Onesiphorus. The noun <hi type="italic">oikos</hi> here means "household" — the family, dependents, and slaves who constitute Onesiphorus's domestic unit.</p>
          <p>The prayer is directed to the <hi type="italic">oikos</hi> rather than to Onesiphorus himself, which may indicate that Onesiphorus is not currently with his household. He has been in Rome (v. 17), and the prayer's structure — mercy for his household now, mercy for him "on that day" (v. 18) — suggests he may still be traveling or that he has died. The text does not state explicitly which situation obtains, and certainty is not possible from the text alone. What is clear is that Paul distinguishes between mercy for the household in the present and mercy for Onesiphorus on the eschatological day.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For he often refreshed me and was not ashamed of my chain"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hoti pollakis me anepsyxen kai tēn halysin mou ouk epaischynthē</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">hoti</hi> ("for, because") explains why Paul prays for mercy on this household. The verb <hi type="italic">anapsychein</hi> ("to refresh, to cool, to revive, to give relief") means literally "to breathe again" or "to cool by blowing." It describes physical and emotional renewal — Onesiphorus brought Paul relief, comfort, and restoration. The adverb <hi type="italic">pollakis</hi> ("often, frequently, many times") indicates that this was not a single visit but a pattern of care: Onesiphorus came to Paul again and again.</p>
          <p>The second reason is stated with the chapter's keynote verb: <hi type="italic">ouk epaischynthē</hi> — "he was not ashamed." The object of potential shame is <hi type="italic">tēn halysin mou</hi> — "my chain." The noun <hi type="italic">halysis</hi> ("chain, bond, fetter") in the singular likely refers to the chain by which Paul was physically attached to a Roman soldier (cf. Acts 28:20, "it is because of the hope of Israel that I am bound with this chain"; Ephesians 6:20, "for which I am an ambassador in chains"; Philippians 1:7, 13-14, 17). The chain was the visible marker of disgrace. To visit a chained prisoner was to associate oneself with his shame. Onesiphorus was not ashamed of Paul's chain. He looked at the disgrace and was not deterred by it. This is the practical demonstration of what Paul commands Timothy in verse 8: "do not be ashamed of the testimony about our Lord, nor of me his prisoner." Onesiphorus did what Paul asks Timothy to do.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But when he arrived in Rome he searched for me earnestly and found me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla genomenos en Rhōmē spoudaiōs ezētēsen me kai heuren</hi>). The adversative <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but") contrasts Onesiphorus's behavior with the desertion of the Asian believers. Where they turned away, he came looking. The adverb <hi type="italic">spoudaiōs</hi> ("earnestly, eagerly, diligently, with great effort") describes the quality of the search: it was not casual or convenient but determined and effortful. The conditions of Paul's imprisonment apparently made him difficult to find — he was not in an open, accessible location. Onesiphorus had to work to locate him. The verb <hi type="italic">zētein</hi> ("to seek, to search for, to look for") followed by <hi type="italic">heuriskein</hi> ("to find") creates a pair: he sought and he found. The effort was rewarded.</p>
          <p>The detail that Onesiphorus was <hi type="italic">genomenos en Rhōmē</hi> ("having arrived in Rome, having come to be in Rome") indicates that he traveled from Asia to Rome. The distance from Ephesus to Rome was approximately 1,400 miles by land or a significant sea journey. Onesiphorus did not stumble across Paul; he undertook a substantial journey to reach him. This magnifies the contrast with those who were nearby (in Asia) and turned away. They were close and retreated; Onesiphorus was far away and came.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"May the Lord grant him to find mercy from the Lord on that day"</hi> (<hi type="italic">dōē autō ho kyrios heurein eleos para kyriou en ekeinē tē hēmera</hi>). Paul prays again, this time for Onesiphorus personally. The prayer is that "the Lord" (<hi type="italic">ho kyrios</hi>) would grant Onesiphorus "to find mercy from the Lord" (<hi type="italic">heurein eleos para kyriou</hi>) "on that day" (<hi type="italic">en ekeinē tē hēmera</hi>). The repetition of <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> ("Lord") is notable: "may the Lord grant him to find mercy from the Lord." Whether the two uses of <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> refer to the same person (Christ) or to different persons (Christ and God the Father) is not determinable from the text alone. The prayer as a whole asks that on the day of final reckoning, Onesiphorus will receive the mercy he showed to Paul. The pattern echoes Jesus' teaching in the Gospels: "Blessed are the merciful, for they shall receive mercy" (Matthew 5:7); "For with the judgment you pronounce you will be judged, and with the measure you use it will be measured to you" (Matthew 7:2). The one who showed mercy to the prisoner will find mercy from the Judge.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en ekeinē tē hēmera</hi> ("on that day") repeats the same expression from verse 12, binding the two prayers together. Paul is confident that God will guard his deposit "until that day" (v. 12); he prays that Onesiphorus will find mercy "on that day" (v. 18). "That day" is the horizon toward which both Paul and Onesiphorus are moving — the day when all faithfulness will be vindicated and all desertion will be exposed.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And you well know all the service he rendered at Ephesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai hosa en Ephesō diēkonēsen, beltion sy ginōskeis</hi>). The final note reminds Timothy that Onesiphorus's faithfulness in Rome was not an isolated act but the continuation of a pattern established in Ephesus. The verb <hi type="italic">diakonein</hi> ("to serve, to minister, to attend to") describes practical service — the same word used for table service (Acts 6:2), for the collection for Jerusalem (Romans 15:25), and for all the varied forms of concrete care that characterize the community's life. The adverb <hi type="italic">beltion</hi> ("better, more fully, very well") with the pronoun <hi type="italic">sy</hi> ("you") personalizes the point: "you yourself know better than anyone" what Onesiphorus did in Ephesus. Timothy is personally acquainted with this man's track record. The mention of Ephesus brings the example home: Onesiphorus served in the very city where Timothy now stands. His faithfulness is not a distant tale but a local model.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.1">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>2 Timothy 1 is a personal, urgent appeal from a prisoner facing possible death to his beloved spiritual son. Every section serves the purpose of equipping Timothy to face the temptation of shame and to choose faithfulness despite its cost.</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Verses 1-2</hi> establish the letter's theological horizon: Paul's apostleship exists in connection with "the promise of life that is in Christ Jesus." The greeting's inclusion of "mercy" between grace and peace signals the letter's concern with God's faithfulness to those who suffer for his sake. Timothy is addressed not as "legitimate child" (as in 1 Timothy) but as "beloved child" — the register is intimate, not institutional.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Verses 3-5</hi> ground the letter in personal relationship and spiritual heritage. Paul's service to God stands in continuity with his Jewish ancestors. His conscience is clean. His prayers for Timothy are unceasing. He remembers Timothy's tears and longs for reunion. And he is confident that Timothy possesses the sincere faith that first dwelt in his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice. The faith is genuine; the question is whether Timothy will act on it.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Verses 6-7</hi> issue the letter's first direct exhortation: rekindle the gift. The gift God gave through the laying on of Paul's hands has not been extinguished, but it needs stirring. The basis for courage is stated: God's Spirit produces not cowardice but power, love, and self-discipline. These three qualities compose the character needed for ministry under pressure.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold">Verses 8-12</hi> form the chapter's theological center. Timothy is commanded not to be ashamed of the gospel testimony or of Paul the prisoner, but to share in suffering for the gospel by God's power. The basis for this command is a compressed statement of God's saving purpose: God saved and called his people not because of their works but because of his own purpose and grace, given in Christ before time began, now revealed through Christ's appearing, which abolished death and brought imperishable life to light through the gospel. Paul himself models the response: appointed for this gospel, suffering because of it, yet not ashamed — because he knows the person in whom he has placed his trust and is convinced that God can guard what he has deposited until the day of final reckoning.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold">Verses 13-14</hi> specify the duty: hold the pattern of sound teaching received from Paul, in the sphere of faith and love, and guard the good deposit — not by human effort alone but through the Holy Spirit who dwells within the community of believers. The deposit is the gospel itself, received from Paul, to be preserved intact and passed on.</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold">Verses 15-18</hi> provide concrete examples that frame the choice before Timothy. On one side stands the mass desertion: all in Asia turned away from Paul, including the named Phygelus and Hermogenes. On the other side stands Onesiphorus, who was not ashamed of Paul's chain, who traveled to Rome and searched earnestly for Paul, who refreshed him often, who served faithfully in Ephesus. Paul prays for mercy on Onesiphorus and his household — the mercy that the merciful receive.</p>
          <p>The chapter's architecture is built around the word "ashamed" (<hi type="italic">epaischynesthai</hi>), which appears three times and creates the letter's controlling contrast. Timothy is told not to be ashamed (v. 8). Paul declares he is not ashamed (v. 12). Onesiphorus was not ashamed (v. 16). Against these three stand all who turned away (v. 15) — those who chose shame avoidance over faithfulness. The chapter presents Timothy with a binary choice: he can join the many who turned away, or he can follow the pattern of Paul and Onesiphorus. There is no middle ground. The gospel demands open identification with Christ and with his imprisoned apostle. The resources for this identification are God's own purpose and grace (v. 9), the spirit of power, love, and self-discipline (v. 7), and the indwelling Holy Spirit (v. 14). The cost is suffering (v. 8). The hope is "that day" (vv. 12, 18) — the day when all deposits will be returned, all faithfulness vindicated, and all mercy repaid.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="2Tim.2">
        <title>2 Timothy 2 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre and occasion:</hi> This is a letter from Paul to Timothy, written from imprisonment (1:8, 16; 2:9). Unlike 1 Timothy, which was a mandate letter dispatched from a position of active ministry to a delegate managing a community in crisis, 2 Timothy is written under the shadow of Paul's approaching death. The tone is urgent and personal. Paul writes as a man who knows his time is short (4:6-8) and who needs Timothy to come to him (4:9, 21) but who also needs Timothy to carry on the work after Paul is gone. The letter functions as a last testament — the passing of responsibility from one generation to the next.</p>
          <p>The setting is recoverable from the letter itself. Paul is imprisoned, likely in Rome (1:17, where Onesiphorus "searched for me earnestly and found me"). He is cold and wants his cloak (4:13). He has already appeared before a court and was not supported by anyone: "At my first defense no one came to stand by me, but all deserted me" (4:16). Demas has deserted him, "in love with this present world" (4:10). Only Luke is with him (4:11). The letter breathes loneliness, urgency, and the weight of unfinished business.</p>
          <p>Yet the letter is not merely personal. The same crisis that prompted 1 Timothy — false teaching in Ephesus — continues and has worsened. Hymenaeus, who appeared in 1 Timothy 1:20 as one "handed over to Satan that he may learn not to blaspheme," reappears in 2 Timothy 2:17 alongside a new companion, Philetus, teaching that "the resurrection has already happened" and "upsetting the faith of some" (2:18). The false teaching is spreading. The problem Paul addressed in 1 Timothy has not been resolved; it has metastasized. Timothy's task is harder now, and Paul will not be available much longer to back him up.</p>
          <p>The letter's central concern is the faithful transmission of the gospel across generations. The chain Paul describes is explicit: "what you have heard from me in the presence of many witnesses, entrust to faithful people who will be able to teach others also" (2:2). Four generations are named: Paul, Timothy, faithful people, others. The gospel is not Paul's personal possession to take with him to death; it is a deposit (<hi type="italic">paratheke</hi>, 1:14) that must be transmitted intact. The entire letter revolves around this imperative: guard the deposit, transmit the deposit, suffer for the deposit, do not be ashamed of the deposit.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (2 Timothy 1):</hi> Chapter 1 opened with a greeting that identified Paul's apostleship as existing "according to the promise of the life that is in Christ Jesus" (1:1) — the purpose of Paul's apostleship is the life that Christ makes available. Paul remembered Timothy's "sincere faith" (<hi type="italic">pistis anypokritos</hi>, 1:5) — the same phrase used in 1 Timothy 1:5 for the faith that is the aim of the charge. This sincere faith "lived first" in Timothy's grandmother Lois and mother Eunice, establishing the theme of generational transmission that chapter 2 will develop.</p>
          <p>Paul then urged Timothy: "do not be ashamed of the testimony about our Lord, nor of me his prisoner, but share in suffering for the gospel by the power of God" (1:8). The call to share in suffering (<hi type="italic">synkakopatheson</hi>) will be repeated at 2:3 with the same verb. Paul grounded this call in God's saving action: God "saved us and called us to a holy calling, not because of our works but because of his own purpose and grace, which he gave us in Christ Jesus before the ages began" (1:9). He then described his own posture: "I am not ashamed, for I know whom I have believed, and I am convinced that he is able to guard until that day what has been entrusted to me" (1:12). The language of deposit and guarding recurred: "Guard the good deposit entrusted to you by the Holy Spirit who dwells in us" (1:14).</p>
          <p>The chapter closed with a painful report. "You are aware that all who are in Asia turned away from me, among whom are Phygelus and Hermogenes" (1:15). The verb <hi type="italic">apostrephein</hi> ("to turn away from") describes wholesale desertion. Against this dark background, one bright figure: Onesiphorus, who "was not ashamed of my chains" (1:16) and "often refreshed me" (1:16) and "searched for me earnestly and found me" (1:17). Paul invoked mercy on Onesiphorus and his household. The chapter ended, then, with a stark contrast between desertion (many) and faithfulness (one). This contrast is the immediate launching point for chapter 2.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (2 Timothy 3):</hi> Chapter 3 opens with an intensified warning: "Understand this, that in the last days there will come times of difficulty" (3:1). A long vice list follows (3:2-5), describing people who are "lovers of self, lovers of money, proud, arrogant, abusive, disobedient to their parents, ungrateful, unholy, heartless, unappeasable, slanderous, without self-control, brutal, not loving good, treacherous, reckless, swollen with conceit, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God, having the appearance of godliness but denying its power." The command: "Avoid such people" (3:5). Some of these people "creep into households and capture weak women, burdened with sins and led astray by various passions, always learning and never able to arrive at a knowledge of the truth" (3:6-7). They are compared to Jannes and Jambres, who "opposed Moses" (3:8) — the Egyptian magicians of the Exodus narrative, drawn from Jewish tradition.</p>
          <p>Paul then turned to Timothy directly: "You, however, have followed my teaching, my conduct, my aim in life, my faith, my patience, my love, my steadfastness, my persecutions and sufferings" (3:10-11). The contrast is explicit: "evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse, deceiving and being deceived. But as for you, continue in what you have learned and have firmly believed" (3:13-14). The chapter concludes with the affirmation that "all Scripture is breathed out by God and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness, that the man of God may be complete, equipped for every good work" (3:16-17).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 2 connects the two:</hi> Chapter 1 ended with the reality of mass desertion and individual faithfulness. Chapter 2 opens with the logical response: "You then, my child, be strengthened in the grace that is in Christ Jesus" (2:1). The conjunction <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("then, therefore") draws the consequence — in the face of everyone else's desertion, Timothy must be strengthened. Chapter 2 then develops the positive content of what faithful service looks like (vv. 1-13), identifies the specific threat of false teaching and its spread (vv. 14-19), and describes how Timothy should conduct himself in response (vv. 20-26). Chapter 3 will intensify the warning about the false teachers and ground Timothy's response in Scripture. The movement from chapter 2 to chapter 3 is continuous: the false teachers of 2:17-18 are the same kind of people described in 3:1-9, and the instruction to correct opponents with gentleness (2:25) is developed alongside the command to "avoid such people" (3:5) — gentleness toward those who might repent, avoidance of those who will not.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.1-2Tim.2.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: The Chain of Transmission</title>
          <p>"You then, my child, be strengthened in the grace that is in Christ Jesus, and what you have heard from me in the presence of many witnesses entrust to faithful people who will be able to teach others also."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"You then, my child, be strengthened in the grace that is in Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">sy oun, teknon mou, endynamou en te chariti te en Christo Iesou</hi>). The pronoun <hi type="italic">sy</hi> ("you," emphatic, placed first for stress) sets Timothy apart from the deserters just mentioned. The conjunction <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("then, therefore, accordingly") draws the inference: because others have turned away, you must stand firm. The combination <hi type="italic">sy oun</hi> is contrastive — "you, by contrast, therefore." Everyone in Asia has turned away (1:15); you, Timothy, must be strengthened.</p>
          <p>The address <hi type="italic">teknon mou</hi> ("my child") continues the father-child language established in the greeting (1:2, "Timothy, my beloved child") and in 1 Timothy 1:2, 18. The relationship is not merely professional; it is generational. Paul is passing the work to the next generation — from father to son.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">endynamousthai</hi> ("to be strengthened, to be empowered") is the passive form of <hi type="italic">endynamoun</hi>. The same verb appeared in 1 Timothy 1:12, where Paul thanked Christ "who has given me strength" (<hi type="italic">to endynamosanti me</hi>). The passive voice is significant: Timothy is not told to generate strength from within himself but to be strengthened — to receive strength from an external source. That source is specified: <hi type="italic">en te chariti te en Christo Iesou</hi> — "in the grace that is in Christ Jesus." The preposition <hi type="italic">en</hi> ("in") locates the sphere in which the strengthening occurs: grace. The grace is itself located <hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus"). The chain is: Timothy is strengthened in grace, and that grace exists in Christ Jesus. The strength for the task ahead is not self-generated resilience but the power of Christ's grace channeled to the servant who remains within that grace.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And what you have heard from me in the presence of many witnesses"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ha ekousas par' emou dia pollon martyron</hi>). The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">ha</hi> ("what things, the things which") identifies the content: everything Timothy heard from Paul. The verb <hi type="italic">akouein</hi> ("to hear") in the aorist (<hi type="italic">ekousas</hi>) points to specific occasions of hearing — not gradual absorption but definite acts of instruction. The preposition <hi type="italic">para</hi> with the genitive (<hi type="italic">par' emou</hi>, "from me, from my side") identifies Paul as the source. The phrase <hi type="italic">dia pollon martyron</hi> ("through many witnesses" or "in the presence of many witnesses") indicates that the instruction was not private or secret. The preposition <hi type="italic">dia</hi> with the genitive can mean "through" (the witnesses as intermediaries) or "in the presence of" (the witnesses as attestors). In this context, the latter is more natural: Paul taught Timothy in settings where many others were present and could attest to what was said. The teaching was public, verifiable, and established.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">martys</hi> ("witness") in first-century usage carries legal weight. A witness is one who can testify to what they saw or heard. The presence of many witnesses makes the tradition Paul entrusted to Timothy a matter of public record, not private opinion. If anyone asks what Paul taught, the witnesses can confirm it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Entrust to faithful people who will be able to teach others also"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tauta parathou pistois anthropois, hoitines hikanoi esontai kai heterous didaxai</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">paratithesthai</hi> ("to deposit, to entrust, to commit to someone's care") — the same verb used in 1 Timothy 1:18 ("this charge I entrust to you") — here describes the act of passing the deposit onward. The middle voice (<hi type="italic">parathou</hi>) indicates personal, deliberate commitment: Timothy is to personally place these teachings into the keeping of others.</p>
          <p>The recipients are <hi type="italic">pistois anthropois</hi> ("faithful people, trustworthy people"). The adjective <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("faithful, trustworthy, reliable") is the same word used for the "faithful saying" later in this chapter (2:11, <hi type="italic">pistos ho logos</hi>). Not just any people — faithful people, people whose reliability has been demonstrated. The quality of the next link in the chain determines whether the deposit survives or is corrupted.</p>
          <p>These faithful people must possess a further qualification: <hi type="italic">hoitines hikanoi esontai kai heterous didaxai</hi> — "who will be competent to teach others also." The adjective <hi type="italic">hikanos</hi> ("sufficient, competent, adequate, able, fit") describes capability — they must have the ability, not merely the willingness, to pass the teaching further. The chain of transmission has four links: Paul to Timothy to faithful people to others. The gospel is not a private treasure but a propagating message. Each generation receives it, guards it, and transmits it. The quality of each link matters: if Timothy entrusts the deposit to unfaithful or incompetent people, the chain breaks.</p>
          <p>This verse is the chapter's programmatic statement. Everything that follows — the metaphors of soldier, athlete, and farmer; the gospel that Paul suffers for; the warnings about false teaching; the instructions about Timothy's conduct — serves this central purpose: the faithful transmission of the gospel across generations.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.3-2Tim.2.7">
          <title>Verses 3-7: Three Metaphors for Faithful Service</title>
          <p>"Share in suffering as a good soldier of Christ Jesus. No soldier gets entangled in civilian pursuits, since his aim is to please the one who enlisted him. An athlete is not crowned unless he competes according to the rules. It is the hard-working farmer who ought to have the first share of the crops. Think over what I say, for the Lord will give you understanding in everything."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Share in suffering as a good soldier of Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">synkakopatheson hos kalos stratiotes Christou Iesou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">synkakopathein</hi> ("to suffer together, to share in hardship, to endure evil together") appeared in 1:8, where Paul urged Timothy to <hi type="italic">synkakopatheson to euangelio</hi> — "share in suffering for the gospel." The <hi type="italic">syn-</hi> prefix ("together with") identifies the suffering as shared — Timothy suffers alongside Paul, alongside Christ, alongside all who serve the gospel. The comparison is to a <hi type="italic">kalos stratiotes</hi> ("good soldier, noble soldier"). The adjective <hi type="italic">kalos</hi> ("good, noble, fine, excellent") marks the soldier as one who fulfills the role well. In 1 Timothy 1:18, Paul used the same military language: Timothy was to "wage the good warfare" (<hi type="italic">strateue ten kalen strateian</hi>). The military metaphor is sustained across both letters.</p>
          <p>The genitive <hi type="italic">Christou Iesou</hi> ("of Christ Jesus") identifies the commander under whom the soldier serves. The soldier does not fight his own war or serve his own interests; he belongs to Christ Jesus. The first-century Roman soldier was the most recognizable image of disciplined service in the Mediterranean world. He left home, submitted to authority, endured hardship, and served a cause larger than himself. Paul draws on this universally understood image.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"No soldier gets entangled in civilian pursuits, since his aim is to please the one who enlisted him"</hi> (<hi type="italic">oudeis strateuomenos empleketai tais tou biou pragmateiais, hina to stratologesanti arese</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">emplekesthai</hi> ("to entangle, to involve, to weave into") describes being caught up in something that restricts freedom of movement. A soldier tangled in a net cannot fight. The noun <hi type="italic">pragmateia</hi> ("business, affair, pursuit, occupation") with <hi type="italic">tou biou</hi> ("of life, of daily living, of civilian existence") describes the ordinary concerns of non-military life — commerce, property, household management, civil affairs. The soldier on campaign sets these aside. His purpose (<hi type="italic">hina</hi>, "in order that") is singular: to <hi type="italic">arese</hi> ("to please") to <hi type="italic">stratologesanti</hi> ("the one who enlisted him, the commanding officer who recruited him"). The verb <hi type="italic">stratologein</hi> ("to enlist soldiers, to recruit an army") appears only here in the New Testament. It describes the act of choosing and enlisting a soldier for service.</p>
          <p>The application to Timothy is direct. He has been enlisted by Christ Jesus. His aim must be singular: to please his commanding officer. The "civilian affairs" that might entangle him could include the speculative controversies promoted by the false teachers, the interpersonal conflicts within the Ephesian community, or any concern that distracts from the mission of guarding and transmitting the gospel.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"An athlete is not crowned unless he competes according to the rules"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ean de kai athle tis, ou stephanoutai ean me nomimos athlese</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">athlein</hi> ("to compete, to contend in athletic games") appears only here and in the following verse in the New Testament. The noun <hi type="italic">stephanos</hi> ("crown, wreath, garland") — the prize given to the victor in the Greek athletic games — is the goal. But the crown has a condition: <hi type="italic">ean me nomimos athlese</hi> — "unless he competes lawfully, according to the rules." The adverb <hi type="italic">nomimos</hi> ("lawfully, according to the rules, legitimately") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:8, where the law was to be used <hi type="italic">nomimos</hi> ("lawfully"). The word connects the two passages: just as the law must be used according to its proper purpose, so the athlete must compete according to the established rules. An athlete who cheats — who cuts corners, who takes shortcuts, who violates the regulations of the competition — is disqualified regardless of his performance. The crown goes only to the one who competed legitimately.</p>
          <p>The athletic games of the Greek world (the Olympic, Isthmian, Nemean, and Pythian games) were among the most prominent cultural institutions of the first century. The rules were strict: athletes underwent a mandatory training period, swore oaths to compete fairly, and faced disqualification for violations. The image would be immediately understood by any first-century hearer. The application is that Timothy's faithfulness must be conducted within the bounds of what is right — not by any means available, but according to the gospel's own standards. Effectiveness through corruption does not earn the crown.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"It is the hard-working farmer who ought to have the first share of the crops"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ton kopionta georon dei proton ton karpon metalambanein</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">kopian</hi> ("to labor, to toil, to work hard, to exhaust oneself") appeared in 1 Timothy 5:17 for elders who "labor in preaching and teaching." The noun <hi type="italic">georgos</hi> ("farmer, cultivator, one who works the land") describes someone whose livelihood depends on sustained physical labor over seasons. The adjective <hi type="italic">proton</hi> ("first") and the verb <hi type="italic">metalambanein</hi> ("to share in, to partake of, to receive a portion") establish the principle: the farmer who does the actual work has the first right to the harvest. This echoes the principle Paul established in 1 Corinthians 9:7 ("Who plants a vineyard and does not eat of its fruit?") and 1 Timothy 5:18 ("the laborer deserves his wages"). The one who works is entitled to the benefit of the work.</p>
          <p>The three metaphors — soldier, athlete, farmer — are not random. Each contributes a distinct element to the portrait of faithful service:</p>
          <p>(1) The <hi type="bold">soldier</hi> contributes single-minded devotion: no entanglement in distractions, total focus on pleasing the commander.</p>
          <p>(2) The <hi type="bold">athlete</hi> contributes disciplined compliance with the rules: effort alone is not sufficient; the effort must be legitimate.</p>
          <p>(3) The <hi type="bold">farmer</hi> contributes patient labor with expectation of reward: the work is hard and the harvest is not immediate, but the one who labors will receive the fruit.</p>
          <p>Together they describe Timothy's situation: he must be focused (like the soldier), disciplined (like the athlete), and patient (like the farmer). Suffering, rule-following, and hard labor are all part of the calling.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Think over what I say, for the Lord will give you understanding in everything"</hi> (<hi type="italic">noei ho lego, dosei gar soi ho kyrios synesin en pasin</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">noein</hi> ("to perceive, to understand, to think over, to grasp with the mind") is an imperative: Timothy is commanded to think. Paul does not expect him to absorb the metaphors passively but to work through their implications. The verb is the same used in 1 Timothy 1:7 for the understanding the false teachers lack (<hi type="italic">me noountes</hi> — "not understanding"). Timothy is to do what they fail to do: think carefully about meaning.</p>
          <p>The promise that accompanies the command is significant: <hi type="italic">dosei gar soi ho kyrios synesin en pasin</hi> — "for the Lord will give you understanding in everything." The noun <hi type="italic">synesis</hi> ("understanding, insight, comprehension, intelligence") describes the capacity to put things together, to see connections, to grasp implications. Paul promises that this capacity will be given by the Lord. Timothy must exercise his mind (<hi type="italic">noei</hi>), but the understanding that results will be a gift (<hi type="italic">dosei</hi>, future active — "he will give"). The effort is Timothy's; the illumination is the Lord's.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.8-2Tim.2.10">
          <title>Verses 8-10: The Gospel Paul Suffers For</title>
          <p>"Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, the offspring of David, as preached in my gospel, for which I suffer, bound with chains as a criminal. But the word of God is not bound! Therefore I endure everything for the sake of the elect, that they also may obtain the salvation that is in Christ Jesus with eternal glory."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, the offspring of David"</hi> (<hi type="italic">mnemoneve Iesoun Christon egegermenon ek nekron, ek spermatos David</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">mnemoneuein</hi> ("to remember, to keep in mind, to call to remembrance") is a present imperative: keep remembering, continually bear in mind. The object of remembrance is not a doctrine or a principle but a person: Jesus Christ. Two things about this person are to be remembered:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="italic">Egegermenon ek nekron</hi> — "risen from the dead." The perfect passive participle <hi type="italic">egegermenon</hi> (from <hi type="italic">egeirein</hi>, "to raise") describes a completed action with ongoing results: Jesus was raised and remains raised. The resurrection is not merely a past event but a present reality. The passive voice indicates that Jesus was raised by another — by God (as Paul states elsewhere: Romans 8:11, "he who raised Christ Jesus from the dead"; 1 Corinthians 6:14; 2 Corinthians 4:14). The resurrection is the foundational fact of the gospel. Its relevance to the chapter's argument will become explicit in verses 17-18, where Hymenaeus and Philetus are teaching that "the resurrection has already happened" — they are distorting the very fact Paul tells Timothy to remember.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="italic">Ek spermatos David</hi> — "from the seed of David, of David's offspring." This identifies Jesus within the lineage of David, the royal line from which Israel's Messiah was expected to come. The promise to David in 2 Samuel 7:12-13 (LXX): "I will raise up your offspring (<hi type="italic">sperma</hi>) after you... and I will establish his kingdom. He shall build a house for my name, and I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever." The Davidic descent locates Jesus within Israel's history and prophetic expectation. He is not a generic savior but the promised king of Israel, the one in whom God's covenant promises to David find their fulfillment. Paul made the same identification in Romans 1:3: "concerning his Son, who was descended from David according to the flesh."</p>
          <p>The two descriptions — risen and Davidic — compress the gospel into its essential coordinates: Jesus is the promised Messiah of Israel (Davidic descent), and God has vindicated him through resurrection from the dead. Everything Paul suffers for, everything Timothy is to guard and transmit, rests on these two facts.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"As preached in my gospel"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kata to euangelion mou</hi>). The preposition <hi type="italic">kata</hi> ("according to, in keeping with") connects the description of Jesus to Paul's gospel. The possessive <hi type="italic">mou</hi> ("my") does not mean Paul invented the gospel but that this is the gospel entrusted to him, the gospel he preaches, the specific form of the message for which he bears responsibility. The same phrase appears in Romans 2:16 ("according to my gospel") and Romans 16:25. Paul's gospel is the gospel about the risen Davidic Messiah.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For which I suffer, bound with chains as a criminal"</hi> (<hi type="italic">en ho kakopatheo mechri desmon hos kakourgos</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">kakopathein</hi> ("to suffer evil, to endure hardship, to bear affliction") echoes the <hi type="italic">synkakopatheson</hi> ("share in suffering") of verse 3 and 1:8. Paul suffers for the gospel — the gospel is the cause of his suffering. The extent is stated: <hi type="italic">mechri desmon</hi> — "to the point of chains, up to and including imprisonment." The noun <hi type="italic">desmos</hi> ("bond, chain, fetter") is the physical manifestation of Paul's imprisonment. He is not merely inconvenienced or socially marginalized; he is in chains.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hos kakourgos</hi> ("as a criminal, as an evildoer") sharpens the description. The noun <hi type="italic">kakourgos</hi> (<hi type="italic">kakos</hi>, "evil" + <hi type="italic">ergon</hi>, "deed/work") means "one who does evil, a criminal." In Luke 23:32-33, 39, the same word describes the criminals crucified alongside Jesus. Paul is treated as a criminal — as one who has committed an offense against the social order. The parallel with Jesus is implicit but unmistakable: the preacher of the gospel suffers the same kind of treatment as the subject of the gospel.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But the word of God is not bound!"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla ho logos tou theou ou dedetai</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but") introduces a sharp contrast. Paul is in chains (<hi type="italic">dedetai</hi> from <hi type="italic">dein</hi>, "to bind"); the word of God is not in chains (<hi type="italic">ou dedetai</hi>). The perfect passive <hi type="italic">dedetai</hi> ("has been bound, stands bound") applied to Paul describes his current state: he is and remains bound. The same verb with the negative applied to the word of God states that, despite Paul's imprisonment, the message itself cannot be imprisoned. The word of God transcends the physical constraints placed on its messenger. This is not defiance but theological assertion: God's word operates beyond the capacity of human authorities to restrain it. The imprisonment of Paul does not silence the gospel. It continues to spread through others — through Timothy, through the faithful people of verse 2, through the chain of transmission that no prison can interrupt.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Therefore I endure everything for the sake of the elect"</hi> (<hi type="italic">dia touto panta hypomeno dia tous eklektous</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">dia touto</hi> ("because of this, for this reason") points back: because the word of God is not bound, Paul can endure everything. His suffering is not meaningless; it serves the gospel's advance. The verb <hi type="italic">hypomenein</hi> ("to endure, to bear up under, to remain, to persevere") describes sustained endurance in the face of ongoing affliction. The word will return in the faithful saying at verse 12 (<hi type="italic">ei hypomenomen</hi>, "if we endure"). Paul practices what the saying commands.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">dia tous eklektous</hi> ("for the sake of the elect, on account of the chosen ones") requires careful attention. The adjective <hi type="italic">eklektos</hi> ("chosen, elect, selected") has deep roots in the LXX, where it consistently describes Israel as God's chosen people — chosen not for private salvation but for corporate purpose. In Isaiah 65:9 (LXX), God speaks of "my chosen ones" (<hi type="italic">tous eklektous mou</hi>) who will inherit his mountains — a restoration promise directed at faithful Israel after exile. In Isaiah 43:20 (LXX), God will give water in the wilderness "to give drink to my chosen people" (<hi type="italic">to genos mou to eklekton</hi>) — the language of corporate identity, not individual selection. In Isaiah 45:4 (LXX), God addresses Cyrus: "For the sake of my servant Jacob and Israel my chosen one" (<hi type="italic">Israēl ho eklektos mou</hi>) — Israel as a corporate entity is the chosen one. In Psalm 105:6 (LXX 104:6): "O offspring of Abraham, his servant, children of Jacob, his chosen ones" (<hi type="italic">eklektoi autou</hi>). The pattern is consistent: <hi type="italic">eklektos</hi> in the LXX describes a people chosen by God for a purpose — to be his witness, his servant, the vehicle through which his purposes reach the world.</p>
          <p>Paul's usage here does not require a departure from this LXX background. He endures everything for the sake of those whom God has chosen — the community of believers, the corporate body that inherits Israel's identity as God's chosen people. His suffering serves their benefit. This is the same logic as Colossians 1:24: "I rejoice in my sufferings for your sake, and in my flesh I am filling up what is lacking in Christ's afflictions for the sake of his body, that is, the church."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"That they also may obtain the salvation that is in Christ Jesus with eternal glory"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hina kai autoi soterias tychosi tes en Christo Iesou meta doxes aioniou</hi>). The <hi type="italic">hina</hi> clause ("in order that") states the purpose of Paul's endurance. The verb <hi type="italic">tynchanein</hi> ("to obtain, to attain, to reach, to gain") with the genitive <hi type="italic">soterias</hi> ("of salvation") describes the goal: the elect are to obtain salvation. The <hi type="italic">kai autoi</hi> ("they also") suggests that Paul himself is already in some sense a participant in this salvation and endures so that others may share in it. The salvation is located <hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus") — it exists within Christ, is accessed through union with Christ. It comes <hi type="italic">meta doxes aioniou</hi> ("with eternal glory") — accompanied by glory that is <hi type="italic">aionios</hi> ("age-long, belonging to the age to come, eternal").</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">soteria</hi> ("salvation, deliverance, rescue, preservation") in the LXX consistently refers to God's acts of rescue — from enemies, from exile, from death. In the Psalms, <hi type="italic">soteria</hi> is the cry of the oppressed to God the deliverer (Psalm 3:8 LXX [3:9], "Salvation belongs to the LORD"; Psalm 27:1 LXX [28:1], "The LORD is my light and my salvation"). The salvation Paul speaks of is not an abstraction but the concrete deliverance that God accomplishes through the risen Davidic Messiah — and it comes loaded with eternal glory.</p>
          <p>The fact that Paul says the elect are to "obtain" this salvation — using a verb of attainment, with a purpose clause indicating that the obtaining is not yet complete for them — indicates that salvation is the goal toward which they are moving, not merely a status already fully possessed. Paul endures so that they may reach it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.11-2Tim.2.13">
          <title>Verses 11-13: The Faithful Saying — Four Conditions and Their Outcomes</title>
          <p>"The saying is trustworthy, for: If we have died with him, we will also live with him; if we endure, we will also reign with him; if we deny him, he also will deny us; if we are faithless, he remains faithful — for he cannot deny himself."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The saying is trustworthy"</hi> (<hi type="italic">pistos ho logos</hi>). This is the fourth occurrence of the formula <hi type="italic">pistos ho logos</hi> ("the saying is faithful/trustworthy") in the Pastoral Epistles (1 Timothy 1:15; 3:1; 4:9; here; and Titus 3:8). As in 1 Timothy 1:15, the formula marks a statement as established, reliable, and worthy of reception by the community. It may introduce a hymn, a confessional fragment, or a recognized communal saying. The rhythmic, parallel structure of what follows (four conditional clauses with balanced outcomes) strongly suggests a hymnic or catechetical composition — a saying already in use in the community, which Paul now endorses and applies.</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") after <hi type="italic">pistos ho logos</hi> is significant. It connects the saying to what Paul has just stated in verses 8-10 about his suffering and endurance for the sake of the elect. The saying provides the theological basis for everything Paul has described: why he endures, what the stakes are, and what governs the relationship between faithfulness and outcome.</p>
          <p>The saying contains four conditional statements. Each uses <hi type="italic">ei</hi> ("if") with the indicative, which in Greek states a condition assumed to be real or at least genuinely possible — these are not hypothetical impossibilities but real scenarios with real consequences.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Line 1: "If we have died with him, we will also live with him"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ei gar synapethanomen, kai syzesomen</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">synapothneskein</hi> ("to die together with, to die alongside") in the aorist (<hi type="italic">synapethanomen</hi>) points to a past, completed act: "if we died with him." The aorist tense indicates a specific, decisive event, not an ongoing process. The prefix <hi type="italic">syn-</hi> ("with, together") identifies the death as shared — our death is joined to his death.</p>
          <p>The most natural first-century referent for "dying with Christ" is baptism. Paul developed this connection extensively in Romans 6:3-8: "Do you not know that all of us who have been baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death? We were buried therefore with him through baptism into death, in order that, just as Christ was raised from the dead by the glory of the Father, we too might walk in newness of life. For if we have been united with him in a death like his, we shall certainly also be united with him in a resurrection like his." The language of <hi type="italic">synapothneskein</hi> in Romans 6:8 is identical: "Now if we have died with Christ (<hi type="italic">ei de apethanomen syn Christo</hi>), we believe that we will also live with him (<hi type="italic">syzesomen auto</hi>)." The parallel is exact.</p>
          <p>However, the context in 2 Timothy 2 — Paul writing from prison, facing death, speaking of suffering and endurance — also allows a more literal dimension: those who share in Christ's death through actual suffering and martyrdom will also share in his life. The two meanings are not mutually exclusive. The baptismal death inaugurates a life of dying-to-self that may culminate in actual death for the gospel's sake.</p>
          <p>The apodosis <hi type="italic">kai syzesomen</hi> ("we will also live with him") uses the future indicative: the living-with-him lies ahead. The life promised is future, eschatological — it corresponds to the "salvation with eternal glory" of verse 10. The verb <hi type="italic">syzein</hi> ("to live together with, to live alongside") describes shared life: the life is not solitary but relational, lived in union with Christ.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Line 2: "If we endure, we will also reign with him"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ei hypomenomen, kai symbasileuomen</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">hypomenein</hi> ("to endure, to remain under, to bear up, to persevere") in the present tense (<hi type="italic">hypomenomen</hi>) describes ongoing action — not a single act but a sustained posture. The shift from the aorist of line 1 (<hi type="italic">synapethanomen</hi>, "we died" — past, completed) to the present of line 2 (<hi type="italic">hypomenomen</hi>, "we are enduring" — current, ongoing) is deliberate. Dying with Christ may be a decisive moment; enduring is a continuous practice. The first describes initiation; the second describes perseverance.</p>
          <p>The apodosis <hi type="italic">kai symbasileuomen</hi> ("we will also reign with him") uses the future indicative: reigning is promised to those who endure. The verb <hi type="italic">symbasileuein</hi> ("to reign together with, to share in royal authority") appears also in 1 Corinthians 4:8, where Paul uses it with irony: "Already you have become rich! Without us you have become kings! And would that you did reign, so that we might share the rule with you!" The ironic use in 1 Corinthians shows that the reigning Paul envisions is not a present reality but a future hope — and it is conditioned on endurance, not on the presumption of arrival.</p>
          <p>The structure of lines 1 and 2 creates a sequence: dying with Christ leads to living with him; enduring leads to reigning with him. The first pair describes the entrance (death/life); the second pair describes the journey (endurance/reign). Both are conditional: the positive outcome depends on the positive condition being met.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Line 3: "If we deny him, he also will deny us"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ei arnesometha, kakeinos arnesetai hemas</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">arneisthai</hi> ("to deny, to disown, to refuse to acknowledge, to repudiate") in the future middle (<hi type="italic">arnesometha</hi>, "we will deny, if we deny") presents the negative possibility with equal directness. The denial described here is the opposite of the endurance in line 2: where endurance is faithful persistence under pressure, denial is the abandonment of the confession under that same pressure.</p>
          <p>The saying echoes Jesus' own words. In Matthew 10:33: "Whoever denies me before men, I also will deny before my Father who is in heaven" (<hi type="italic">hostis an arnesetai me emprosthen ton anthropon, arnesomai kagō auton emprosthen tou patros mou tou en ouranois</hi>). In Luke 12:9: "The one who denies me before men will be denied before the angels of God." The parallel is almost certainly deliberate. The hymnic fragment incorporated into Paul's letter here draws on a saying of Jesus that was well known in the early communities. The verb <hi type="italic">arneisthai</hi> in the context of persecution describes the act of repudiating one's allegiance to Christ — saying, under pressure, "I do not know him" or "I am not one of his." Peter's denial of Jesus (using the same verb, <hi type="italic">arneisthai</hi>, in Matthew 26:70, 72, 75; Mark 14:68, 70, 72; Luke 22:57, 61; John 13:38, 18:25, 27) is the paradigmatic case of such denial in the community's memory.</p>
          <p>The apodosis <hi type="italic">kakeinos arnesetai hemas</hi> ("that one also will deny us") states the consequence: Christ will deny those who deny him. The demonstrative pronoun <hi type="italic">ekeinos</hi> ("that one") refers to Christ. The denial is reciprocal: denial provokes denial. The future tense (<hi type="italic">arnesetai</hi>) locates Christ's denial in the future — at the judgment, at the final appearing, at "that day" (1:12, 18; 4:8).</p>
          <p>The connection to the chapter's surrounding context is critical. Paul has just described his own endurance (v. 10, <hi type="italic">panta hypomeno</hi>) and will soon discuss Hymenaeus and Philetus, who "have swerved from the truth" (v. 18). In 1 Timothy 1:19-20, Hymenaeus had already "made shipwreck of his faith." The saying warns that such departure from the truth — if it constitutes a denial of Christ — will be met with Christ's own denial of the denier.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Line 4: "If we are faithless, he remains faithful — for he cannot deny himself"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ei apistoumen, ekeinos pistos menei, arnesasthai gar heauton ou dynatai</hi>). This is the line that requires the most careful analysis, because its meaning depends entirely on how it relates to the three lines that precede it.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">apisteuein</hi> ("to be faithless, to be unfaithful, to disbelieve, to prove untrustworthy") in the present tense (<hi type="italic">apistoumen</hi>) describes an ongoing state of unfaithfulness. The adjective <hi type="italic">apistos</hi> and its verbal form in the LXX consistently describe covenant unfaithfulness — the failure to trust God, the violation of covenantal obligations. In Numbers 14:11 (LXX), God says: "How long will this people despise me? And how long will they not believe (<hi type="italic">ou pisteuousin</hi>) in me, in spite of all the signs that I have done among them?" Israel's <hi type="italic">apistia</hi> was met not with unconditional preservation but with judgment: the generation that refused to believe did not enter the land (Numbers 14:22-23; Hebrews 3:19, "they were unable to enter because of unbelief").</p>
          <p>The apodosis <hi type="italic">ekeinos pistos menei</hi> ("that one remains faithful") states that Christ remains <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("faithful, trustworthy"). The verb <hi type="italic">menein</hi> ("to remain, to abide, to continue, to stay") describes constancy: Christ's faithfulness does not waver. But faithful to what? This is the decisive question.</p>
          <p>The explanatory clause <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") provides the answer: <hi type="italic">arnesasthai gar heauton ou dynatai</hi> — "for he cannot deny himself." The verb <hi type="italic">arneisthai</hi> — the same verb used in line 3 for the denial of Christ and Christ's denial of the denier — now describes what Christ cannot do: he cannot deny himself. The reflexive pronoun <hi type="italic">heauton</hi> ("himself") makes Christ the object of his own potential denial. To deny himself would be to contradict his own nature, to act against his own word, to break his own promises and warnings.</p>
          <p>The question is: what does it mean for Christ to "deny himself"? The answer must be found in the structure of the saying itself. The saying has stated four things:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>Those who died with him will live with him.</item>
            <item>Those who endure will reign with him.</item>
            <item>Those who deny him, he will deny.</item>
            <item>Those who are faithless, he remains faithful.</item>
          </list>
          <p>If "he remains faithful" means that Christ saves the faithless regardless of their faithlessness — that unfaithfulness on the human side does not affect the outcome — then line 4 contradicts line 3. Line 3 states that denial of Christ results in Christ's denial of the denier. If line 4 then states that faithlessness is met with salvation anyway, the saying undercuts its own warning. The structure collapses into incoherence: why state a real consequence (denial) in line 3 if line 4 erases consequences entirely?</p>
          <p>The coherent reading, the one that maintains the integrity of all four lines, is this: Christ remains faithful to his own stated terms. He remains faithful to the promises of lines 1 and 2 (those who die with him will live; those who endure will reign), and he remains faithful to the warnings of line 3 (those who deny will be denied). His faithfulness is comprehensive — he keeps his promises and he executes his warnings, because both are expressions of who he is. To fail to keep either — to fail to deliver on his promises or to fail to follow through on his warnings — would be to deny himself.</p>
          <p>This reading is supported by the foundational self-revelation of God in Exodus 34:6-7 (LXX), which holds together mercy and judgment as equally characteristic of God's nature: "The LORD, the LORD, a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger, and abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness (<hi type="italic">alethinos</hi>), keeping steadfast love for thousands, forgiving iniquity and transgression and sin, but who will by no means clear the guilty." God's faithfulness includes both the positive (forgiving iniquity) and the negative (not clearing the guilty). To omit either would be to act unfaithfully to his own character.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">menei</hi> ("he remains") echoes <hi type="italic">menein</hi> ("to remain, to abide"), which throughout the Pauline corpus and the broader NT describes constancy in a position or state. Christ remains in his faithfulness. He does not shift, waver, or capitulate. He is the same in his promises and in his warnings. The unfaithful may change; he does not.</p>
          <p>The faithful saying, taken as a whole, presents a symmetrical architecture:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item><hi type="bold">Lines 1-2:</hi> Positive conditions with positive outcomes (dying/living, enduring/reigning)</item>
            <item><hi type="bold">Lines 3-4:</hi> Negative conditions with negative outcomes (denying/being denied, faithlessness/God's faithful execution of consequences)</item>
          </list>
          <p>The saying is not a comforting assurance that faithlessness has no consequences. It is a comprehensive statement of the reliability of Christ's word: what he promised, he will do; what he warned, he will fulfill. The hearer is invited to locate themselves within the saying — to be among those who endure, not among those who deny.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.14-2Tim.2.19">
          <title>Verses 14-19: The Threat of False Teaching — Gangrene in the Community</title>
          <p>"Remind them of these things, and charge them before God not to quarrel about words, which does no good, but only ruins the hearers. Do your best to present yourself to God as one approved, a worker who has no need to be ashamed, rightly handling the word of truth. But avoid irreverent babble, for it will lead people into more and more ungodliness, and their talk will spread like gangrene. Among them are Hymenaeus and Philetus, who have swerved from the truth, saying that the resurrection has already happened. They are upsetting the faith of some. But God's firm foundation stands, bearing this seal: 'The Lord knows those who are his,' and, 'Let everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity.'"</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Remind them of these things"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tauta hypomimneske</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">hyomimneskein</hi> ("to remind, to call to remembrance, to bring back to mind") is a present imperative: keep reminding, make this a habitual practice. The pronoun <hi type="italic">tauta</hi> ("these things") refers back to everything Paul has just stated — the chain of transmission, the soldier-athlete-farmer metaphors, the gospel of the risen Davidic Messiah, and above all the faithful saying with its conditions and consequences. Timothy is to keep these truths before the community's consciousness.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And charge them before God not to quarrel about words"</hi> (<hi type="italic">diamartyromenos enopion tou theou me logomachein</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">diamartyresthai</hi> ("to solemnly charge, to testify earnestly") is the same word used in 1 Timothy 5:21 for the cosmic charge before God, Christ Jesus, and the elect angels. The solemnity is the same here: this charge is made <hi type="italic">enopion tou theou</hi> ("before God, in God's presence"). The infinitive <hi type="italic">logomachein</hi> ("to fight about words, to quarrel over terminology") appears only here in the New Testament. It is a compound of <hi type="italic">logos</hi> ("word") + <hi type="italic">machesthai</hi> ("to fight, to battle, to war"). The word fights Paul opposes are not the legitimate exercise of careful teaching (which he commends in the next verse) but the contentious wrangling over terminology that produces no edification.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which does no good, but only ruins the hearers"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ep' ouden chresimon, epi katastrophe ton akouonton</hi>). The adjective <hi type="italic">chresimon</hi> ("useful, profitable, beneficial") with the negative <hi type="italic">ouden</hi> ("nothing") states that word-quarreling has zero benefit. The noun <hi type="italic">katastrophe</hi> ("ruin, destruction, overturning, overthrow") is the word from which "catastrophe" derives. It means the complete overturning and destruction of something. The genitive <hi type="italic">ton akouonton</hi> ("of those who hear, of the listeners") identifies who is destroyed: the audience. The teachers who engage in word-fights may be unharmed by their own activity, but those who listen to them are ruined. The word <hi type="italic">katastrophe</hi> echoes the "shipwreck" of 1 Timothy 1:19 (<hi type="italic">nauagein</hi>) — in both cases, the false teaching destroys those who encounter it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Do your best to present yourself to God as one approved"</hi> (<hi type="italic">spoudason seauton dokimon parastesai to theo</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">spoudazein</hi> ("to be diligent, to make every effort, to be eager, to hasten") conveys urgency: Timothy is to apply himself with maximal effort. The verb <hi type="italic">paristanai</hi> ("to present, to place beside, to stand before") describes Timothy presenting himself before God as before an examiner or a judge. The adjective <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> ("approved, tested, proven, genuine") describes something that has been tested and found authentic. The word was used for metals that had been assayed and found pure, for coins that had been tested and found to be genuine rather than counterfeit. Timothy is to be a proven, tested, authentic worker — one who can stand before God's examination without being found wanting.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"A worker who has no need to be ashamed"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ergaten anepaisychynton</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">ergates</hi> ("worker, laborer") identifies Timothy as one who works — not an idle theorist but an active laborer in the gospel. The adjective <hi type="italic">anepaischyntos</hi> ("not ashamed, having no cause for shame") is composed of the alpha-privative + <hi type="italic">epaischynesthai</hi> ("to be ashamed"). The theme of shame runs through the letter: Timothy is not to be ashamed of the testimony of the Lord (1:8); Paul is not ashamed of his chains (1:12); Onesiphorus was not ashamed of Paul's imprisonment (1:16). Now the shame language appears in a different register: Timothy should conduct his work so that he has no reason for shame before God. The false teachers should be ashamed (but are not); Timothy should not be ashamed (and will not be, if he works rightly).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Rightly handling the word of truth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">orthotomounta ton logon tes aletheias</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">orthotomein</hi> ("to cut straight, to handle rightly, to guide on a straight path") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of <hi type="italic">orthos</hi> ("straight, right, correct") + <hi type="italic">temnein</hi> ("to cut"). The literal meaning is "cutting straight" — as a craftsman cuts wood along a straight line, as a road-builder cuts a path directly through terrain. The metaphor describes precision, accuracy, and directness in handling the word of truth. The opposite of <hi type="italic">orthotomounta</hi> is the deviant, crooked, wandering handling of truth practiced by the false teachers — they do not cut straight but veer off into tangents, speculations, and distortions.</p>
          <p>The "word of truth" (<hi type="italic">logos tes aletheias</hi>) is the gospel. In Ephesians 1:13, Paul wrote: "In him you also, when you heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation, and believed in him, were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit." In Colossians 1:5: "the word of truth, the gospel." The word of truth is not any word or every word but the specific message of the gospel — the message about the risen Davidic Messiah that Paul has just summarized in verse 8. To handle this word rightly is to teach it accurately, apply it faithfully, and preserve it from distortion.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But avoid irreverent babble"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tas de bebelous kenophonias periistaso</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">periistasthai</hi> ("to stand around, to avoid, to shun, to keep away from") in the middle voice means to go around something, to give it wide berth. The noun <hi type="italic">kenophonia</hi> ("empty talk, fruitless discussion, meaningless chatter") combines <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> ("empty, void, without content") + <hi type="italic">phone</hi> ("voice, sound, utterance"). The adjective <hi type="italic">bebelos</hi> ("profane, unhallowed, common, irreverent") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:9 for the "unholy and profane" who are subject to the law's restraint, and in 1 Timothy 4:7 for "irreverent, silly myths." The empty talk Paul opposes is not merely unproductive; it is profane — it desecrates what is sacred by mixing the holy gospel with worthless speculation.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For it will lead people into more and more ungodliness"</hi> (<hi type="italic">epi pleion gar prokopsousin asebeias</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">prokoptein</hi> ("to advance, to progress, to go forward") is bitterly ironic: the empty talkers make progress — but their progress is in the wrong direction. They advance further into <hi type="italic">asebeia</hi> ("ungodliness, impiety, failure to honor God"). The comparative <hi type="italic">epi pleion</hi> ("to a greater extent, more and more") indicates escalation: the progression is not static but accelerating. Once the trajectory is set toward ungodliness, the movement increases in speed. This echoes 3:13, where "evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse" (<hi type="italic">prokopsousin epi to cheiron</hi>).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And their talk will spread like gangrene"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ho logos auton hos gangraina nomen hexei</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">gangraina</hi> ("gangrene, spreading sore, necrosis") describes the medical condition in which living tissue dies and the death spreads to surrounding tissue. The noun <hi type="italic">nome</hi> ("pasture, spreading, feeding") describes the grazing of an animal across a field — the gangrene "feeds" or "pastures" on healthy tissue, consuming and killing it as it spreads. The verb <hi type="italic">echein</hi> ("to have, to hold") with <hi type="italic">nomen</hi> means "to have pasture, to spread, to advance by feeding." The metaphor is devastating: the false teaching is not a self-contained error that remains with its originators. It is a necrotic disease that spreads through the body, killing tissue as it goes. The body is the community. The living tissue is the faith of the members. The gangrene consumes faith wherever it touches.</p>
          <p>The medical metaphor connects to the "sound teaching" (<hi type="italic">hygiainousa didaskalia</hi>, literally "healthy teaching") of 1 Timothy 1:10; 6:3; 2 Timothy 1:13; 4:3; Titus 1:9; 2:1. If sound teaching is healthy teaching, then false teaching is diseased teaching — and gangrene is among the most destructive diseases the ancient world knew. The contrast between the "word of truth" that Timothy is to handle rightly (v. 15) and the "talk" (<hi type="italic">logos</hi>) of the false teachers that spreads like gangrene (v. 17) is the contrast between health and necrosis, life and death.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Among them are Hymenaeus and Philetus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hon estin Hymenaios kai Philetos</hi>). Two individuals are named. Hymenaeus appeared in 1 Timothy 1:20, where he and Alexander had been "handed over to Satan that they may learn not to blaspheme." The discipline described in 1 Timothy 1:20 was pedagogical — its purpose was that they might "learn." The reappearance of Hymenaeus here, still teaching falsely, raises the possibility that the discipline did not achieve its intended effect, or that he has not yet completed the learning process, or that he resisted the correction entirely. His pairing with a new name, Philetus (not mentioned elsewhere in the NT), suggests that the false teaching has attracted new adherents since 1 Timothy was written.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Who have swerved from the truth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hoitines peri ten aletheian estochesan</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">astochein</hi> ("to miss the mark, to deviate from the target, to swerve") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:6 for those who "swerved from" the aims of love, good conscience, and sincere faith, and in 1 Timothy 6:21 for those who "swerved from the faith." The verb is an archery metaphor: they aimed and missed. The target they missed is <hi type="italic">ten aletheian</hi> ("the truth") — the truth of the gospel, the word of truth that Timothy is to handle rightly (v. 15). The preposition <hi type="italic">peri</hi> with the accusative ("concerning, about, in the area of") locates the miss: they have gone astray specifically in the domain of truth.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Saying that the resurrection has already happened"</hi> (<hi type="italic">legontes ten anastasin ede gegonenai</hi>). This is the specific content of their error. The noun <hi type="italic">anastasis</hi> ("resurrection, rising, standing up again") — the central eschatological hope of the early communities — is the subject. The adverb <hi type="italic">ede</hi> ("already") and the perfect infinitive <hi type="italic">gegonenai</hi> ("to have happened, to have taken place") relocate the resurrection from the future to the past. These teachers claim that the resurrection is a completed event, not a future expectation.</p>
          <p>The distortion is serious because it undermines the entire eschatological framework of the gospel. If the resurrection has already happened, then there is no future bodily resurrection to hope for. The "hope" that Paul mentioned in verse 10 — "salvation with eternal glory" — loses its content. What remains is either a purely spiritual reinterpretation of resurrection (the soul has already risen; the body does not matter) or a realized eschatology that collapses the future into the present and eliminates the need for endurance and perseverance.</p>
          <p>This connects directly to the faithful saying of verses 11-13. The saying promised: "if we died with him, we will also live with him; if we endure, we will also reign with him." The future tenses (<hi type="italic">syzesomen, symbasileuomen</hi>) depend on the reality of a future resurrection and reign. If Hymenaeus and Philetus are right that the resurrection has already happened, then the future living and reigning promised by the saying are either already realized or irrelevant. The saying's call to endurance becomes pointless if the promised outcome has already arrived. Why endure suffering for a future hope that is, in fact, already past?</p>
          <p>Paul's command to "remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead" (v. 8) also takes on new significance in this light. The resurrection Paul commands Timothy to remember is the resurrection of Jesus — a specific, historical, bodily event. If Hymenaeus and Philetus have redefined "resurrection" to mean something other than bodily rising, they have distorted the very gospel Paul preaches. Timothy is to remember the bodily resurrection of Jesus because that resurrection is the pattern and guarantee of the future resurrection of believers (cf. 1 Corinthians 15:20-23, where Christ's resurrection is "the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep").</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"They are upsetting the faith of some"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai anatrepousin ten tinon pistin</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">anatrepein</hi> ("to overturn, to upset, to overthrow, to destroy") means to turn something upside down, to demolish it, to wreck it. The object is <hi type="italic">ten pistin</hi> — "the faith" — specifically the faith of <hi type="italic">tinon</hi> ("of some, of certain people"). Not the faith of all, but the faith of some. The false teaching is having its effect: real people in the community are having their faith overturned, destroyed, demolished. The gangrene is spreading; the tissue is dying.</p>
          <p>The phrase echoes 1 Timothy 1:19, where certain persons had "made shipwreck of their faith" (<hi type="italic">peri ten pistin enauagesan</hi>). The metaphors are different (shipwreck there, overturning here) but the reality is the same: faith can be destroyed by false teaching. The faith of believers is not invulnerable; it can be wrecked, overturned, upset. The community exists in genuine danger.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But God's firm foundation stands"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ho mentoi stereos themelios tou theou hesteken</hi>). The adversative <hi type="italic">mentoi</hi> ("but, nevertheless, however") introduces a contrast with the alarming spread of false teaching. Despite the gangrene, despite the overturning of some people's faith, something stands firm. The noun <hi type="italic">themelios</hi> ("foundation, base, underlying support") describes the lowest level of a structure — the part on which everything else rests. The adjective <hi type="italic">stereos</hi> ("firm, solid, hard, unyielding") characterizes this foundation as immovable. The genitive <hi type="italic">tou theou</hi> ("of God") identifies the foundation as God's own — it belongs to God, it was laid by God, it is God's construction. The perfect tense <hi type="italic">hesteken</hi> ("stands, has been standing, remains standing") indicates a settled state: the foundation was established and continues to stand.</p>
          <p>What is the foundation? The context suggests that it is the community of God's people — the assembly (<hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi>) that Paul called "the pillar and buttress of the truth" in 1 Timothy 3:15. But it may also refer to God's purpose itself, which stands despite human failure. The emphasis is on stability: whatever the false teachers do, God's foundation is not shaken by it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Bearing this seal: 'The Lord knows those who are his'"</hi> (<hi type="italic">echon ten sphragida tauten: egnō kyrios tous ontas autou</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">sphragis</hi> ("seal, stamp, mark of ownership, mark of authenticity") describes the inscription on the foundation stone. In the ancient world, foundation stones of important buildings were inscribed with identifying marks — the name of the builder, the dedication, the purpose. The seal authenticates and identifies. The foundation of God bears a seal — an inscription that declares its identity and purpose.</p>
          <p>The first inscription — <hi type="italic">egnō kyrios tous ontas autou</hi> ("The Lord knew/knows those who are his") — is drawn from Numbers 16:5 (LXX). The context in Numbers is the rebellion of Korah, Dathan, and Abiram against the leadership of Moses and Aaron. Korah and 250 leaders of the congregation challenged Moses: "You have gone too far! For all in the congregation are holy, every one of them, and the LORD is among them. Why then do you exalt yourselves above the assembly of the LORD?" (Numbers 16:3). Moses' response: "In the morning the LORD will show who is his and who is holy, and will bring him near to him. The one whom he chooses he will bring near to him" (Numbers 16:5 LXX: <hi type="italic">gnōsetai ho theos tous ontas autou kai tous hagious</hi> — "God will know those who are his and those who are holy").</p>
          <p>The outcome of the Korah rebellion was devastating: the earth opened and swallowed Korah, Dathan, and Abiram with their households (Numbers 16:31-33), and fire consumed the 250 who offered incense (Numbers 16:35). The allusion is not comforting in the way it is sometimes read. Paul is not merely saying "God knows who his people are, so don't worry." He is invoking a narrative in which God distinguished between those who were truly his and those who had rebelled against his appointed leaders — and the rebels were destroyed. The application to the current situation is pointed: Hymenaeus and Philetus have challenged the apostolic teaching just as Korah challenged Moses. God knows who is truly his, and the implication of the Korah narrative is that the rebels will face judgment.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">egnō</hi> ("knew") is the aorist of <hi type="italic">ginoskein</hi> ("to know, to recognize, to acknowledge"). In the LXX, God's "knowing" is not mere cognitive awareness but relational acknowledgment — it is the knowledge of covenant relationship. When God "knows" someone, he recognizes them as his own. Amos 3:2 (LXX): "You only have I known (<hi type="italic">egnōn</hi>) of all the families of the earth; therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities." God's knowing of Israel did not exempt them from punishment but made them more accountable. The knowing is selective and relational, and it carries responsibility as well as privilege.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And, 'Let everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity'"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai, aposteto apo adikias pas ho onomazōn to onoma kyriou</hi>). The second inscription on the foundation stone is a command directed not at God but at those who claim to belong to him. The verb <hi type="italic">aphistanai</hi> ("to depart, to withdraw, to stand away from, to remove oneself") in the imperative (<hi type="italic">aposteto</hi>) commands active separation — a deliberate movement away from <hi type="italic">adikia</hi> ("iniquity, unrighteousness, injustice, wrongdoing").</p>
          <p>This inscription appears to draw from Numbers 16:26 (LXX), where Moses said to the congregation: "Depart from the tents of these wicked men" (<hi type="italic">apostete apo ton skenon ton anthropon ton sklerōn touton</hi>) — the command to separate from the rebels before God's judgment fell. The connection to the Korah narrative reinforces the reading: just as Israel was commanded to separate from the rebels before the earth swallowed them, so those who name the Lord's name must depart from the iniquity represented by the false teachers.</p>
          <p>There may also be an echo of Isaiah 52:11 (LXX): "Depart, depart, go out from there; touch no unclean thing; go out from the midst of her; purify yourselves, you who bear the vessels of the LORD." The call to separation from uncleanness, addressed to those who serve God, carries the same logic: belonging to God requires separation from what opposes God.</p>
          <p>The two seals together state two complementary truths:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">God's side:</hi> The Lord knows who belongs to him. He distinguishes the genuine from the counterfeit. The false teachers may confuse the community, but they do not confuse God.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">The human side:</hi> Those who claim the Lord's name must depart from iniquity. Claiming the name without departing from iniquity is a contradiction that the foundation stone itself condemns.</p>
          <p>The combination means that the community cannot simply wait passively for God to sort things out. The command to "depart from iniquity" demands action from those who call themselves the Lord's. They must separate themselves from the false teaching and the false teachers. God's knowledge of his own does not eliminate human responsibility; it intensifies it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.20-2Tim.2.21">
          <title>Verses 20-21: Vessels in a Great House — The Possibility of Change</title>
          <p>"Now in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver but also of wood and clay, some for honorable use, some for dishonorable. Therefore, if anyone cleanses himself from what is dishonorable, he will be a vessel for honorable use, set apart as holy, useful to the master of the house, ready for every good work."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Now in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver but also of wood and clay"</hi> (<hi type="italic">en megale de oikia ouk estin monon skeue chrysa kai argyra alla kai xylina kai ostrakina</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">oikia</hi> ("house, household") connects to the <hi type="italic">oikos theou</hi> ("house of God") of 1 Timothy 3:15 and to the <hi type="italic">oikonomia theou</hi> ("stewardship of God") of 1 Timothy 1:4. The adjective <hi type="italic">megale</hi> ("great, large") indicates a substantial household. The noun <hi type="italic">skeuos</hi> ("vessel, container, instrument, utensil") describes the various implements found in such a household. The materials range from precious (<hi type="italic">chrysa</hi>, "golden"; <hi type="italic">argyra</hi>, "silver") to common (<hi type="italic">xylina</hi>, "wooden"; <hi type="italic">ostrakina</hi>, "earthenware, clay").</p>
          <p>The distinction is functional: <hi type="italic">kai ha men eis timen ha de eis atimian</hi> — "some for honor, some for dishonor." The noun <hi type="italic">time</hi> ("honor, value, respect") and its opposite <hi type="italic">atimia</hi> ("dishonor, shame, disgrace") describe the uses to which the vessels are put. In a great household, some vessels are used for noble purposes — serving guests, holding precious items, displaying at feasts. Others are used for menial or unclean purposes — waste, refuse, common labor. The metaphor is not about the inherent value of the vessel's material but about the use to which it is assigned.</p>
          <p>Paul uses a similar vessel metaphor in Romans 9:21-23: "Has the potter no right over the clay, to make out of the same lump one vessel for honorable use and another for dishonorable use?" In Romans 9, the emphasis falls on the potter's sovereignty. Here in 2 Timothy 2, the emphasis shifts dramatically — it falls on the vessel's capacity to change its status.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Therefore, if anyone cleanses himself from what is dishonorable"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ean oun tis ekkatharei heauton apo touton</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("therefore, then") draws the application from the metaphor. The verb <hi type="italic">ekkathairein</hi> ("to cleanse thoroughly, to purge, to purify") with the reflexive pronoun <hi type="italic">heauton</hi> ("himself") places the responsibility for cleansing on the vessel itself. The preposition <hi type="italic">apo touton</hi> ("from these things") identifies what must be cleansed away: the dishonorable things — the irreverent babble, the word-quarreling, the gangrene teaching, the iniquity that the previous verse commanded departure from.</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ean</hi> ("if") with the subjunctive (<hi type="italic">ekkatharei</hi>) presents a genuine, open condition: this may or may not happen. The cleansing is possible but not automatic. It depends on the person's action. The reflexive pronoun makes this inescapable: "if anyone cleanses himself." No one else cleanses the vessel; the vessel cleanses itself.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"He will be a vessel for honorable use"</hi> (<hi type="italic">estai skeuos eis timen</hi>). The future indicative <hi type="italic">estai</hi> ("he will be") states the result of the cleansing with the certainty of a promise: the one who cleanses himself will be a vessel for honor. The transformation is real and effective. The category can be changed. A vessel that was used for dishonorable purposes can become a vessel for honorable use through the process of cleansing.</p>
          <p>This is the most important structural element of the metaphor for the chapter's argument. The vessel metaphor does not describe fixed, permanent categories. It describes current function that can be altered through cleansing. The person who is presently associated with the dishonorable things — the false teaching, the empty talk, the iniquity — can become a vessel for honor by cleansing themselves from those associations. The metaphor answers the implicit question raised by verses 17-18: is there hope for those whose faith has been upset by the false teachers? The answer is yes — if they cleanse themselves.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Set apart as holy, useful to the master of the house, ready for every good work"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hegiasmenon, euchreston to despote, eis pan ergon agathon hetoimasmenon</hi>). Three qualities characterize the cleansed vessel:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="italic">Hegiasmenon</hi> ("set apart, sanctified, made holy") — the perfect passive participle of <hi type="italic">hagiazein</hi> ("to make holy, to sanctify, to set apart for sacred use"). The vessel has been set apart for God's use, removed from the common and dedicated to the sacred. The perfect tense indicates a completed state with ongoing results: the vessel stands sanctified.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="italic">Euchreston to despote</hi> ("useful to the master") — the adjective <hi type="italic">euchrestos</hi> ("useful, serviceable, beneficial") combines <hi type="italic">eu</hi> ("well") + <hi type="italic">chrestos</hi> ("usable, serviceable"). The dative <hi type="italic">to despote</hi> ("to the master, to the owner") identifies the one served: the master of the great house, which is God. The word <hi type="italic">despotes</hi> ("master, lord, owner") was used in 1 Timothy 6:1-2 for the master of slaves and carries the sense of absolute authority. A vessel for honor is one the master can use; a vessel for dishonor is one the master sets aside.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="italic">Eis pan ergon agathon hetoimasmenon</hi> ("prepared for every good work") — the perfect passive participle of <hi type="italic">hetoimazein</hi> ("to prepare, to make ready") describes readiness. The vessel is not merely clean but prepared — ready for deployment, available for service. The phrase <hi type="italic">pan ergon agathon</hi> ("every good work") echoes 1 Timothy 5:10 (the enrolled widow who "devoted herself to every good work") and anticipates 3:17 ("that the man of God may be complete, equipped for every good work"). The cleansed vessel is equipped for comprehensive usefulness.</p>
          <p>The verse's implications are profound. If the community is the great house, and if vessels within it can be either honorable or dishonorable, and if the difference is determined by whether the vessel cleanses itself from what is dishonorable, then the status of every member of the community is, in principle, changeable. Those who have been swept up in the false teaching can cleanse themselves and become vessels for honor. Those who are currently vessels for honor can, by implication, become vessels for dishonor if they fail to maintain their separation from iniquity. The metaphor is dynamic, not static. It describes a present condition that depends on present choices, not a permanent assignment determined before the vessel was made.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2.22-2Tim.2.26">
          <title>Verses 22-26: The Lord's Servant — Flight, Pursuit, and the Hope of Repentance</title>
          <p>"So flee youthful passions and pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace, along with those who call on the Lord from a pure heart. Have nothing to do with foolish, ignorant controversies; you know that they breed quarrels. And the Lord's servant must not be quarrelsome but kind to everyone, able to teach, patiently enduring evil, correcting his opponents with gentleness. God may perhaps grant them repentance leading to a knowledge of the truth, and they may come to their senses and escape from the snare of the devil, after being captured by him to do his will."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So flee youthful passions"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tas de neoterikas epithymias pheuge</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">pheugein</hi> ("to flee, to escape, to run away from") is a present imperative: keep fleeing, make it a habitual practice. The noun <hi type="italic">epithymia</hi> ("desire, longing, craving, passion") is qualified by the adjective <hi type="italic">neoterikos</hi> ("youthful, characteristic of youth"). These are not specified as exclusively sexual desires (though sexual desire is not excluded); the adjective <hi type="italic">neoterikos</hi> encompasses the full range of youthful tendencies: impulsiveness, love of controversy, desire for prestige, impatience with authority, the attraction of novelty. In the context of this chapter, where the false teachers generate controversies and word-fights, the "youthful passions" may include the intellectual appetite for speculative debate, the desire to be seen as sophisticated, and the temptation to engage the false teachers on their own terms rather than maintaining disciplined fidelity to the deposit.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace"</hi> (<hi type="italic">dioke de dikaiosynen, pistin, agapen, eirenen</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">diokein</hi> ("to pursue, to chase, to run after, to prosecute") creates a contrast with <hi type="italic">pheugein</hi>: Timothy is to run away from the passions and run toward four virtues. The list echoes 1 Timothy 6:11, where Paul commanded Timothy: "Pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, steadfastness, gentleness." The overlap (righteousness, faith, love) confirms that these are the core virtues of the gospel life.</p>
          <p>The four virtues:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="italic">Dikaiosyne</hi> ("righteousness, justice, right conduct") — conformity to God's standard, the lived expression of being right with God.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="italic">Pistis</hi> ("faith, trust, faithfulness, fidelity") — the relational trust in God that the faithful saying has just made central. This is the faith that endures (v. 12), not the faith that is upset (v. 18).</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="italic">Agape</hi> ("love") — the aim of the charge in 1 Timothy 1:5, the love that issues from a pure heart, good conscience, and sincere faith.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="italic">Eirene</hi> ("peace") — not merely the absence of conflict but the positive state of wholeness and reconciliation. In a community torn by controversies and factions, the pursuit of peace is an active, demanding task.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Along with those who call on the Lord from a pure heart"</hi> (<hi type="italic">meta ton epikaloumenon ton kyrion ek katharas kardias</hi>). The preposition <hi type="italic">meta</hi> ("with, together with, alongside") identifies companions: Timothy is not to pursue these virtues alone but alongside a community of fellow pursuers. The participle <hi type="italic">epikaloumenon</hi> (from <hi type="italic">epikaleisthai</hi>, "to call upon, to invoke, to appeal to") describes those who call on the Lord — the common self-description of believers (Acts 9:14, 21; Romans 10:12-13; 1 Corinthians 1:2). The qualification <hi type="italic">ek katharas kardias</hi> ("from a pure heart") echoes 1 Timothy 1:5, where the aim of the charge was "love that issues from a pure heart" (<hi type="italic">ek katharas kardias</hi>). The pure heart is the undivided heart — the heart that is oriented toward God without contamination by the impurities of the false teaching. Timothy is to find his community among those whose hearts are pure, not among those whose teaching spreads like gangrene.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Have nothing to do with foolish, ignorant controversies"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tas de moras kai apaideutos zeteseis paraitou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">paraiteisthai</hi> ("to refuse, to decline, to reject") appeared in 1 Timothy 5:11 for refusing to enroll younger widows. Here it means to refuse engagement with the controversies. The adjective <hi type="italic">moros</hi> ("foolish, stupid, dull") and <hi type="italic">apaideuotos</hi> ("uneducated, undisciplined, untrained, ignorant") — from the alpha-privative + <hi type="italic">paideuein</hi> ("to educate, to train, to discipline") — characterize the controversies as both foolish and arising from lack of proper education. The noun <hi type="italic">zetesis</hi> ("controversy, debate, investigation, dispute") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:4 for the "speculations" that myths and genealogies promote. These are the same kinds of disputes: they are not genuine intellectual inquiry but undisciplined, misdirected, foolish wrangling.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"You know that they breed quarrels"</hi> (<hi type="italic">eidos hoti gennosi machas</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">gennan</hi> ("to beget, to give birth to, to produce, to generate") with <hi type="italic">machas</hi> ("fights, battles, quarrels") personifies the controversies as parents that give birth to conflict. The word <hi type="italic">mache</hi> ("fight, battle, quarrel") is the noun form of the <hi type="italic">machesthai</hi> component in <hi type="italic">logomachein</hi> ("word-fighting") from verse 14. The connection is explicit: the foolish controversies produce the word-fights Paul has already condemned.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And the Lord's servant must not be quarrelsome"</hi> (<hi type="italic">doulon de kyriou ou dei machesthai</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">doulos</hi> ("servant, slave, bondservant") with the genitive <hi type="italic">kyriou</hi> ("of the Lord") identifies Timothy's fundamental status: he is the Lord's slave. The verb <hi type="italic">dei</hi> ("it is necessary, it must be") states an absolute requirement. The infinitive <hi type="italic">machesthai</hi> ("to fight, to quarrel, to be contentious") is negated: the Lord's servant must not be a fighter. This is not a prohibition of all conflict — Paul has been commanding Timothy to charge, correct, and rebuke throughout both letters. It is a prohibition of the kind of contentious, quarrelsome temperament that the false teachers exhibit. The Lord's servant engages in correction; the Lord's servant does not engage in fighting.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But kind to everyone, able to teach, patiently enduring evil"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla epion einai pros pantas, didaktikon, anexikakon</hi>). Three positive qualities replace the quarrelsome temperament:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="italic">Epios</hi> ("gentle, kind, mild, caring") — the word describes the gentleness of a nurse with children (cf. 1 Thessalonians 2:7, where Paul described himself as "gentle among you, like a nursing mother taking care of her own children"). The phrase <hi type="italic">pros pantas</hi> ("toward all, to everyone") makes the gentleness universal: not merely kind to friends but kind to everyone, including opponents.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="italic">Didaktikos</hi> ("able to teach, skilled in teaching, apt at instruction") — the same word appeared as a qualification for the overseer in 1 Timothy 3:2. Teaching ability is not merely knowledge of content but the capacity to communicate truth effectively. The Lord's servant corrects error not through force but through competent instruction.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="italic">Anexikakos</hi> ("patient when wronged, bearing evil without resentment, enduring ill-treatment") — composed of <hi type="italic">anechein</hi> ("to bear, to endure") + <hi type="italic">kakos</hi> ("evil, harm, wrong"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. It describes the capacity to absorb mistreatment without retaliation, to endure harm without striking back. The Lord's servant is not immune to wrong but is able to bear it with patient endurance.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Correcting his opponents with gentleness"</hi> (<hi type="italic">en prauteti paideuonta tous antidiatithemenous</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">prautes</hi> ("gentleness, meekness, mildness, humility") is not weakness but strength under control — the quality of a powerful horse that has been trained to respond to a gentle hand. The verb <hi type="italic">paideuein</hi> ("to train, to educate, to discipline, to correct") — the same word used in 1 Timothy 1:20 for the pedagogical purpose of handing Hymenaeus and Alexander over to Satan ("that they may learn") — here describes the Lord's servant's activity toward opponents. The participle <hi type="italic">antidiatithemenous</hi> (from <hi type="italic">antidiatithesthai</hi>, "to set oneself in opposition, to oppose, to resist") describes those who have positioned themselves against the truth. They are opponents, resisters, those who stand on the other side.</p>
          <p>The instruction is remarkable: the opponents are to be corrected, not destroyed; educated, not excommunicated; and the correction is to be administered with gentleness, not with violence. The Lord's servant confronts error by teaching truth gently, not by attacking error aggressively.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"God may perhaps grant them repentance leading to a knowledge of the truth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">mepote do autois ho theos metanoian eis epignosin aletheias</hi>). The particle <hi type="italic">mepote</hi> ("perhaps, lest perhaps, in the hope that") introduces genuine uncertainty. The outcome is not guaranteed. God may grant repentance; he may not. The verb <hi type="italic">didonai</hi> ("to give, to grant") identifies repentance as a gift from God, not merely a human decision. The noun <hi type="italic">metanoia</hi> ("repentance, change of mind, turning") describes a fundamental reorientation of thought and life. The phrase <hi type="italic">eis epignosin aletheias</hi> ("toward a full knowledge of the truth") identifies the destination of repentance: the truth from which the opponents have swerved (v. 18). The noun <hi type="italic">epignosis</hi> (<hi type="italic">epi</hi> + <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi>, "full knowledge, deep knowledge, recognition") is a strengthened form of <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> — not mere acquaintance but thorough, experiential knowledge.</p>
          <p>The structure is: God gives repentance; repentance leads to knowledge of truth. The gift comes from God; the knowledge comes through the repentance. The opponents cannot arrive at truth on their own — they need God to grant them the capacity to turn around. This is why the Lord's servant corrects with gentleness: harsh treatment would close the door that God might open. The servant creates the conditions under which God might act; God does the acting.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And they may come to their senses and escape from the snare of the devil"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ananepsosi ek tes tou diabolou pagidos</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">ananephein</hi> ("to come to one's senses, to become sober again, to recover from intoxication") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of <hi type="italic">ana</hi> ("up, again") + <hi type="italic">nephein</hi> ("to be sober, to be clear-headed"). The metaphor is of waking from a drugged or drunken state: the opponents are not merely mistaken but intoxicated, their minds clouded, their judgment impaired. The repentance God might grant would be a sobering-up, a return to clear-headedness.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">pagis</hi> ("snare, trap, noose") describes a hunting device used to capture animals. In the LXX, the <hi type="italic">pagis</hi> is a recurring image for the traps that ensnare the unwary: Psalm 91:3 (LXX 90:3), "He will deliver you from the snare of the fowler"; Psalm 124:7 (LXX 123:7), "We have escaped like a bird from the snare of the fowlers; the snare is broken, and we have escaped." The snare belongs to <hi type="italic">tou diabolou</hi> ("the devil, the slanderer, the accuser"). The opponents are not merely wrong; they are trapped. They are held in a snare set by the devil.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"After being captured by him to do his will"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ezogemenoi hyp' autou eis to ekeinou thelema</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">zogrein</hi> ("to capture alive, to take alive, to catch") appears only here and in Luke 5:10 in the New Testament. In Luke 5:10, Jesus tells Simon Peter: "From now on you will be catching people" (<hi type="italic">anthropous ese zogreon</hi>) — a positive use of the metaphor. Here the usage is negative: the opponents have been captured alive by the devil. The perfect passive participle (<hi type="italic">ezogremenoi</hi>) describes their current state: they have been captured and remain captured. The purpose of their capture is stated: <hi type="italic">eis to ekeinou thelema</hi> — "for his will, to do his will." The pronoun <hi type="italic">ekeinou</hi> ("of that one, his") is ambiguous — it could refer to the devil (they do the devil's will) or to God (they are captured by the devil, but the purpose is ultimately God's will). Most naturally in context, it refers to the devil: the opponents have been captured by the devil and now serve the devil's purposes, whether they realize it or not.</p>
          <p>This final verse completes the chapter's account of the false teachers' situation. They have swerved from the truth (v. 18), their teaching spreads like gangrene (v. 17), they are upsetting the faith of some (v. 18), and they are themselves captives of the devil, doing his will (v. 26). Yet the chapter does not close the door on them. The possibility of repentance exists — God "may perhaps" grant it. The Lord's servant works toward that possibility through gentle, patient correction. The hope is that the captives may "come to their senses" — that the intoxication may lift, the snare may break, and they may return to the truth.</p>
          <p>The connection to 1 Timothy 1:20 is structural. There, Hymenaeus and Alexander were "handed over to Satan that they may learn not to blaspheme." The discipline was pedagogical — its purpose was learning. Here, the opponents are "captured by the devil" and the hope is that God may grant them repentance. In both cases, the situation involves captivity in the devil's domain and the possibility of correction. The language has shifted from discipline (handing over) to captivity (being captured), but the hope remains: learning, repentance, recovery, escape.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.2">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>2 Timothy 2 is the central chapter of Paul's final letter, containing its most urgent instructions for the faithful transmission and defense of the gospel. Every section addresses Timothy's responsibility in the face of Paul's approaching death and the continuing threat of false teaching.</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Verses 1-2</hi> establish the chain of transmission. In the face of mass desertion (1:15), Timothy must be strengthened in grace and must entrust the gospel to faithful people who can teach others. The gospel's survival depends on the quality of each link in the chain.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Verses 3-7</hi> employ three metaphors — soldier, athlete, farmer — to describe the character required for faithful service: single-minded devotion (the soldier who avoids entanglement), disciplined compliance with the rules (the athlete who competes lawfully), and patient labor with expectation of reward (the farmer who works hard). Timothy must think over these metaphors and trust the Lord for understanding.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Verses 8-10</hi> ground everything in the gospel itself: the risen Davidic Messiah. Paul suffers for this gospel to the point of chains, but the word of God is not chained. His endurance serves the elect, so that they may obtain salvation with eternal glory.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold">Verses 11-13</hi> present the faithful saying, which is the theological center of the chapter. Four conditional statements establish a comprehensive framework of reliability: those who died with Christ will live with him; those who endure will reign with him; those who deny him will be denied by him; and when humans prove faithless, Christ remains faithful to his own stated terms — both his promises and his warnings — because he cannot deny himself. The saying holds together promise and warning as equally binding, equally certain, equally grounded in Christ's character.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold">Verses 14-19</hi> identify the specific threat. Timothy must remind the community of these truths and charge them not to quarrel about words. He must handle the word of truth rightly — cutting straight, not veering off. The irreverent babble of the false teachers spreads like gangrene, and Hymenaeus and Philetus are named as examples: they have swerved from the truth by teaching that the resurrection has already happened, and they are upsetting the faith of some. Yet God's firm foundation stands, bearing a double seal drawn from the Korah narrative in Numbers 16: the Lord knows who is truly his (and will judge the rebels), and those who claim his name must depart from iniquity (and separate from the rebels before judgment falls).</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold">Verses 20-21</hi> use the vessel metaphor to assert the possibility of change within the household. A great house contains vessels for honor and dishonor, but the categories are not fixed: if anyone cleanses himself from what is dishonorable, he will be a vessel for honorable use, set apart as holy, useful to the master, prepared for every good work. The metaphor is dynamic, not deterministic. Present status depends on present choices.</p>
          <p>(7) <hi type="bold">Verses 22-26</hi> describe Timothy's personal conduct. He is to flee youthful passions, pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace alongside the pure-hearted community, and refuse engagement with foolish controversies. As the Lord's servant, he must not be quarrelsome but kind, teachable, patient under mistreatment, and gentle in correcting opponents — in the hope that God may grant them repentance, leading them to the knowledge of truth, and that they may wake from their intoxication and escape the devil's snare.</p>
          <p>The chapter's controlling architecture is the tension between the certainty of God's foundation and the genuine danger of human failure. God's foundation stands firm (v. 19), and Christ remains faithful (v. 13). But people within the community can swerve from the truth (v. 18), have their faith overturned (v. 18), deny Christ and be denied by him (v. 12), and be captured by the devil to do his will (v. 26). The community exists between these two realities: the unshakeable faithfulness of God and the real vulnerability of human faith. Timothy's task is to strengthen the chain of transmission, model faithful endurance, cut the word of truth straight, and gently correct those who have gone astray — not because the outcome is guaranteed, but because God "may perhaps" grant repentance. The chapter does not promise that every captive will be freed or every wanderer will return. It promises that the Lord's servant will do the work of correction with gentleness, and that God's foundation will stand regardless of how many vessels in the house prove dishonorable. The responsibility of every person who names the Lord's name is to depart from iniquity and cleanse themselves from what is dishonorable — because the categories of honor and dishonor are determined not by prior assignment but by present faithfulness.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="2Tim.3">
        <title>2 Timothy 3 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre and occasion:</hi> This is a letter from Paul to Timothy, written from imprisonment (1:8, 16; 2:9), under the shadow of Paul's approaching death (4:6-8). The letter functions as a final testament — the passing of responsibility, warning, and encouragement from Paul to his spiritual son and successor. The crisis of false teaching in Ephesus, which prompted 1 Timothy, has worsened. Hymenaeus, who was handed over to Satan in 1 Timothy 1:20, has reappeared in 2 Timothy 2:17 alongside a new companion, Philetus, teaching that the resurrection has already happened and upsetting the faith of some (2:18). The mass desertion Paul reported in 1:15 — "all who are in Asia turned away from me" — indicates that the social cost of remaining aligned with Paul and his gospel has become too high for most. Paul writes from isolation, with only Luke remaining with him (4:11), Demas having deserted (4:10), and others dispersed to various locations (4:10-12). The letter is personal, urgent, and suffused with the awareness that Paul's time is short and the work must continue without him.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (2 Timothy 2):</hi> Chapter 2 addressed the practical requirements and theological foundations for Timothy's ministry in a community under threat from false teaching. It opened with the imperative to transmit the gospel through a chain of faithful people (2:1-2), employed three metaphors — soldier, athlete, farmer — to describe the character required for faithful service (2:3-7), and grounded everything in the gospel of the risen Davidic Messiah for which Paul suffers in chains (2:8-10). The faithful saying of 2:11-13 established a comprehensive framework of reliability: dying with Christ leads to living with him, enduring leads to reigning with him, denying him leads to being denied by him, and Christ remains faithful to all his stated terms because he cannot deny himself. Paul then identified the specific threat: Hymenaeus and Philetus, whose teaching that the resurrection has already happened spreads like gangrene and upsets the faith of some (2:14-18). Against this threat, God's firm foundation stands, bearing a double seal drawn from the Korah rebellion in Numbers 16: "The Lord knows those who are his" and "Let everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity" (2:19). The vessel metaphor of 2:20-21 established that status within the household is not fixed but changeable through cleansing. And the chapter closed with instructions for the Lord's servant to correct opponents with gentleness, in the hope that God might grant them repentance and escape from the devil's snare (2:22-26).</p>
          <p>The closing of chapter 2 is critical for understanding chapter 3's opening. Chapter 2 ended on a note of qualified hope: the opponents are captured by the devil, doing his will (2:26), but God "may perhaps" grant them repentance (2:25). The opponents are real, their error is dangerous, their captivity is genuine — but the door to restoration has not been slammed shut. Chapter 3 will now intensify the description of these opponents, extend their behavior into an eschatological framework, and provide Timothy with the resources to stand against them.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (2 Timothy 4):</hi> Chapter 4 opens with what may be the most solemn charge in the entire letter: "I charge you in the presence of God and of Christ Jesus, who is to judge the living and the dead, and by his appearing and his kingdom: preach the word; be ready in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with complete patience and teaching" (4:1-2). The charge is eschatological in orientation — it invokes the judgment of Christ and his kingdom. The reason for the charge follows immediately: "For the time is coming when people will not endure sound teaching, but having itching ears they will accumulate for themselves teachers to suit their own passions, and will turn away from listening to the truth and wander off into myths" (4:3-4). This prediction directly extends the description of the false teachers in 3:1-9 and 3:13. Timothy is commanded: "As for you, always be sober-minded, endure suffering, do the work of an evangelist, fulfill your ministry" (4:5). Then comes Paul's farewell: "For I am already being poured out as a drink offering, and the time of my departure has come. I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith. Henceforth there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me on that day, and not only to me but also to all who have loved his appearing" (4:6-8). Paul's life is the model of what chapter 3 has described: faithful endurance through suffering, grounded in the scriptures, leading to the crown on "that day."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 3 connects the two:</hi> Chapter 2 ended by describing the opponents' condition (captured by the devil) and the hope of their recovery (God may grant repentance). Chapter 3 opens by placing these opponents within a broader eschatological framework: the "last days" bring "times of difficulty" (3:1), and the people described in the vice list of 3:2-5 are the same kind of people Paul has been warning about throughout the letter. The movement from chapter 2 to chapter 3 is not a change of subject but an escalation of the same argument. Chapter 2 dealt with specific false teachers (Hymenaeus and Philetus) and their specific error (the resurrection has already happened). Chapter 3 broadens the lens to describe the character type that produces such teachers, locates them within the eschatological pattern of the "last days," compares them to the Egyptian magicians who opposed Moses, and then pivots sharply to Timothy: "You, however" (3:10, <hi type="italic">sy de</hi>). The contrast between the opponents (3:1-9, 13) and Timothy (3:10-12, 14-17) is the chapter's structural backbone. Chapter 3 then provides Timothy with his ultimate resource — the sacred writings that have been his foundation since childhood (3:15-17) — which leads directly into the solemn charge of 4:1-2 to preach the word. The chapter is a bridge: it explains why the charge of chapter 4 is so urgent (because the opposition is so dangerous) and equips Timothy for the charge (because Scripture is so sufficient).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3.1-2Tim.3.5">
          <title>Verses 1-5: The Character of the "Last Days" — People Within the Community</title>
          <p>"But understand this, that in the last days there will come times of difficulty. For people will be lovers of self, lovers of money, proud, arrogant, abusive, disobedient to their parents, ungrateful, unholy, heartless, unappeasable, slanderous, without self-control, brutal, not loving good, treacherous, reckless, swollen with conceit, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God, having the appearance of godliness but denying its power. Avoid such people."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But understand this, that in the last days there will come times of difficulty"</hi> (<hi type="italic">touto de ginōske, hoti en eschatais hēmerais enstēsontai kairoi chalepoi</hi>). The imperative <hi type="italic">ginōske</hi> ("know, understand, recognize") commands Timothy to grasp what follows — not as speculation but as certain knowledge. The demonstrative <hi type="italic">touto</hi> ("this") points forward to the content of the warning.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en eschatais hēmerais</hi> ("in the last days") requires careful attention to its first-century meaning. The expression has deep roots in the LXX. In Isaiah 2:2 (LXX), "in the last days" (<hi type="italic">en tais eschatais hēmerais</hi>) introduces the vision of the mountain of the Lord being established and the nations streaming to it. In Micah 4:1 (LXX), the identical phrase opens the parallel vision. In Hosea 3:5 (LXX), "in the last days" (<hi type="italic">ep' eschatou tōn hēmerōn</hi>) describes Israel returning to seek the Lord their God and David their king. In Daniel 2:28 (LXX, Theodotion), Nebuchadnezzar's dream concerns "what will be in the last days" (<hi type="italic">en eschatais hēmerais</hi>). In Genesis 49:1 (LXX), Jacob gathers his sons to tell them "what will happen to you in the last days" (<hi type="italic">ep' eschatōn tōn hēmerōn</hi>). The phrase in the LXX consistently points to a decisive future period when God's purposes come to their climax — not necessarily the final moments of cosmic history, but the age of fulfillment toward which Israel's story has been moving.</p>
          <p>Crucially, the early communities understood themselves as already living in the last days. In Acts 2:17, Peter quotes Joel 2:28-32 (LXX 3:1-5) and applies it to the present: "In the last days (<hi type="italic">en tais eschatais hēmerais</hi>), God declares, I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh." Peter's quotation reframes Joel's "after these things" (<hi type="italic">meta tauta</hi>) as "in the last days," and declares that what is happening at Pentecost is the fulfillment of that prophecy. The last days have begun. In Hebrews 1:2, God has spoken "in these last days" (<hi type="italic">ep' eschatou tōn hēmerōn toutōn</hi>) through his Son — the author places the present moment within the last days. In 1 Peter 1:20, Christ was foreknown before the foundation of the world but "was made manifest in the last times" (<hi type="italic">ep' eschatou tōn chronōn</hi>) for the sake of believers. In 1 John 2:18, "it is the last hour" (<hi type="italic">eschatē hōra estin</hi>).</p>
          <p>Paul's use of "in the last days" here, therefore, does not describe a remote future period that has no bearing on Timothy's present situation. The last days are the present age — the age inaugurated by Christ's death and resurrection, the age in which the community now lives. The "times of difficulty" (<hi type="italic">kairoi chalepoi</hi>) that Paul describes are not distant predictions but descriptions of what Timothy is already encountering or will shortly encounter. The verb <hi type="italic">enstēsontai</hi> (from <hi type="italic">enistanai</hi>, "to be present, to stand in, to set in") in the future tense can describe what is about to begin or what is already setting in. In 2 Timothy 3:1-5, the vice list that follows describes people Paul tells Timothy to "avoid" (3:5) — a command that only makes sense if such people are present or imminent, not centuries away. The connection to the false teachers of chapter 2 is immediate: these are not abstract future figures but the very kind of people Timothy is already dealing with.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> ("time, season, decisive moment, opportunity") is distinct from <hi type="italic">chronos</hi> ("duration, period, span of time"). A <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> is a significant moment, a time charged with meaning and consequence. The adjective <hi type="italic">chalepos</hi> ("difficult, hard, harsh, fierce, dangerous") appears only here and in Matthew 8:28 in the New Testament, where it describes the Gadarene demoniacs as "so fierce that no one could pass that way." The word conveys genuine danger — these are not merely inconvenient times but genuinely threatening ones.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">The vice list (vv. 2-4):</hi> "For people will be lovers of self, lovers of money, proud, arrogant, abusive, disobedient to their parents, ungrateful, unholy, heartless, unappeasable, slanderous, without self-control, brutal, not loving good, treacherous, reckless, swollen with conceit, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God."</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") explains why the times will be difficult: they will be difficult because of the character of the people who populate them. The subject is <hi type="italic">hoi anthrōpoi</hi> ("the people, the human beings") — not a specific named group but a character type. The list contains nineteen descriptors, arranged with rhetorical care. The list opens with two compound words beginning with <hi type="italic">phil-</hi> ("lover of"): <hi type="italic">philautoi</hi> ("lovers of self") and <hi type="italic">philargyroi</hi> ("lovers of money"). It closes with another <hi type="italic">phil-</hi> compound: <hi type="italic">philēdonoi</hi> ("lovers of pleasure") set in explicit contrast with <hi type="italic">philotheoi</hi> ("lovers of God"). The framing is architectural: the list begins and ends with what people love, and the wrong loves (self, money, pleasure) have displaced the right love (God). The entire vice list is a catalog of what happens when the orientation of love is disordered.</p>
          <p>The individual terms:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Philautoi</hi></hi> ("lovers of self") — self-love as the organizing principle of life, the orientation that makes the self the center around which everything else revolves. This is the root from which the other vices grow.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Philargyroi</hi></hi> ("lovers of money, avaricious") — the same word appeared in 1 Timothy 6:10 in its noun form (<hi type="italic">philargyria</hi>, "love of money"), where Paul declared it "a root of all kinds of evils" through which "some have wandered away from the faith." The connection to the false teachers of 1 Timothy, who saw godliness as a "means of gain" (1 Tim 6:5), is direct.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Alazonēs</hi></hi> ("boasters, braggarts, pretentious people") — in Romans 1:30, Paul included <hi type="italic">alazōn</hi> in the vice list describing humanity that has exchanged God's truth for a lie. The <hi type="italic">alazon</hi> is one who claims more than is warranted, who inflates the self beyond reality.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Hyperēphanoi</hi></hi> ("proud, arrogant, haughty") — in the LXX, this word consistently describes those who set themselves against God. In Proverbs 3:34 (LXX): "The Lord opposes the arrogant (<hi type="italic">hyperēphanois</hi>) but gives grace to the humble." James 4:6 and 1 Peter 5:5 both quote this proverb. In Luke 1:51, Mary sings that God "has scattered the proud (<hi type="italic">hyperēphanous</hi>) in the thoughts of their hearts." The proud person is not merely socially obnoxious but positioned against God's own order.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Blasphēmoi</hi></hi> ("blasphemous, revilers, slanderers") — the word can refer to blasphemy against God or abusive speech against other people. In 1 Timothy 1:13, Paul described himself as formerly a "blasphemer" (<hi type="italic">blasphēmon</hi>), and in 1 Timothy 1:20, Hymenaeus and Alexander were handed over to Satan "that they may learn not to blaspheme" (<hi type="italic">mē blasphēmein</hi>). The reappearance of this word ties the vice list to the specific false teachers of the letter.</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Goneusin apeitheis</hi></hi> ("disobedient to parents") — the breakdown of the household order. In the LXX, the command to honor father and mother (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) is foundational. In Deuteronomy 21:18-21, the "stubborn and rebellious son" who will not obey his parents is subject to severe judgment. In Romans 1:30, disobedience to parents appears in the list of behaviors characterizing humanity that has turned from God. The inclusion of this domestic vice among theological and social ones indicates that the corruption is comprehensive — it penetrates even the most basic human relationships.</p>
          <p>(7) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Acharistoi</hi></hi> ("ungrateful, thankless") — the failure to acknowledge what one has received. Ingratitude is the refusal to recognize dependence, the denial of the gift-nature of existence. In Luke 6:35, God is "kind to the ungrateful (<hi type="italic">acharistous</hi>) and the evil." In Romans 1:21, humanity "did not honor God or give thanks (<hi type="italic">ēucharistēsan</hi>) to him" — the failure of gratitude is near the beginning of the descent into sin.</p>
          <p>(8) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Anosioi</hi></hi> ("unholy, impious") — the alpha-privative of <hi type="italic">hosios</hi> ("holy, devout, pious"). In 1 Timothy 1:9, the <hi type="italic">anosioi</hi> appeared in the list of those for whom the law was given: "the lawless and disobedient, the ungodly and sinners, the unholy and profane." The repetition across both letters binds the vice lists together.</p>
          <p>(9) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Astorgoi</hi></hi> ("heartless, without natural affection") — the alpha-privative of <hi type="italic">storge</hi> ("natural family affection"). The word describes the absence of the most basic human bond: the love between parents and children, between family members. It appeared in Romans 1:31 in the same position in Paul's list there. The breakdown extends beyond the household order of disobedience to parents to the utter absence of the affection that makes family life possible.</p>
          <p>(10) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Aspondoi</hi></hi> ("unappeasable, implacable, irreconcilable") — the alpha-privative of <hi type="italic">spondē</hi> ("a libation poured to ratify a treaty, a truce"). The <hi type="italic">aspondos</hi> is one who refuses to make peace, who will not enter into treaty, who remains in permanent hostility. No reconciliation is possible with such a person because they refuse the very concept of reconciliation.</p>
          <p>(11) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Diaboloi</hi></hi> ("slanderers, false accusers") — the plural of <hi type="italic">diabolos</hi>. The word means literally "one who throws across" (<hi type="italic">dia + ballein</hi>) — one who throws accusations, who creates division through false charges. In 1 Timothy 3:11, the deacons' wives (or female deacons) must not be <hi type="italic">diabolous</hi> ("slanderers"). The word is also the title used for the devil himself (<hi type="italic">ho diabolos</hi>, "the slanderer, the accuser"), and its use here for human beings who engage in the devil's characteristic activity is pointed: these people do the devil's work of accusation and division.</p>
          <p>(12) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Akrateis</hi></hi> ("without self-control, intemperate") — the alpha-privative of <hi type="italic">kratos</hi> ("strength, mastery, control"). The <hi type="italic">akratēs</hi> is one who lacks mastery over impulses, who is ruled by desires rather than ruling them. This stands in direct contrast to the <hi type="italic">sōphronismos</hi> ("self-discipline, sound-mindedness") that God's Spirit produces (1:7).</p>
          <p>(13) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Anēmeroi</hi></hi> ("brutal, savage, untamed") — the alpha-privative of <hi type="italic">hēmeros</hi> ("tame, gentle, civilized"). The word describes behavior that has crossed the line from human conduct into something feral. It appears only here in the New Testament.</p>
          <p>(14) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Aphilagathoi</hi></hi> ("not loving good, hostile to goodness") — the alpha-privative of <hi type="italic">philagathos</hi> ("lover of good"). In Titus 1:8, the overseer must be <hi type="italic">philagathos</hi> ("a lover of what is good"). The people described here are the exact opposite of what community leadership requires.</p>
          <p>(15) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Prodotai</hi></hi> ("traitors, betrayers") — from <hi type="italic">prodidonai</hi> ("to give over beforehand, to betray"). In Luke 6:16, Judas Iscariot is called <hi type="italic">prodotēs</hi> ("traitor"). In Acts 7:52, Stephen accuses the council: "you have now betrayed (<hi type="italic">prodotai</hi>) and murdered" the Righteous One. The word carries the weight of deliberate treachery — not accidental failure but purposeful betrayal of trust.</p>
          <p>(16) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Propeteis</hi></hi> ("reckless, headlong, rash") — from <hi type="italic">propiptein</hi> ("to fall forward"). The <hi type="italic">propetēs</hi> rushes forward without thought, acts without consideration, speaks without weighing consequences. In Acts 19:36, the town clerk of Ephesus urges the crowd not to be <hi type="italic">propeteis</hi> ("reckless") — the same city where Timothy now serves.</p>
          <p>(17) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Tetyphōmenoi</hi></hi> ("swollen with conceit, blinded by pride, puffed up") — the perfect passive participle of <hi type="italic">typhoun</hi> ("to wrap in smoke, to blind with conceit, to puff up"). The same verb appeared in 1 Timothy 3:6, where a new convert might become <hi type="italic">tetyphōtheis</hi> ("puffed up with conceit") and fall into the judgment of the devil. In 1 Timothy 6:4, the false teacher is <hi type="italic">tetyphōtai</hi> ("puffed up with conceit"), understanding nothing. The repetition is deliberate: the vice list describes the same character trait that has already been identified as the hallmark of the false teachers in both letters.</p>
          <p>(18-19) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Philēdonoi mallon ē philotheoi</hi></hi> ("lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God") — the final pair creates the climactic contrast. The comparative <hi type="italic">mallon ē</hi> ("rather than, more than") sets the two loves in opposition: pleasure versus God. These are not people who have no religious identity at all; they are people whose love for pleasure has displaced their love for God. The fact that they are "lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God" presupposes that they are in a position where love of God might be expected of them. They are not pagans who have never heard; they are people within or adjacent to the community who have chosen the wrong love.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Having the appearance of godliness but denying its power"</hi> (<hi type="italic">echontes morphōsin eusebeias tēn de dynamin autēs ērnēmenoi</hi>). This is the defining characteristic that locates these people within the community, not outside it. The noun <hi type="italic">morphōsis</hi> ("outward form, appearance, semblance, embodiment") appears elsewhere in the New Testament only in Romans 2:20, where Paul describes the Jewish teacher who has "the embodiment of knowledge and truth" (<hi type="italic">tēn morphōsin tēs gnōseōs kai tēs alētheias</hi>) in the law — the external structure that contains the reality. Here the <hi type="italic">morphōsis</hi> is of <hi type="italic">eusebeia</hi> ("godliness, piety, proper reverence toward God"), the same word that has been a key term throughout both letters. In 1 Timothy 2:2, the community was to pray for a peaceful life so they could live in "godliness" (<hi type="italic">eusebeia</hi>). In 1 Timothy 3:16, the "mystery of godliness" (<hi type="italic">to tēs eusebeias mystērion</hi>) was confessed in a hymnic fragment about Christ. In 1 Timothy 4:7-8, Timothy was to train himself "for godliness" (<hi type="italic">pros eusebeian</hi>), which "holds promise for the present life and also for the life to come." In 1 Timothy 6:3, sound teaching is teaching that "accords with godliness" (<hi type="italic">kata eusebeian</hi>). In 1 Timothy 6:5-6, the false teachers imagined godliness to be a "means of gain" (<hi type="italic">porismos</hi>), while genuine "godliness with contentment is great gain."</p>
          <p>These people possess the <hi type="italic">morphōsis</hi> — the external form, the visible shape, the religious surface — of <hi type="italic">eusebeia</hi>. They look godly. They participate in the community's life. They have the outward markers of devotion. But they have denied (<hi type="italic">ērnēmenoi</hi>, perfect middle participle of <hi type="italic">arneisthai</hi>, "to deny, to disown, to repudiate") its power (<hi type="italic">dynamin</hi>). The perfect tense indicates a settled, completed act with ongoing results: they denied the power at some point and continue in that denial. The verb <hi type="italic">arneisthai</hi> is the same verb used in the faithful saying of 2:12-13: "if we deny (<hi type="italic">arnesometha</hi>) him, he also will deny us." The echo is structural: denying the power of godliness is a form of denying Christ himself, because the power of godliness is the <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> that God gives (1:7, "God gave us a spirit not of cowardice but of power [<hi type="italic">dynameōs</hi>]"). The power (<hi type="italic">dynamis</hi>) they deny is the same power Paul attributed to God's Spirit in 1:7 — the power that enables courage, self-discipline, and faithfulness under pressure. To deny this power while maintaining the outward form is to retain religion as a social identity while gutting it of its transformative reality.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Avoid such people"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai toutous apotrepou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">apotrepesthai</hi> ("to turn away from, to avoid, to shun") in the middle voice means to turn oneself away. The present imperative commands ongoing practice: keep avoiding them, make it habitual. The demonstrative <hi type="italic">toutous</hi> ("these people") refers to the entire list just described. The command to avoid them presupposes that they are present and accessible — one cannot be told to avoid people who do not yet exist. This confirms that the "last days" are not a remote future but a present reality in which Timothy must make practical decisions about association.</p>
          <p>The command connects to 2:19, where the second seal on God's foundation read: "Let everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity." The same logic operates here: those who belong to God must separate from those who corrupt the community. The command also connects to 2:22, where Timothy was told to flee youthful passions and pursue righteousness "along with those who call on the Lord from a pure heart." The community Timothy is to cultivate is defined both positively (the pure-hearted) and negatively (avoiding those with the form but not the power of godliness).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3.6-2Tim.3.9">
          <title>Verses 6-9: The Infiltrators and Their Precedent — Jannes and Jambres</title>
          <p>"For among them are those who creep into households and capture weak women, burdened with sins and led astray by various passions, always learning and never able to arrive at a knowledge of the truth. Just as Jannes and Jambres opposed Moses, so these people also oppose the truth, people corrupted in mind and disqualified regarding the faith. But they will not get very far, for their folly will be plain to all, as was that of those two."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For among them are those who creep into households"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ek toutōn gar eisin hoi endynontes eis tas oikias</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") explains what makes these people particularly dangerous: their method is infiltration. The preposition <hi type="italic">ek</hi> ("out of, from among") identifies the infiltrators as a subset of the people described in the vice list — not all of them creep into households, but this is the activity of some among them. The verb <hi type="italic">endynein</hi> ("to creep in, to worm one's way in, to enter by stealth") appears only here in the New Testament. The word connotes covert, surreptitious entry — not a bold frontal approach but a worming inward through deception. The noun <hi type="italic">oikia</hi> ("house, household") describes the domestic setting. In the first-century world, the household was the basic unit of community life. The <hi type="italic">ekklēsia</hi> met in houses (Romans 16:5; 1 Corinthians 16:19; Colossians 4:15; Philemon 2). To creep into households was to infiltrate the very spaces where the community gathered, taught, and worshiped.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And capture weak women"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai aichmalōtizontes gynaikaria</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">aichmalōtizein</hi> ("to take captive, to capture as a prisoner of war, to lead into captivity") is a military term. The verb appears in Luke 21:24 for the Jews being "led captive" (<hi type="italic">aichmalōtisthēsontai</hi>) among all nations, and in Romans 7:23 for the law of sin "making me captive" (<hi type="italic">aichmalōtizonta me</hi>). In 2 Corinthians 10:5, Paul uses the same verb positively: "taking every thought captive (<hi type="italic">aichmalōtizontes</hi>) to obey Christ." The metaphor is imprisonment: the infiltrators take captives.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">gynaikarion</hi> (diminutive of <hi type="italic">gynē</hi>, "woman") is literally "little women" or "weak women." The diminutive form does not necessarily carry contempt for women as such; it describes vulnerability — women who are susceptible because of their specific condition, which is immediately specified. The description that follows explains why these particular women are vulnerable, not why women in general are inherently weak.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Burdened with sins and led astray by various passions"</hi> (<hi type="italic">sesōreumena hamartiais, agomena epithymiais poikilais</hi>). Two participles describe the condition that creates the vulnerability. First, <hi type="italic">sesōreumena</hi> (from <hi type="italic">sōreuein</hi>, "to heap up, to pile on, to load") — they are "loaded up" with sins (<hi type="italic">hamartiais</hi>). The perfect passive participle indicates a cumulative condition: sins have been piled on them over time and they carry the weight. Second, <hi type="italic">agomena</hi> (from <hi type="italic">agein</hi>, "to lead, to drive, to conduct") — they are "being led" by various (<hi type="italic">poikilais</hi>, "manifold, diverse, variegated") desires (<hi type="italic">epithymiais</hi>). The present passive participle describes an ongoing condition: they are continually driven by shifting desires. The combination of guilt (loaded with sins) and instability (driven by desires) makes them susceptible to teachers who promise new revelation, secret knowledge, or alternative paths to resolution. The false teachers exploit precisely this vulnerability.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Always learning and never able to arrive at a knowledge of the truth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">pantote manthanonta kai mēdepote eis epignōsin alētheias elthein dynamena</hi>). This is one of the chapter's most penetrating descriptions. The adverb <hi type="italic">pantote</hi> ("always, at all times") paired with <hi type="italic">mēdepote</hi> ("never, at no time") creates a devastating contrast: perpetual motion without arrival. The verb <hi type="italic">manthanein</hi> ("to learn, to study, to be taught") in the present participle describes ongoing, continuous learning. These captive women are not indifferent to religious instruction; they are voracious consumers of it. They are always in the posture of learning. But the learning never produces the result it should.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">epignōsis</hi> ("full knowledge, deep recognition, thorough understanding") is the strengthened form of <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> ("knowledge"). In 2:25, Paul expressed the hope that God might grant the opponents "repentance leading to a knowledge of the truth" (<hi type="italic">metanoian eis epignōsin alētheias</hi>). The identical phrase here — <hi type="italic">eis epignōsin alētheias</hi> — describes the destination these women can never reach. The contrast with 2:25 is structural: the opponents might receive repentance leading to knowledge of the truth, but the women they have captured are stuck in perpetual learning that never arrives at that knowledge. The problem is not intellectual capacity but spiritual orientation: driven by desires rather than by genuine seeking, loaded with unresolved guilt rather than cleansed by repentance, they mistake the accumulation of information for the attainment of truth.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dynamena</hi> (from <hi type="italic">dynasthai</hi>, "to be able, to have the capacity") states inability: they are "not able" to arrive. The inability is not constitutional — it is not that God made them incapable — but conditional: their current state of being burdened with sins and led by desires renders them unable to reach the truth. The possibility of change is implicit: if the burden were lifted and the desires reoriented, the capacity to arrive might be restored. This connects to the vessel metaphor of 2:20-21, where cleansing from what is dishonorable changes a vessel's status.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Just as Jannes and Jambres opposed Moses"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hon tropon de Iannēs kai Iambrēs antestēsan Mōysei</hi>). The comparative phrase <hi type="italic">hon tropon</hi> ("in the way that, just as") establishes a parallel between the current opponents and a specific pair of opponents from Israel's history. The names Jannes and Jambres do not appear in the canonical Hebrew scriptures or the LXX text of Exodus. They come from Jewish tradition that identified the Egyptian magicians who opposed Moses in Exodus 7:11, 22; 8:7, 18-19; 9:11. The names were widely known in Second Temple Judaism: they appear in the <hi type="italic">Damascus Document</hi> (CD 5:17-19), which warns that "Jannes and his brother" were raised up by Belial to oppose Moses and Aaron. They appear in <hi type="italic">Targum Pseudo-Jonathan</hi> on Exodus 1:15; 7:11. Pliny the Elder (<hi type="italic">Natural History</hi> 30.2.11) mentions "Iannes" as a famous magician. The tradition was well established and would have been recognized by Timothy and by any Jewish or Jewish-educated hearer.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">anthistanai</hi> ("to oppose, to resist, to set oneself against, to withstand") in the aorist (<hi type="italic">antestēsan</hi>) describes a completed act of opposition. The dative <hi type="italic">Mōysei</hi> ("Moses") identifies the object of opposition. The parallel is precise: as Jannes and Jambres opposed Moses — God's authorized agent for delivering Israel — so these false teachers oppose the truth. The comparison carries several implications drawn from the Exodus narrative:</p>
          <p>(1) Jannes and Jambres initially appeared to replicate what Moses did. In Exodus 7:11-12, Pharaoh's magicians "did the same things by their secret arts" — they turned their staffs into snakes, turned water to blood, and produced frogs. Their imitation was real enough to give Pharaoh a reason to dismiss Moses' authority. The false teachers similarly produce a convincing imitation of genuine teaching — they have the "appearance of godliness" (3:5) and engage in constant learning activity (3:7).</p>
          <p>(2) The imitation had limits. In Exodus 8:18-19, the magicians could not produce gnats: "the magicians tried by their secret arts to produce gnats, but they could not." They confessed to Pharaoh: "This is the finger of God." The imitation broke down when the reality exceeded the capacity of the counterfeit. Paul will make the same point in verse 9: the false teachers "will not get very far."</p>
          <p>(3) The magicians suffered judgment. In Exodus 9:11, "the magicians could not stand before Moses because of the boils, for the boils came upon the magicians as well as upon all the Egyptians." The opponents of God's authorized messenger were struck with the same plagues as the rest of the disobedient. The implication is that the false teachers who oppose the truth face a similar fate.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So these people also oppose the truth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">houtōs kai houtoi anthistantai tē alētheia</hi>). The present tense <hi type="italic">anthistantai</hi> ("are opposing") describes ongoing, current activity — these people are presently in active opposition to the truth. The dative <hi type="italic">tē alētheia</hi> ("the truth") identifies what they resist: not merely Paul's authority or Timothy's teaching, but the truth itself. In 2:15, Timothy was to "rightly handle the word of truth" (<hi type="italic">ton logon tēs alētheias</hi>). In 2:18, Hymenaeus and Philetus had "swerved from the truth" (<hi type="italic">peri tēn alētheian ēstochēsan</hi>). In 2:25, the hope was repentance leading to "knowledge of the truth" (<hi type="italic">epignōsin alētheias</hi>). The truth is the consistent reference point throughout the letter; the false teachers are consistently defined by their deviation from it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"People corrupted in mind and disqualified regarding the faith"</hi> (<hi type="italic">anthrōpoi katephtharmenoi ton noun, adokimoi peri tēn pistin</hi>). Two descriptions characterize these opponents:</p>
          <p>First, <hi type="italic">katephtharmenoi ton noun</hi> ("corrupted in mind, ruined in their thinking"). The verb <hi type="italic">kataphtheirein</hi> ("to corrupt thoroughly, to destroy, to ruin") in the perfect passive participle describes a completed state of corruption — their minds (<hi type="italic">nous</hi>, "mind, understanding, capacity for rational thought") have been corrupted and remain corrupted. In 1 Timothy 6:5, the same concept appeared with a different compound: <hi type="italic">diephtharmenōn ton noun</hi> ("people of corrupted mind"), describing the false teachers who imagine godliness to be a means of gain. The corruption is intellectual and moral simultaneously: these people cannot think straight about the truth because their mental capacity has been ruined.</p>
          <p>Second, <hi type="italic">adokimoi peri tēn pistin</hi> ("disqualified regarding the faith, failing the test concerning the faith"). The adjective <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> ("unapproved, failing the test, disqualified, counterfeit") is the exact opposite of <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> ("approved, tested, proven genuine") from 2:15, where Timothy was to present himself to God as one "approved" (<hi type="italic">dokimon</hi>). The word was used for metals that failed the assay, for coins that proved counterfeit. These people have been tested regarding the faith (<hi type="italic">peri tēn pistin</hi>, "concerning the faith, in the area of the faith") and have failed. The faith here is not merely personal trust but the faith — the body of apostolic teaching, the deposit that Timothy guards (1:14). When measured against this standard, the false teachers are found to be counterfeit.</p>
          <p>The contrast with Timothy is precise and deliberate: Timothy is to be <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> (2:15); these people are <hi type="italic">adokimoi</hi> (3:8). Timothy rightly handles the word of truth (2:15); these people oppose the truth (3:8). Timothy has a sound mind through God's Spirit of <hi type="italic">sōphronismos</hi> (1:7); these people have corrupted minds (3:8). The antithesis runs throughout the letter.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But they will not get very far, for their folly will be plain to all, as was that of those two"</hi> (<hi type="italic">all' ou prokopsousin epi pleion, hē gar anoia autōn ekdēlos estai pasin, hōs kai hē ekeinōn egeneto</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">prokoptein</hi> ("to advance, to make progress, to go forward") appeared in 2:16 to describe the "progress" of the empty talkers — their progress in ungodliness. Here the same verb is negated: they will not advance further (<hi type="italic">ou prokopsousin epi pleion</hi>). The irony is that in 3:13, Paul will say that "evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse" (<hi type="italic">prokopsousin epi to cheiron</hi>) — they will "advance" in the wrong direction. The two uses of <hi type="italic">prokoptein</hi> in this chapter create a paradox: the false teachers will not advance in their deceptive project (v. 9), yet they will advance in their own wickedness (v. 13). Their influence will be checked, but their personal corruption will continue.</p>
          <p>The reason their project will fail is that their <hi type="italic">anoia</hi> ("folly, mindlessness, lack of understanding") will become <hi type="italic">ekdēlos</hi> ("clearly visible, evident to all, perfectly obvious") to all (<hi type="italic">pasin</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">anoia</hi> is composed of the alpha-privative + <hi type="italic">nous</hi> ("mind") — it means "mindlessness, irrationality." This is the end point of the mental corruption described above: their minds, having been corrupted (<hi type="italic">katephtharmenoi ton noun</hi>), produce folly (<hi type="italic">anoia</hi>) that eventually becomes visible to everyone.</p>
          <p>The comparison <hi type="italic">hōs kai hē ekeinōn egeneto</hi> ("as was that of those two") returns to Jannes and Jambres. Their folly became plain — when they could not produce gnats (Exodus 8:18-19), when boils covered them (Exodus 9:11), when the plagues distinguished between Israel and Egypt in ways their magic could not replicate. The tradition that Jannes and Jambres were eventually exposed and destroyed was well known. Paul assures Timothy that the same exposure will occur with the current opponents: time will reveal the counterfeit for what it is.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3.10-2Tim.3.12">
          <title>Verses 10-12: The Contrast — Timothy's Following of Paul</title>
          <p>"You, however, have followed my teaching, my conduct, my aim in life, my faith, my patience, my love, my steadfastness, my persecutions and sufferings that happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra — which persecutions I endured, yet from them all the Lord rescued me. Indeed, all who desire to live a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"You, however, have followed my teaching"</hi> (<hi type="italic">sy de parēkolouthēsas mou tē didaskalia</hi>). The pronoun <hi type="italic">sy</hi> ("you," emphatic, placed first) combined with the adversative particle <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("however, but, on the other hand") creates the same contrastive structure as 2:1 (<hi type="italic">sy oun</hi>, "you then") — Timothy is set in sharp contrast with the people just described. They are lovers of self; Timothy follows Paul. They have the form of godliness but deny its power; Timothy has observed Paul's teaching, conduct, and suffering firsthand.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">parakolouthein</hi> ("to follow closely, to accompany, to trace carefully, to follow as a disciple") means more than casual acquaintance. The word describes someone who has walked alongside another person closely enough to have observed every aspect of their life. In Luke 1:3, the author uses the same verb to describe having "followed all things closely" (<hi type="italic">parēkolouthēkoti</hi>) — careful, first-hand investigation. Timothy has not merely heard Paul's teaching from a distance; he has accompanied Paul, observed his life, and internalized his example.</p>
          <p>What Timothy has followed is presented as a list of nine items, all governed by the possessive <hi type="italic">mou</hi> ("my, of me"). Each item is an aspect of Paul's life that Timothy has had opportunity to observe:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Didaskalia</hi></hi> ("teaching, instruction, doctrine") — what Paul taught. This is the content of the deposit (1:14; 2:2), the pattern of sound words (1:13), the word of truth (2:15). Timothy has followed Paul's teaching not in the sense of being a student in a classroom but in the sense of being an apprentice who has learned by proximity.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Agōgē</hi></hi> ("conduct, way of life, manner of living") — how Paul behaved. The word appears only here in the New Testament. It describes the total pattern of someone's life — their habits, decisions, relationships, daily behavior. Paul's teaching was authenticated by his conduct; Timothy observed both.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Prothesis</hi></hi> ("purpose, aim, intention, resolve") — what Paul was aiming at. The same word appeared in 1:9 for God's own "purpose" (<hi type="italic">prothesin</hi>) in saving and calling. Paul has a purpose that governs his life, and Timothy has observed it: the faithful transmission of the gospel, the building of communities, the service of the elect.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Pistis</hi></hi> ("faith, trust, faithfulness, fidelity") — Paul's personal trust in God and his faithfulness to the commission. Timothy has seen Paul believe under pressure, maintain trust when circumstances argued against it.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Makrothymia</hi></hi> ("patience, long-suffering, forbearance") — the capacity to bear with people and situations over extended periods without losing composure. The word describes the patience of God himself (Romans 2:4; 9:22) and is listed as a fruit of the Spirit (Galatians 5:22).</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Agapē</hi></hi> ("love") — the same love that is the aim of sound teaching (1 Tim 1:5), part of the spirit God gives (1:7), and the pursuit Timothy was commanded to follow (2:22). Timothy has observed Paul loving people — even difficult people, even opponents.</p>
          <p>(7) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Hypomonē</hi></hi> ("steadfastness, endurance, perseverance") — the same word used in the faithful saying of 2:12 (<hi type="italic">ei hypomenomen</hi>, "if we endure"). Paul does not merely commend endurance; he practices it, and Timothy has watched him practice it.</p>
          <p>(8) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Diōgmois</hi></hi> ("persecutions") — the external attacks Paul has suffered for the gospel's sake.</p>
          <p>(9) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Pathēmasin</hi></hi> ("sufferings") — the broader category of what Paul has experienced, encompassing but not limited to the persecutions.</p>
          <p>The list moves from the interior (teaching, conduct, purpose, faith) through relational qualities (patience, love, steadfastness) to external experiences (persecutions, sufferings). Timothy has observed the whole person — not just Paul's public ministry but his private character, not just his triumphs but his sufferings.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"That happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hoia moi egeneto en Antiocheia, en Ikoniō, en Lystrois</hi>). The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">hoia</hi> ("such as, the kind that") introduces specific examples. Three cities are named, and all three belong to Paul's first missionary journey as recorded in Acts 13-14:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Antioch</hi> (of Pisidia, not the Antioch of Syria) — Acts 13:50 records that in Pisidian Antioch, "the Jews incited the devout women of high standing and the leading men of the city, stirred up persecution (<hi type="italic">diōgmon</hi>) against Paul and Barnabas, and drove them out of their district."</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Iconium</hi> — Acts 14:1-6 describes how in Iconium, "an attempt was made by both Gentiles and Jews, with their rulers, to mistreat them and to stone them," and Paul and Barnabas fled.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Lystra</hi> — Acts 14:19-20 records that in Lystra, "Jews came from Antioch and Iconium, and having persuaded the crowds, they stoned Paul and dragged him out of the city, supposing him to be dead."</p>
          <p>The significance of these three cities for Timothy is personal and geographic. According to Acts 16:1, Timothy was from Lystra (or Derbe, the next city in the sequence). The persecutions Paul experienced at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra were the persecutions that occurred in Timothy's home region, the events that would have been local history for Timothy and his family. When Paul says "you have followed my persecutions and sufferings that happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra," he is reminding Timothy of events that Timothy's own community witnessed — events that occurred before or around the time Timothy joined Paul's mission. Timothy's grandmother Lois and mother Eunice (1:5) may have been among those who saw Paul stoned and dragged out of Lystra, supposing him dead. Timothy's decision to follow Paul (Acts 16:3) was made with full knowledge of what that following would cost — he had seen the cost with his own eyes.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which persecutions I endured, yet from them all the Lord rescued me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hoious diōgmous hypēnegka, kai ek pantōn me errysato ho kyrios</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">hypopherein</hi> ("to bear, to endure, to undergo") describes carrying a weight underneath — sustaining it, not collapsing under it. Paul bore the persecutions; they did not break him. The verb <hi type="italic">rhyesthai</hi> ("to rescue, to deliver, to save from danger") in the aorist (<hi type="italic">errysato</hi>) describes specific, completed acts of deliverance. The agent of rescue is <hi type="italic">ho kyrios</hi> ("the Lord").</p>
          <p>The language echoes the Psalms. Psalm 34:19 (LXX 33:20): "Many are the afflictions of the righteous, but the Lord delivers him out of them all" (<hi type="italic">ek pasōn autōn rhysetai auton ho kyrios</hi>). The verbal parallel is nearly exact. Paul's experience of deliverance from persecution is presented as the fulfillment of the psalmist's confidence: the Lord delivers the righteous from their afflictions. The statement does not mean that Paul was never harmed (he was stoned and left for dead at Lystra); it means that the Lord brought him through every trial without the destruction of his mission or his faith. The rescue is comprehensive — <hi type="italic">ek pantōn</hi> ("from all of them") — not selective.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Indeed, all who desire to live a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai pantes de hoi thelontes eusebōs zēn en Christō Iēsou diōchthēsontai</hi>). The particle <hi type="italic">kai... de</hi> ("and indeed, yes and") introduces a generalizing statement. Paul moves from his own specific experience to a universal principle. The scope is stated without qualification: <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") who possess a particular desire will face a particular outcome.</p>
          <p>The desire is specified: <hi type="italic">hoi thelontes eusebōs zēn en Christō Iēsou</hi> — "those who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus." The verb <hi type="italic">thelein</hi> ("to will, to wish, to desire, to purpose") describes not a passing inclination but a settled intention. The adverb <hi type="italic">eusebōs</hi> ("godly, piously, with proper reverence") is the adverbial form of <hi type="italic">eusebeia</hi>, the key term discussed above. The phrase <hi type="italic">en Christō Iēsou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus") specifies the sphere in which this godly living occurs — not generic piety but godliness rooted in and expressed through Christ.</p>
          <p>The outcome is stated as a future certainty: <hi type="italic">diōchthēsontai</hi> ("they will be persecuted"). The passive voice leaves the agent of persecution unspecified — it will come from various sources. The statement is absolute: all who desire this, will experience this. There are no exceptions. Godly living in Christ Jesus does not lead to social acceptance; it leads to persecution.</p>
          <p>This verse connects directly to the preceding description of the false teachers. Those who have the "appearance of godliness but deny its power" (3:5) escape persecution precisely because they have hollowed out their godliness. Their form without power does not threaten the world's arrangements. But genuine godliness — godliness with power, the kind animated by the Spirit of power, love, and self-discipline (1:7) — provokes resistance. The false teachers' avoidance of persecution is itself evidence of their inauthenticity: if all who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus will be persecuted, then those who are not persecuted have either not desired to live godly or are not doing so in Christ Jesus.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3.13">
          <title>Verse 13: The Trajectory of Deception</title>
          <p>"While evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse, deceiving and being deceived."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"While evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ponēroi de anthrōpoi kai goētes prokopsousin epi to cheiron</hi>). The adversative <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("but, while, on the other hand") sets the trajectory of the evil against the trajectory of the faithful. The adjective <hi type="italic">ponēros</hi> ("evil, wicked, bad, malicious") describes moral corruption, not merely error. The noun <hi type="italic">goēs</hi> ("impostor, sorcerer, charlatan, juggler") appears only here in the New Testament. In classical Greek, the <hi type="italic">goēs</hi> was originally a sorcerer or enchanter — one who used incantations and spells. By the first century, the word had broadened to mean a deceiver, a fraud, someone who uses trickery to mislead. The connection to the Jannes and Jambres comparison is deliberate: Jannes and Jambres were the paradigmatic <hi type="italic">goētes</hi> — sorcerers who used their craft to oppose God's truth. The current opponents are their successors.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">prokoptein</hi> ("to advance, to make progress") appeared in 2:16 for the false teachers' "progress" toward ungodliness, and in 3:9 for their limited advance (they will not get very far). Here the paradox is stated explicitly: they will advance (<hi type="italic">prokopsousin</hi>), but their advance is <hi type="italic">epi to cheiron</hi> ("toward the worse, from bad to worse"). Their progress is deterioration. The more they advance, the worse they become. This is the opposite of the genuine progress (<hi type="italic">prokopē</hi>) that Paul hoped for in the communities (cf. Philippians 1:25, "your progress and joy in the faith"). The false teachers' progress is regress — a deepening of corruption, not an ascent toward truth.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Deceiving and being deceived"</hi> (<hi type="italic">planōntes kai planōmenoi</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">planein</hi> ("to lead astray, to deceive, to cause to wander") appears in both the active participle (<hi type="italic">planōntes</hi>, "deceiving, leading others astray") and the passive participle (<hi type="italic">planōmenoi</hi>, "being deceived, being led astray themselves"). The double use creates a picture of mutual and self-reinforcing deception. The false teachers are not merely cynical manipulators who know the truth and deliberately hide it; they are themselves deceived. They lead others astray because they have themselves been led astray. The deception is both outward-directed (they deceive others) and inward-experienced (they are themselves in the grip of deception). This connects to 2:26, where the opponents were described as "captured by the devil to do his will" — they are instruments of a deception larger than themselves.</p>
          <p>The combination of active and passive deception also explains why the women they capture are "always learning and never able to arrive at a knowledge of the truth" (3:7): the teachers themselves do not possess the truth, so no amount of learning from them can produce it in their students. The blind lead the blind, and both end in the ditch (cf. Matthew 15:14; Luke 6:39).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3.14-2Tim.3.15">
          <title>Verses 14-15: Timothy's Foundation — What He Learned and From Whom</title>
          <p>"But as for you, continue in what you have learned and have firmly believed, knowing from whom you learned it, and how from childhood you have been acquainted with the sacred writings, which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But as for you, continue in what you have learned and have firmly believed"</hi> (<hi type="italic">sy de mene en hois emathes kai epistōthēs</hi>). The pronoun <hi type="italic">sy</hi> ("you," emphatic) combined with the adversative <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("but, however") creates the second major contrast of the chapter (the first was 3:10, <hi type="italic">sy de parēkolouthēsas</hi>). The evil and the impostors advance from bad to worse (3:13); Timothy, by contrast, is to continue (<hi type="italic">mene</hi>, present imperative of <hi type="italic">menein</hi>, "to remain, to abide, to stay, to continue, to stand firm"). The verb <hi type="italic">menein</hi> is one of the most important words in the New Testament for the concept of perseverance. In John 15:4-7, Jesus commands: "Abide (<hi type="italic">meinate</hi>) in me, and I in you... If you abide in me, and my words abide in you..." In 1 John 2:24, "Let what you heard from the beginning abide in you." In Hebrews 13:1, "Let brotherly love continue (<hi type="italic">menetō</hi>)." The verb describes remaining in a position already occupied, not arriving at a new position. Timothy is not told to discover something new but to stay where he is.</p>
          <p>What Timothy is to continue in is specified by two verbs: <hi type="italic">emathes</hi> ("you learned," from <hi type="italic">manthanein</hi>, "to learn") and <hi type="italic">epistōthēs</hi> ("you were firmly convinced, you were assured," from <hi type="italic">pistousthai</hi>, "to be made faithful, to be confirmed, to be made certain"). The first verb describes the reception of information; the second describes the inner conviction that the information is true. Timothy both learned and was persuaded. The two verbs together describe a faith that is both informed and convicted — not ignorant credulity but knowledge accompanied by settled confidence.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Knowing from whom you learned it"</hi> (<hi type="italic">eidōs para tinōn emathes</hi>). The participle <hi type="italic">eidōs</hi> ("knowing," from <hi type="italic">oida</hi>, "to know with certainty") modifies the imperative <hi type="italic">mene</hi>: Timothy is to continue in what he learned, knowing from whom he learned it. The preposition <hi type="italic">para</hi> ("from, from beside") with the genitive <hi type="italic">tinōn</hi> ("whom") identifies the source of Timothy's learning. The plural <hi type="italic">tinōn</hi> ("from whom," plural) indicates multiple sources. Who are these sources?</p>
          <p>In the immediate context, two groups have been identified: (1) Paul himself, from whom Timothy has "followed my teaching" (3:10) and who transmitted the deposit (1:13-14; 2:2); (2) Timothy's grandmother Lois and mother Eunice, in whom sincere faith first dwelt (1:5). The next verse will identify the childhood instruction Timothy received, pointing to the maternal and grandmaternal influence. The plural "from whom" thus encompasses both family and apostolic instruction — the two channels through which Timothy received his formation.</p>
          <p>The emphasis on source is deliberate and strategic. In a community where false teachers are active, the question "from whom did you learn?" becomes a test of authenticity. The false teachers' doctrine comes from corrupted minds (3:8); Timothy's learning comes from Paul, from Lois, from Eunice — from sources whose faithfulness has been demonstrated. The chain of transmission that 2:2 described (Paul to Timothy to faithful people to others) has its roots here: the reliability of the source determines the reliability of the teaching.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And how from childhood you have been acquainted with the sacred writings"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai hoti apo brephous ta hiera grammata oidas</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">kai hoti</hi> ("and that") introduces a further specification of what Timothy knows. The noun <hi type="italic">brephos</hi> ("infant, baby, small child") with the preposition <hi type="italic">apo</hi> ("from") identifies the starting point: from infancy, from the earliest possible age, Timothy has known the sacred writings. The phrase <hi type="italic">ta hiera grammata</hi> ("the sacred writings, the holy letters") is a standard designation for the Jewish scriptures — what we would call the Hebrew Bible or the Old Testament, in their Greek translation (the LXX). The adjective <hi type="italic">hieros</hi> ("sacred, holy, devoted to the gods") marks these writings as belonging to the divine sphere. The noun <hi type="italic">gramma</hi> ("letter, writing, document") in the plural refers to the written texts themselves.</p>
          <p>Timothy's acquaintance with the scriptures began in infancy. This confirms the picture from 1:5: his grandmother Lois and mother Eunice, Jewish women of faith, raised him in the knowledge of Israel's scriptures. Acts 16:1 confirms that Timothy's mother was a Jewish believer; his father was Greek. The household was mixed, but the maternal instruction was Jewish, and it included thorough grounding in the sacred writings. This childhood formation is now invoked as the foundation that will sustain Timothy against the false teachers. They are "always learning and never able to arrive at a knowledge of the truth" (3:7); Timothy has known the truth-bearing writings since he was an infant.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ta dynamena se sophisai eis sōtērian dia pisteōs tēs en Christō Iēsou</hi>). The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">ta</hi> ("which") refers to the sacred writings. Their capacity is stated: <hi type="italic">dynamena</hi> ("being able, having the power to") — the scriptures possess inherent capacity. The verb <hi type="italic">sophizein</hi> ("to make wise, to instruct, to give wisdom") appears elsewhere in the New Testament only in 2 Peter 1:16, where Peter distinguishes true testimony from "cleverly devised myths" (<hi type="italic">sesophismenois mythois</hi>). In the LXX, the word family is prominent: Psalm 19:7 (LXX 18:8), "the testimony of the Lord is sure, making wise (<hi type="italic">sophizousa</hi>) the simple." Psalm 119:98 (LXX 118:98), "Your commandment makes me wiser (<hi type="italic">esophisas me</hi>) than my enemies." The sacred writings make Timothy wise — not merely informed but wise, possessing the practical discernment that comes from understanding God's ways.</p>
          <p>The wisdom the scriptures produce has a specific direction: <hi type="italic">eis sōtērian</hi> ("for salvation, toward salvation, unto salvation"). The preposition <hi type="italic">eis</hi> ("into, toward, for the purpose of") indicates the goal toward which the wisdom moves. The scriptures make Timothy wise in a way that leads to salvation. The noun <hi type="italic">sōtēria</hi> ("salvation, deliverance, rescue") in the LXX consistently refers to God's acts of rescue — from enemies, from exile, from death (cf. the discussion at 2:10). Here the salvation is not limited to a single moment but describes the comprehensive deliverance that God accomplishes — the "salvation with eternal glory" of 2:10 toward which the elect are moving.</p>
          <p>The means by which the scriptures' wisdom leads to salvation is specified: <hi type="italic">dia pisteōs tēs en Christō Iēsou</hi> ("through faith that is in Christ Jesus"). The preposition <hi type="italic">dia</hi> ("through, by means of") identifies faith as the channel. The faith is located <hi type="italic">en Christō Iēsou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus") — it is not generic religious faith but specific trust in and fidelity to Christ Jesus. The structure of the verse is layered: the sacred writings produce wisdom, the wisdom is directed toward salvation, and the salvation is accessed through faith in Christ Jesus. The scriptures do not save automatically; they save by producing wisdom that leads to a faith that is in Christ.</p>
          <p>This verse makes an extraordinary claim about the Jewish scriptures — written centuries before Christ's incarnation — and their relationship to Christ. The sacred writings that Timothy's grandmother taught him from infancy are "able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus." The writings of Moses, the Psalms, the Prophets point toward and find their fulfillment in Christ. This is the same claim Paul made in Romans 1:2 (the gospel was "promised beforehand through his prophets in the holy scriptures") and in 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 ("Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures... he was raised on the third day in accordance with the Scriptures"). The sacred writings are not superseded by the gospel but are the foundational wisdom that leads to the gospel's reception.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3.16-2Tim.3.17">
          <title>Verses 16-17: The Nature and Purpose of Scripture</title>
          <p>"All Scripture is breathed out by God and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness, that the man of God may be complete, equipped for every good work."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"All Scripture is breathed out by God"</hi> (<hi type="italic">pasa graphē theopneustos</hi>). The adjective <hi type="italic">pasa</hi> ("all, every") with the noun <hi type="italic">graphē</hi> ("writing, scripture") covers the totality of the sacred writings just mentioned in verse 15. The noun <hi type="italic">graphē</hi> in the singular often refers to a specific passage of scripture ("the scripture says," as in Romans 4:3; 9:17; Galatians 4:30), but <hi type="italic">pasa graphē</hi> — "every scripture" or "all scripture" — extends the claim to the whole collection. The immediate referent, given the context of verse 15 ("the sacred writings which you have known from childhood"), is the Jewish scriptures — the Torah, the Prophets, and the Writings that constituted the scriptures available to Timothy from infancy. The claim covers whatever falls within the category of "sacred writings" (<hi type="italic">ta hiera grammata</hi>).</p>
          <p>The adjective <hi type="italic">theopneustos</hi> ("God-breathed, breathed out by God, inspired by God") appears only here in the New Testament and is extremely rare in Greek literature before this text. It is a compound of <hi type="italic">theos</hi> ("God") + <hi type="italic">pneustos</hi> (from <hi type="italic">pnein</hi>, "to breathe, to blow"). The word could theoretically be read as either active ("breathing God, filled with God's breath") or passive ("breathed by God, produced by God's breathing"). The passive reading is overwhelmingly more natural in context: the scriptures were produced by God's breath, they originate from God's own exhalation.</p>
          <p>The concept of God's breath as the agent of creation and communication has deep roots in Israel's scriptures. In Genesis 2:7 (LXX), God "breathed" (<hi type="italic">enephysēsen</hi>) into Adam's nostrils the breath of life (<hi type="italic">pnoēn zōēs</hi>). In Psalm 33:6 (LXX 32:6), "By the word of the Lord the heavens were made, and by the breath (<hi type="italic">pneumati</hi>) of his mouth all their host." In Job 32:8 (LXX), "But it is the spirit (<hi type="italic">pneuma</hi>) in man, the breath (<hi type="italic">pnoē</hi>) of the Almighty, that makes him understand." In 2 Samuel 23:2, David says: "The Spirit of the Lord speaks through me; his word is on my tongue." In Isaiah 59:21: "My Spirit that is upon you, and my words that I have put in your mouth." The breath/spirit of God is the agent through which God communicates his word to and through human authors.</p>
          <p>To say that scripture is <hi type="italic">theopneustos</hi> is to say that its origin lies in God's own breath — God breathed it out, God is its ultimate source. The claim does not specify the mechanism (dictation, inspiration, illumination, or some other mode) but identifies the source: the scriptures came from God. This is not a new claim in Jewish thought; it is the standard understanding of the prophetic and scriptural writings. The novelty, if any, is the use of a single adjective to compress this understanding into a word.</p>
          <p>The grammatical question of whether <hi type="italic">theopneustos</hi> is predicative ("all scripture is God-breathed and [is] profitable") or attributive ("all God-breathed scripture is also profitable") has been debated, but both readings yield essentially the same meaning in context. If predicative, Paul asserts two things about all scripture: it is God-breathed and it is profitable. If attributive, Paul says that all God-breathed scripture — which is all scripture, since the identification of the sacred writings as God-breathed is assumed rather than argued — is also profitable. In either reading, the practical conclusion is the same: the scriptures that Timothy has known since childhood are both God-breathed and useful.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ōphelimos pros didaskalian, pros elegmon, pros epanorthōsin, pros paideian tēn en dikaiosynē</hi>). The adjective <hi type="italic">ōphelimos</hi> ("profitable, useful, beneficial, advantageous") appeared in 1 Timothy 4:8, where Paul said that "bodily training is of some value (<hi type="italic">ōphelimon</hi>), but godliness is profitable (<hi type="italic">ōphelimos</hi>) in every way." The word describes practical usefulness — the scriptures are not merely venerable or interesting but useful, functional, productive of results.</p>
          <p>The usefulness is specified in four directions, each introduced by the preposition <hi type="italic">pros</hi> ("for, toward, for the purpose of"):</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Didaskalian</hi></hi> ("teaching, instruction, doctrine") — the communication of truth, the establishment of right understanding. The noun <hi type="italic">didaskalia</hi> has appeared throughout both letters as a central concern: "sound teaching" (<hi type="italic">hygiainousa didaskalia</hi>, 1 Tim 1:10; 4:6; 2 Tim 4:3; Titus 1:9; 2:1), "teaching that accords with godliness" (1 Tim 6:3), the teaching from which some will depart (1 Tim 4:1). Scripture is the source and standard for this teaching.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Elegmon</hi></hi> ("reproof, refutation, conviction, rebuke") — the exposure and correction of error. The noun <hi type="italic">elegmos</hi> appears only here in the New Testament. The related verb <hi type="italic">elegchein</hi> ("to convict, to reprove, to expose") appeared in 1 Timothy 5:20 ("rebuke [those who persist in sin] in the presence of all") and in Titus 1:9 (the overseer must be "able to rebuke those who contradict"). Scripture provides the basis for refuting falsehood — it is the standard against which error is measured and exposed.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Epanorthōsin</hi></hi> ("correction, restoration, setting right, amendment") — the positive counterpart to reproof. Where reproof identifies what is wrong, correction establishes what is right. The noun <hi type="italic">epanorthōsis</hi> appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of <hi type="italic">epi</hi> ("upon") + <hi type="italic">ana</hi> ("up, again") + <hi type="italic">orthoun</hi> ("to set upright, to make straight"). The word describes the act of setting something upright again that has fallen over, restoring something to its proper position. Scripture not only identifies error but provides the path back to truth.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold"><hi type="italic">Paideian tēn en dikaiosynē</hi></hi> ("training in righteousness, discipline in right conduct") — the ongoing formation of character. The noun <hi type="italic">paideia</hi> ("training, education, discipline, instruction") is a comprehensive word in Greek culture, covering the entire process by which a person is formed from childhood into a mature, competent adult. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">paideia</hi> is consistently associated with God's disciplining activity. Proverbs 3:11 (LXX): "My son, do not despise the Lord's discipline (<hi type="italic">paideias kyriou</hi>) or be weary of his reproof." Hebrews 12:5-11 develops the concept of divine <hi type="italic">paideia</hi> at length: "The Lord disciplines (<hi type="italic">paideuei</hi>) the one he loves." The phrase <hi type="italic">en dikaiosynē</hi> ("in righteousness") specifies the domain of the training: the scripture trains people in <hi type="italic">dikaiosynē</hi> — right conduct, right relationship with God, conformity to God's character and standards.</p>
          <p>The four purposes together describe a comprehensive pedagogical function. Teaching establishes truth. Reproof exposes error. Correction restores the erring to the right path. Training in righteousness forms the character that sustains faithfulness over time. The sequence moves from the cognitive (teaching and reproof) to the practical (correction and training). Scripture addresses both what is believed and how life is lived.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"That the man of God may be complete, equipped for every good work"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hina artios ē ho tou theou anthrōpos, pros pan ergon agathon exērtismenos</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">hina</hi> ("in order that, so that") introduces the purpose for which scripture's fourfold function exists. The phrase <hi type="italic">ho tou theou anthrōpos</hi> ("the man of God, the person belonging to God") is the title used for Timothy. In 1 Timothy 6:11, Paul addressed Timothy directly: "But as for you, O man of God, flee these things." The phrase has deep roots in the LXX, where it designates individuals who serve as God's authorized representatives. Moses is "the man of God" (<hi type="italic">anthrōpos tou theou</hi>) in Deuteronomy 33:1; Joshua 14:6; 1 Chronicles 23:14; Ezra 3:2. Elijah is "a man of God" in 1 Kings 17:18, 24. Elisha is "the man of God" in 2 Kings 4:7, 9, 16, 21, 22, 25, 27, and many other verses. David is "the man of God" in 2 Chronicles 8:14; Nehemiah 12:24, 36. The title identifies a person who belongs to God, who serves God, who speaks and acts on God's behalf. Timothy stands in this lineage.</p>
          <p>The adjective <hi type="italic">artios</hi> ("complete, capable, proficient, fit for a task") appears only here in the New Testament. It describes someone who is fully formed for their function — not lacking anything essential, not deficient in any necessary quality. A tool that is <hi type="italic">artios</hi> is one that is fit for its purpose; a person who is <hi type="italic">artios</hi> is one who can fulfill their role completely.</p>
          <p>The participle <hi type="italic">exērtismenos</hi> (from <hi type="italic">exartizein</hi>, "to complete, to finish, to equip thoroughly, to furnish completely") reinforces the concept with an intensified form. The prefix <hi type="italic">ex-</hi> ("out, thoroughly, completely") intensifies the base <hi type="italic">artizein</hi> ("to prepare, to equip"). The word describes thorough, comprehensive equipping — not partial preparation but complete readiness. The phrase <hi type="italic">pros pan ergon agathon</hi> ("for every good work") specifies what the equipping is for: every good work, without exception or limitation. The expression <hi type="italic">pan ergon agathon</hi> ("every good work") appeared in 2:21, where the cleansed vessel was described as "prepared for every good work" (<hi type="italic">eis pan ergon agathon hetoimasmenon</hi>). The verbal echo binds the two passages: just as the cleansed vessel is prepared for every good work, so the man of God who is formed by scripture is equipped for every good work. The difference is that 2:21 described the preparation through cleansing from dishonor, while 3:17 describes the preparation through the formative work of God-breathed scripture. Both processes contribute to the same result: a person ready for comprehensive service.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.3">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>2 Timothy 3 is structured around two sharp contrasts — between the false teachers and Timothy, between the corrupting influence of deception and the formative power of scripture — and moves the letter from the specific warnings of chapter 2 to the solemn charge of chapter 4.</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Verses 1-5</hi> describe the character of people who will mark the "last days" — an era that the early communities understood as already present. The vice list of nineteen descriptors catalogs a comprehensive corruption that begins with disordered love (self, money, pleasure rather than God) and culminates in the defining trait: having the outward form of godliness while denying its power. These are not outsiders but people within or adjacent to the community who maintain religious appearances while rejecting the transformative reality. Timothy is commanded to avoid them.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Verses 6-9</hi> describe the method and precedent of the false teachers. They infiltrate households, take captive vulnerable people who are burdened with guilt and driven by shifting desires, and produce students who are always learning but never arriving at truth. The comparison to Jannes and Jambres — the Egyptian magicians who opposed Moses — locates the current opponents within a scriptural pattern: they imitate the genuine article, they briefly succeed, but their folly eventually becomes evident to everyone. Their opposition to the truth identifies them as corrupted in mind and disqualified regarding the faith — the exact opposite of Timothy, who is to present himself as approved (2:15).</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Verses 10-12</hi> pivot to Timothy with the emphatic contrast "You, however." Timothy has followed Paul's teaching, conduct, purpose, faith, patience, love, and steadfastness through persecutions and sufferings — specifically the persecutions at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra, in Timothy's own home region. The Lord rescued Paul from all of them. The section concludes with a universal principle: all who desire to live a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted. The false teachers' avoidance of persecution is itself evidence that their godliness lacks power.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold">Verse 13</hi> states the trajectory of the wicked: evil people and impostors will advance from bad to worse, deceiving others and being deceived themselves. The mutual, self-reinforcing nature of the deception explains why their students can never arrive at truth: the teachers themselves do not possess it.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold">Verses 14-15</hi> ground Timothy's resistance in two foundations: the sources from whom he learned (Paul, Lois, Eunice — people whose faithfulness has been demonstrated) and the sacred writings he has known since infancy. These writings are "able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus" — the Jewish scriptures, read in light of Christ, provide the wisdom that leads to salvation. The faith that mediates this salvation is specifically faith in Christ Jesus.</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold">Verses 16-17</hi> make the chapter's climactic claim about the nature and function of scripture. All scripture is God-breathed — its origin lies in God's own breath — and it is useful for four purposes: teaching (establishing truth), reproof (exposing error), correction (restoring the erring), and training in righteousness (forming character). The goal of this fourfold function is that the man of God may be complete and equipped for every good work. Scripture is not merely an archive of ancient texts but a living, functional instrument by which God forms his servants for comprehensive service.</p>
          <p>The chapter's overall architecture serves the letter's central concern: the faithful transmission and defense of the gospel across generations. The false teachers represent the threat — they are corrupted, corrupting, and escalating in their wickedness. Timothy represents the alternative — grounded in Paul's example, rooted in the sacred writings, formed by God-breathed scripture for every good work. The chapter does not promise that the false teachers will disappear or that the difficulty will ease. It promises that their folly will eventually become evident (3:9) and that the man of God who is formed by scripture will be complete and equipped (3:17). The resource for standing against the corruption of the last days is not superior intellect, institutional power, or strategic brilliance but the ancient scriptures, known since childhood, breathed out by God, and made wise for salvation through faith in the risen Davidic Messiah. This is the foundation from which the solemn charge of 4:1-2 — "preach the word" — will issue. Timothy is to preach because he has been equipped by scripture; he has been equipped by scripture because God breathed it; and the word he preaches is the word of the truth from which the false teachers have swerved but which remains, like God's firm foundation (2:19), unshaken.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="2Tim.4">
        <title>2 Timothy 4 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre and occasion:</hi> This is the final chapter of Paul's last letter to Timothy, written from prison in Rome under the expectation of approaching death. The letter's genre — testamentary exhortation, the farewell words of a dying figure to his successor — reaches its climax here. Everything the letter has built toward converges in this chapter: the solemn charge to preach the word, the warning that people will abandon sound teaching, Paul's own final testimony, and the personal details of a man preparing for the end. The testamentary genre requires a final charge (vv. 1-5), a review of one's life and legacy (vv. 6-8), last instructions and personal requests (vv. 9-18), and a closing (vv. 19-22). Chapter 4 provides all of these in sequence.</p>
          <p>The letter's setting, recoverable from its own details, has been established across the preceding chapters. Paul is imprisoned in Rome (1:17), likely in severe confinement that required Onesiphorus to search earnestly to find him (1:17). He is cold and wants his cloak (4:13). His associates have largely deserted him (1:15; 4:10, 16). Only Luke remains with him (4:11). He has already appeared in court once ("my first defense," 4:16) and anticipates that his end is near (4:6). The false teaching crisis in Ephesus that prompted 1 Timothy continues: Hymenaeus has resurfaced alongside Philetus (2:17), their teaching spreads like gangrene (2:17), and the faith of some has been overturned (2:18). Against this backdrop of desertion, danger, and doctrinal corruption, Paul writes his final words to the one person he trusts to carry the work forward.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (2 Timothy 3):</hi> Chapter 3 opened with a warning cast in eschatological terms: "Understand this, that in the last days there will come times of difficulty" (3:1). A long vice list followed (3:2-5), describing people who possess "the appearance of godliness but deny its power" (3:5). The command was stark: "Avoid such people" (3:5). Some of these people infiltrate households, capturing "weak women, burdened with sins and led astray by various passions, always learning and never able to arrive at a knowledge of the truth" (3:6-7). They were compared to Jannes and Jambres, the Egyptian magicians who "opposed Moses" (3:8) — figures drawn from the Exodus narrative via Jewish tradition. Like those magicians, "their folly will be plain to all" (3:9).</p>
          <p>Paul then turned to Timothy with a sharp contrast: "You, however, have followed my teaching, my conduct, my aim in life, my faith, my patience, my love, my steadfastness, my persecutions and sufferings that happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra" (3:10-11). The locations are specific: these are cities in Timothy's own region, places where Timothy witnessed Paul's sufferings firsthand (Acts 13:50-14:20). Paul stated: "the Lord rescued me from them all" (3:11). The rescue is past and attested; it grounds the expectation that suffering is survivable through God's faithfulness. Paul then declared: "Indeed, all who desire to live a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted" (3:12) — a universal principle, not a contingent warning. Persecution is not a sign that something has gone wrong but the expected condition of faithful living.</p>
          <p>The chapter's contrast sharpened: "evil people and impostors will go on from bad to worse, deceiving and being deceived. But as for you, continue in what you have learned and have firmly believed, knowing from whom you learned it" (3:13-14). The verb <hi type="italic">prokoptein</hi> ("to advance, to progress") appeared here as in 2:16, describing advancement in the wrong direction — the impostors make progress into deeper deception. Against this downward trajectory, Timothy is anchored by two things: what he has learned (the content) and from whom he learned it (the source). The "whom" is ambiguous — it could refer to Paul, to Lois and Eunice (1:5), or to the community of witnesses (2:2). All are relevant. Timothy's education was not from anonymous sources but from known, attested, trustworthy people.</p>
          <p>The chapter concluded with a statement about Scripture: "from childhood you have been acquainted with the sacred writings, which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus" (3:15). The "sacred writings" (<hi type="italic">hiera grammata</hi>) are the Scriptures of Israel — the Torah, the Prophets, and the Writings as available in the first century. Timothy knew them from childhood, through his Jewish mother Eunice and grandmother Lois (1:5; Acts 16:1). These writings are "able to make you wise for salvation" — but the wisdom they produce is accessed "through faith in Christ Jesus." The Scriptures point toward Christ; their salvific potential is realized through faith in him.</p>
          <p>Then came the summary statement: "All Scripture is breathed out by God and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness, that the man of God may be complete, equipped for every good work" (3:16-17). The adjective <hi type="italic">theopneustos</hi> ("God-breathed, breathed out by God") describes Scripture as carrying God's breath — it comes from God in the way that breath comes from the one who breathes. Four functions are listed: teaching (<hi type="italic">didaskalia</hi>), reproof (<hi type="italic">elegmos</hi>), correction (<hi type="italic">epanorthosis</hi>), and training in righteousness (<hi type="italic">paideia en dikaiosyne</hi>). The purpose is stated: "that the man of God may be complete (<hi type="italic">artios</hi>), equipped (<hi type="italic">exertismenos</hi>) for every good work (<hi type="italic">pan ergon agathon</hi>)." The phrase "every good work" echoes 2:21, where the cleansed vessel is "prepared for every good work." The person who is shaped by Scripture and the person who cleanses himself from what is dishonorable arrive at the same destination: readiness for comprehensive service.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 4 connects:</hi> Chapter 3 ended with Scripture's purpose: equipping the man of God for every good work. Chapter 4 opens with a solemn charge that specifies what those good works are: "Preach the word; be ready in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with complete patience and teaching" (4:2). The four functions of Scripture listed in 3:16 (teaching, reproof, correction, training) correspond directly to the activities commanded in 4:2 (teaching, reproving, rebuking, exhorting). Scripture equips; the charge deploys. Chapter 3 supplied the weapon; chapter 4 orders the soldier into battle.</p>
          <p>The connection is also marked by the shift from description to command. Chapter 3 described the landscape — the false teachers, their methods, their trajectory, and the Scriptural resource available to Timothy. Chapter 4 issues the marching orders — what Timothy must do in this landscape, and why the urgency is absolute. The conjunction <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("therefore") that some manuscripts include at 4:1 (whether original or not, the logical connection is unmistakable) draws the consequence: given the resources described in chapter 3, Timothy must act as chapter 4 commands.</p>
          <p>The chapter also completes the letter's testamentary structure. The farewell speech of a dying leader characteristically ends with a personal review of the speaker's life, a final expression of confidence, and last practical instructions. Paul's "I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith" (4:7) is the letter's climactic moment — the dying father's summary of his life, offered as a model for the son who must now carry on alone.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.1-2Tim.4.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: The Solemn Charge — Preach the Word</title>
          <p>"I charge you in the presence of God and of Christ Jesus, who is to judge the living and the dead, and by his appearing and his kingdom: preach the word; be ready in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with complete patience and teaching."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I charge you in the presence of God and of Christ Jesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">diamartyromai enopion tou theou kai Christou Iesou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">diamartyresthai</hi> ("to solemnly charge, to testify earnestly, to adjure") is the most serious form of commissioning language available to Paul. The same verb appeared in 1 Timothy 5:21, where Paul charged Timothy "in the presence of God and of Christ Jesus and of the elect angels" — the same cosmic audience, the same weight of solemnity. It appeared again in 2 Timothy 2:14, where Timothy was to charge the community "before God" not to quarrel about words. The verb's prefix <hi type="italic">dia-</hi> ("through, thoroughly") intensifies the testimony: this is not a casual request but a thorough, penetrating, binding charge.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">enopion tou theou kai Christou Iesou</hi> ("in the presence of God and of Christ Jesus") establishes the witnesses to the charge. The preposition <hi type="italic">enopion</hi> ("before, in the sight of, in the presence of") places the charge under divine observation. What Paul commands, God and Christ Jesus observe. Timothy receives this instruction not in a private conversation but in a courtroom, with God and Christ as witnesses. The weight is unmistakable: to neglect this charge is to fail before God himself.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Who is to judge the living and the dead"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tou mellontos krinein zontas kai nekrous</hi>). The participle <hi type="italic">mellontos</hi> (from <hi type="italic">mellein</hi>, "to be about to, to be going to, to intend") with the infinitive <hi type="italic">krinein</hi> ("to judge") describes Christ Jesus as the one who is about to judge or who is destined to judge. The object of the judgment is comprehensive: <hi type="italic">zontas kai nekrous</hi> — "living and dead." No one escapes the scope of this judgment. Those alive at the time of judgment and those who have already died are equally subject to it. The phrase echoes the early community's confession: in Acts 10:42, Peter preaches that God appointed Jesus "as judge of the living and the dead" (<hi type="italic">krites zonton kai nekron</hi>). The formula appears to be an established confessional statement that Paul invokes here to heighten the charge's gravity.</p>
          <p>The relevance to the letter's argument is direct. Paul is about to die (4:6). Timothy will continue living and working. Both the dying apostle and the living delegate stand before the same judge. The charge Paul gives is issued under the authority of the one who will evaluate how both of them discharged their responsibilities. Paul's confidence about "that day" (1:12, 18; 4:8) and the faithful saying's warning about denial and its consequences (2:12-13) are grounded in this reality: Christ Jesus judges.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And by his appearing and his kingdom"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ten epiphaneian autou kai ten basileian autou</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">kai</hi> with the accusative creates an additional ground for the charge. Paul charges Timothy not only "in the presence of" God and Christ but also "by" or "in view of" Christ's appearing and kingdom. The nouns are governed by the verb <hi type="italic">diamartyromai</hi>, which here takes a double construction: the charge is made before divine witnesses and in view of divine events.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">epiphaneia</hi> ("appearing, manifestation, visible arrival") has been used in this letter for Christ's first appearing (1:10, where Christ "abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel") and will be used later in this chapter for his future appearing (4:8, "all who have loved his appearing"). Here the reference is to the future appearing — the return of Christ in visible manifestation, the event that will bring the judgment just described. The word carried connotations in the Hellenistic world of a god or king becoming visible, arriving in power. The emperor's visit to a city was an <hi type="italic">epiphaneia</hi>. Christ's future appearing is the ultimate such event.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">basileia</hi> ("kingdom, royal rule, reign, sovereignty") describes the realm or rule that Christ's appearing will establish in its fullness. The kingdom of God or of Christ is the sphere of God's sovereign authority. In the LXX, the kingdom belongs to God: Psalm 103:19 (LXX 102:19), "The LORD has established his throne in the heavens, and his kingdom rules over all." Daniel 2:44, "the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that shall never be destroyed." Daniel 7:14, "to him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve him; his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away, and his kingdom one that shall not be destroyed." The kingdom is what the faithful saying promised to those who endure: "if we endure, we will also reign with him" (2:12). Timothy's charge is issued in the shadow of that coming kingdom. What he does now will be evaluated by the king when the kingdom arrives in its fullness.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Preach the word"</hi> (<hi type="italic">keryxon ton logon</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">keryssein</hi> ("to herald, to proclaim, to announce publicly, to preach") is the activity of the <hi type="italic">keryx</hi> ("herald") described in 1:11, where Paul was appointed "a preacher (<hi type="italic">keryx</hi>) and apostle and teacher." Timothy is now commanded to do what Paul was appointed to do. The imperative <hi type="italic">keryxon</hi> is aorist, conveying urgency: do it, do it now, get on with it.</p>
          <p>The object is <hi type="italic">ton logon</hi> — "the word." Not "a word" or "words" but the word — the definite article marks a specific, known body of content. In the context of this letter, "the word" is the gospel Paul has been describing: the message about the risen Davidic Messiah (2:8), the deposit entrusted to Timothy (1:14), the word of truth that must be rightly handled (2:15), the message for which Paul suffers in chains but which itself "is not bound" (2:9). "The word" is also the content of the "sacred writings" just described in 3:15-16 — Scripture itself, which equips the man of God. The command is comprehensive: proclaim the apostolic gospel as the herald of the king, in continuity with the Scriptures of Israel.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Be ready in season and out of season"</hi> (<hi type="italic">epistēthi eukairōs akairōs</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">ephistanai</hi> ("to stand by, to be at hand, to be present, to be ready, to attend to") in the aorist imperative (<hi type="italic">epistēthi</hi>) commands readiness — Timothy is to stand at his post, present and available. The adverbs <hi type="italic">eukairōs</hi> ("in season, at a convenient time, when the opportunity is favorable") and <hi type="italic">akairōs</hi> ("out of season, at an inconvenient time, when circumstances are unfavorable") form a pair that covers every possible moment. The words share a root: <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> ("time, season, opportune moment"). The prefix <hi type="italic">eu-</hi> ("well, good") marks favorable times; the prefix <hi type="italic">a-</hi> (alpha-privative) marks unfavorable times. Timothy is to preach in good times and bad, when it is convenient and when it is not, when the audience is receptive and when they resist, when the political climate permits and when it threatens.</p>
          <p>The instruction is particularly pointed given what verses 3-4 will say: a time is coming when people will not endure sound teaching. That time of rejection is an <hi type="italic">akairos</hi> — an unfavorable season. Timothy must preach even then. The readiness demanded is not dependent on the audience's response but on the herald's fidelity to his commission.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Reprove, rebuke, and exhort"</hi> (<hi type="italic">elegxon, epitimēson, parakaleson</hi>). Three imperatives specify the forms the preaching takes:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Reprove</hi> (<hi type="italic">elegxon</hi>, from <hi type="italic">elegchein</hi>, "to convict, to expose, to reprove, to bring to light"). The verb describes the act of demonstrating that someone is in error — exposing the falsehood so that the person confronted can see it. In Titus 1:9, the overseer must "be able to give instruction in sound doctrine and also to rebuke (<hi type="italic">elegchein</hi>) those who contradict it." In Titus 1:13, "rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith." The reproof is an act of revelation: it brings what is hidden into the open.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Rebuke</hi> (<hi type="italic">epitimēson</hi>, from <hi type="italic">epitiman</hi>, "to rebuke, to censure, to warn, to charge sternly"). The verb carries a stronger note of authority than <hi type="italic">elegchein</hi>. In the Synoptic Gospels, Jesus "rebukes" demons (Mark 1:25; 9:25), the wind and sea (Mark 4:39), and Peter (Mark 8:33). The word implies the exercise of authority over what is wrong — not merely exposing the error but commanding it to stop. Where reproof reveals, rebuke restrains.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Exhort</hi> (<hi type="italic">parakaleson</hi>, from <hi type="italic">parakalein</hi>, "to call alongside, to encourage, to urge, to comfort, to exhort"). The verb encompasses both encouragement and appeal. It is the verb from which the noun <hi type="italic">paraklēsis</hi> ("encouragement, exhortation, comfort") derives — the same noun used in Romans 12:8 for the gift of exhortation, and in Acts 4:36 as the basis for Barnabas's name ("son of encouragement"). The exhortation is the positive counterpart to reproof and rebuke: where reproof corrects error and rebuke restrains wrong, exhortation urges the right course and strengthens the one who follows it.</p>
          <p>The three verbs together describe the full range of pastoral speech: correcting what is wrong (reproof), commanding what is wrong to stop (rebuke), and encouraging what is right (exhortation). The order is not accidental: first the error is exposed, then it is confronted, then the hearer is urged toward the truth.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"With complete patience and teaching"</hi> (<hi type="italic">en pasē makrothymia kai didachē</hi>). The prepositional phrase <hi type="italic">en</hi> ("in, with") describes the manner in which the reproving, rebuking, and exhorting are to be carried out. Two qualities govern the manner:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Patience</hi> (<hi type="italic">makrothymia</hi>, "patience, long-suffering, forbearance, steadfastness"). The noun is composed of <hi type="italic">makros</hi> ("long") + <hi type="italic">thymos</hi> ("passion, anger, wrath"). Literally, it describes one who is "long before anger" — one who endures provocation without retaliating. The adjective <hi type="italic">pasē</hi> ("all, every, complete") intensifies: not partial patience but complete patience, patience that does not run out. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">makrothymia</hi> is characteristic of God himself: Exodus 34:6 (LXX), "The LORD, the LORD, a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger (<hi type="italic">makrothymos</hi>)." God's patience with Israel's repeated failures is the foundational instance of <hi type="italic">makrothymia</hi>. Timothy is to embody this divine quality in his pastoral work.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Teaching</hi> (<hi type="italic">didachē</hi>, "teaching, instruction, doctrine"). The noun describes the content-rich activity of systematic instruction. The reproof, rebuke, and exhortation are not to be exercised as raw authority but as acts of teaching — grounded in content, supported by argument, rooted in the word being preached. This connects directly to 2:24-25, where the Lord's servant must be "able to teach" (<hi type="italic">didaktikon</hi>) and must correct opponents with gentleness. The teaching is the vehicle through which the patience becomes effective: Timothy does not merely wait out his opponents but teaches them through the process of correction.</p>
          <p>The charge as a whole — "preach the word; be ready in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with complete patience and teaching" — is the most comprehensive statement of Timothy's commission in the entire letter. It gathers up everything Paul has been saying since the opening chapter: rekindle the gift (1:6), do not be ashamed (1:8), guard the deposit (1:14), entrust the teaching to faithful people (2:2), rightly handle the word of truth (2:15), correct opponents with gentleness (2:25), and be equipped by Scripture for every good work (3:17). The charge is the distillation of the entire letter into a single command: preach.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.3-2Tim.4.4">
          <title>Verses 3-4: The Coming Rejection of Sound Teaching</title>
          <p>"For the time is coming when people will not endure sound teaching, but having itching ears they will accumulate for themselves teachers to suit their own passions, and will turn away from listening to the truth and wander off into myths."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For the time is coming when people will not endure sound teaching"</hi> (<hi type="italic">estai gar kairos hote tēs hygiainousēs didaskalias ouk anexontai</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") explains why the charge of verses 1-2 is so urgent. The urgency is not merely that Timothy ought to preach but that a time is approaching — perhaps has already begun — when preaching will be resisted. The noun <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> ("time, season, appointed moment") describes not mere chronological time (<hi type="italic">chronos</hi>) but a qualitative season, a period defined by its character. The future verb <hi type="italic">estai</hi> ("there will be") marks this as something ahead, though the chapter 3 description of present false teachers suggests the process is already underway and will intensify.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">anechesthai</hi> ("to bear with, to endure, to tolerate, to put up with") in the middle voice with the negative <hi type="italic">ouk</hi> describes refusal to listen. The teaching itself is not the problem; the hearers are the problem. They will not endure — will not tolerate, will not bear with — the teaching. The noun <hi type="italic">didaskalia</hi> ("teaching, instruction, doctrine") is qualified by the participle <hi type="italic">hygiainousēs</hi> (from <hi type="italic">hygiainein</hi>, "to be healthy, to be sound, to be in good condition") — "sound teaching," literally "healthy teaching." The medical metaphor characteristic of the Pastoral Epistles recurs: sound teaching is healthy teaching, teaching that produces well-being in those who receive it. The people Paul describes reject what is healthy in favor of what is diseased. The phrase <hi type="italic">hygiainousa didaskalia</hi> has appeared throughout the letters: 1 Timothy 1:10 ("whatever else is contrary to sound teaching"), 1 Timothy 6:3 ("the sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ and the teaching that accords with godliness"), 2 Timothy 1:13 ("Follow the pattern of the sound words that you have heard from me"), Titus 1:9, 2:1. Its consistent use marks the apostolic gospel as the standard of health against which all other teaching is diagnosed as diseased.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But having itching ears they will accumulate for themselves teachers to suit their own passions"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla kata tas idias epithymias heautois episōreusousin didaskalous knēthomenoi tēn akoēn</hi>). The adversative <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but") introduces the alternative the people choose. The phrase <hi type="italic">kata tas idias epithymias</hi> ("according to their own desires, in keeping with their own passions") identifies the governing criterion: not truth but desire. The adjective <hi type="italic">idias</hi> ("their own, personal, private") emphasizes that the standard is internal and self-generated — they select teachers based on what they want to hear, not on what is true. The noun <hi type="italic">epithymia</hi> ("desire, craving, longing, passion") in this context describes the appetite for teaching that flatters, entertains, or confirms what they already want to believe. In 2:22, Paul told Timothy to "flee youthful passions" (<hi type="italic">neoterikas epithymias</hi>); here the passions drive the selection of teachers.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">episōreuein</hi> ("to heap up, to accumulate, to pile on") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of <hi type="italic">epi</hi> ("upon") + <hi type="italic">sōreuein</hi> ("to heap, to pile"). The image is of piling up teachers one on top of another — not seeking a single reliable instructor but collecting a mass of voices that collectively tell them what they want to hear. The reflexive <hi type="italic">heautois</hi> ("for themselves") underscores the self-serving nature of the accumulation: they are not seeking teachers for the community's benefit but for their own gratification.</p>
          <p>The participle <hi type="italic">knēthomenoi</hi> (from <hi type="italic">knēthein</hi>, "to scratch, to itch, to tickle") with <hi type="italic">tēn akoēn</hi> ("the hearing, the ear") creates a vivid physical metaphor: they have "itching ears," ears that need to be scratched. The itch is the craving for novelty, for flattery, for teaching that stimulates without demanding change. The teachers they accumulate are the ones who scratch the itch — who provide the sensation the ears demand. The metaphor is particularly apt in the context of the Pastoral Epistles' medical language: an itch is a symptom of a disorder, and scratching provides temporary relief while worsening the underlying condition. The hearers' desire for ear-scratching teachers is itself a symptom of the deeper disease that the sound teaching was meant to cure.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And will turn away from listening to the truth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai apo men tēs alētheias tēn akoēn apostrepsousin</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">apostrephein</hi> ("to turn away, to turn back, to divert") appeared in 1:15 for those who "turned away from" Paul (<hi type="italic">apestraphēsan me pantes hoi en tē Asia</hi>). The same verb now describes a turning away not from Paul personally but from the truth itself. The noun <hi type="italic">alētheia</hi> ("truth") has been the letter's benchmark: Hymenaeus and Philetus "swerved from the truth" (2:18); the Lord's servant corrects opponents in the hope that God will grant them "repentance leading to a knowledge of the truth" (2:25); Timothy was told to "rightly handle the word of truth" (2:15). The truth is the apostolic gospel, the content of sound teaching, the word Paul commands Timothy to preach. The people described here actively divert their hearing away from this truth. The <hi type="italic">men... de</hi> construction (<hi type="italic">apo men tēs alētheias... epi de tous mythous</hi>) creates a balanced contrast: away from the truth on one side, toward myths on the other.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And wander off into myths"</hi> (<hi type="italic">epi de tous mythous ektrapēsontai</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">ektrepesthai</hi> ("to turn aside, to wander off, to be diverted") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:6 for those who "swerved" from love, good conscience, and sincere faith, and in 1 Timothy 5:15 for widows who "turned aside after Satan." The consistent usage describes deviation from the right path — not a sudden catastrophic departure but a gradual turning, a drifting off course. The destination of the wandering is <hi type="italic">tous mythous</hi> — "the myths." The noun <hi type="italic">mythos</hi> ("myth, fable, invented story") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:4, where Paul warned against "myths and endless genealogies, which promote speculations rather than the stewardship from God that is by faith." In 1 Timothy 4:7, Timothy was told to "have nothing to do with irreverent, silly myths." In Titus 1:14, the command was to avoid "Jewish myths." The myths represent the alternative content that replaces the gospel when people reject sound teaching — speculative, genealogical, and legendary material that offers intellectual stimulation without moral transformation.</p>
          <p>The movement described in verses 3-4 is a progressive deterioration:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>Step 1: They will not endure sound teaching (rejection of the healthy).</item>
            <item>Step 2: They will accumulate teachers to suit their own passions (replacement with the pleasant).</item>
            <item>Step 3: They will turn away from the truth (active departure from what is real).</item>
            <item>Step 4: They will wander off into myths (arrival at what is false).</item>
          </list>
          <p>The sequence is not presented as surprising or unprecedented. It is the logical outcome of the process described throughout the letter: the false teaching that began as word-quarreling (2:14) and spread like gangrene (2:17) produces a community that has lost its appetite for truth and craves falsehood. The progression from 1 Timothy to 2 Timothy — from managing a crisis to watching it metastasize — finds its culmination in this prediction: the time is coming when the sound teaching will be rejected en masse.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.5">
          <title>Verse 5: The Counter-Command to Timothy</title>
          <p>"As for you, always be sober-minded, endure suffering, do the work of an evangelist, fulfill your ministry."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"As for you"</hi> (<hi type="italic">sy de</hi>). The pronoun <hi type="italic">sy</hi> ("you," emphatic, placed first) with the adversative particle <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("but, however") creates the same contrastive structure that has appeared throughout the letter: "they" will do one thing; "you, however," must do the opposite. The construction echoes 3:10 (<hi type="italic">sy de parēkolouthēsas mou tē didaskalia</hi> — "You, however, have followed my teaching") and 3:14 (<hi type="italic">sy de mene en hois emathes</hi> — "But as for you, continue in what you have learned"). Each time, the <hi type="italic">sy de</hi> marks the point where Paul turns from describing the behavior of the faithless to commanding the behavior of the faithful. The pattern is consistent: the world will deteriorate; Timothy must not.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Always be sober-minded"</hi> (<hi type="italic">nēphe en pasin</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">nēphein</hi> ("to be sober, to be clear-headed, to be self-controlled, to be watchful") is the same word family as the <hi type="italic">ananēphein</hi> ("to come to one's senses, to sober up") of 2:26, where Paul hoped the opponents might "come to their senses and escape from the snare of the devil." The opponents need to recover sobriety; Timothy must maintain it. The verb's primary sense is literal sobriety — the state of being free from the distorting effects of wine or intoxicants. Applied metaphorically, it describes mental clarity, freedom from the clouded judgment that false teaching and emotional pressure produce. The phrase <hi type="italic">en pasin</hi> ("in all things, in everything, always") makes the sobriety comprehensive: not sober in some matters and distracted in others, but clear-headed in everything.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Endure suffering"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kakopathēson</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">kakopathein</hi> ("to suffer evil, to endure hardship, to bear affliction") has been one of the letter's recurring terms. In 1:8, Timothy was commanded to <hi type="italic">synkakopathēson</hi> ("share in suffering") for the gospel. In 2:3, the same command: <hi type="italic">synkakopathēson hos kalos stratiōtēs</hi> ("share in suffering as a good soldier"). In 2:9, Paul described his own experience: <hi type="italic">en hō kakopathō</hi> ("for which I suffer"). The verb runs like a thread through the entire letter, binding Paul's experience to Timothy's commission. Paul suffers; Timothy is called to suffer with him and after him. The suffering is not accidental but structural: it is the expected condition of faithful gospel ministry in a hostile environment. The imperative is aorist (<hi type="italic">kakopathēson</hi>), conveying directness: suffer. Accept it. Do not try to avoid it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Do the work of an evangelist"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ergon poiēson euangelistou</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">euangelistēs</hi> ("evangelist, one who announces good news, a bearer of the gospel") appears elsewhere in the New Testament only in Acts 21:8 (Philip "the evangelist") and Ephesians 4:11 ("And he gave the apostles, the prophets, the evangelists, the shepherds and teachers"). The word is composed of <hi type="italic">eu</hi> ("good, well") + <hi type="italic">angellein</hi> ("to announce, to bring a message") — one who announces good news. Paul does not say Timothy is an evangelist as a fixed office; he says Timothy is to do the work (<hi type="italic">ergon</hi>) of an evangelist — to perform the function of gospel proclamation. The noun <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> ("work, task, deed") is the practical, concrete activity. The imperative <hi type="italic">poiēson</hi> ("do, make, perform") commands action: not merely hold the title but perform the labor.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Fulfill your ministry"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tēn diakonian sou plērophoresōn</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">plērophorein</hi> ("to fulfill completely, to carry out fully, to bring to full measure") is composed of <hi type="italic">plērēs</hi> ("full") + <hi type="italic">phorein</hi> ("to carry, to bear"). The word describes bringing something to its complete measure — filling it to the brim, carrying it through to completion. The noun <hi type="italic">diakonia</hi> ("ministry, service, commission") is modified by <hi type="italic">sou</hi> ("your") — this is Timothy's specific service, the particular commission entrusted to him. The command is not merely to begin or to continue but to complete: bring your ministry to its full measure, leave nothing undone, carry the work through to the end.</p>
          <p>The four commands of verse 5 — be sober, suffer, evangelize, fulfill — summarize Timothy's calling in four dimensions: mental clarity (sobriety), physical endurance (suffering), public proclamation (evangelism), and vocational completion (fulfilling ministry). They answer the four-step deterioration of verses 3-4 point by point: where others lose their sobriety and become intoxicated by itching-ear teachers, Timothy must remain sober. Where others avoid the cost of faithfulness, Timothy must endure suffering. Where others turn from the truth to myths, Timothy must do the work of proclaiming the good news. Where others abandon their calling, Timothy must fulfill his.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.6-2Tim.4.8">
          <title>Verses 6-8: Paul's Final Testimony — The Fight Finished, the Crown Awaiting</title>
          <p>"For I am already being poured out as a drink offering, and the time of my departure has come. I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith. Henceforth there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me on that day, and not only to me but also to all who have loved his appearing."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For I am already being poured out as a drink offering"</hi> (<hi type="italic">egō gar ēdē spendomai</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") connects Paul's testimony to the charge he has just given Timothy. The reason Timothy must fulfill his ministry is that Paul is finishing his. The pronoun <hi type="italic">egō</hi> ("I," emphatic) draws attention to Paul himself: I, for my part, am already being poured out. The shift from commanding Timothy to describing his own situation creates the letter's emotional climax.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">spendesthai</hi> ("to be poured out as a drink offering, to be offered as a libation") is the passive of <hi type="italic">spendein</hi>. In the LXX, the drink offering (<hi type="italic">spondē</hi>, from <hi type="italic">spendein</hi>) was a regular component of the sacrificial system. In Numbers 15:1-10, drink offerings of wine accompanied the burnt offerings and peace offerings. In Numbers 28:7, "Its drink offering shall be a quarter of a hin for each lamb. In the Holy Place you shall pour out a drink offering of strong drink to the LORD." In Exodus 29:40-41, the drink offering accompanied the daily sacrifice of lambs. The drink offering was poured out — it was not consumed by the offerer or returned to him but was given entirely, poured onto the altar or onto the ground before the LORD. It was a total, irreversible giving.</p>
          <p>Paul uses this specific sacrificial metaphor to describe his death. His life is the liquid being poured out; his death is the pouring. The present tense <hi type="italic">spendomai</hi> ("I am being poured out") indicates that the process is already underway — not a future possibility but a present reality. The adverb <hi type="italic">ēdē</hi> ("already, now, at this point") confirms the immediacy. Paul is not describing a distant prospect but a current experience. He used the same verb in Philippians 2:17: "Even if I am to be poured out as a drink offering (<hi type="italic">ei kai spendomai</hi>) upon the sacrificial offering of your faith, I am glad and rejoice with you all." In Philippians, the pouring was hypothetical (<hi type="italic">ei</hi>, "if"); here in 2 Timothy, it is actual (<hi type="italic">ēdē</hi>, "already"). The hypothesis of Philippians has become the reality of 2 Timothy.</p>
          <p>The sacrificial language reframes Paul's approaching death. He is not a victim of Roman justice; he is a priest offering the final sacrifice. His death is not a meaningless termination but a liturgical act — the last libation poured out to God. The image dignifies the death by placing it within the framework of Israel's worship.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And the time of my departure has come"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ho kairos tēs analseōs mou ephestēken</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> ("time, season, appointed moment") — the same word from verse 3 for the "time" when people will not endure sound teaching — now describes a different appointed moment: the time of Paul's departure. The noun <hi type="italic">analysis</hi> ("departure, loosing, unmooring, dissolution, release") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of <hi type="italic">ana</hi> ("up") + <hi type="italic">lysis</hi> ("loosing, releasing, untying"). The word was used for the loosing of a ship from its moorings before sailing, for the breaking of camp before a march, and for the releasing of a person from bondage. In Philippians 1:23, Paul used the related verb <hi type="italic">analysai</hi> ("to depart") when he spoke of his "desire to depart (<hi type="italic">analysai</hi>) and be with Christ, which is far better." The word does not describe death as extinction but as departure — movement from one location to another, the untying of what holds one in place.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">ephistanai</hi> (<hi type="italic">ephestēken</hi>, "has come upon, has arrived, stands near") is the perfect tense: the time has arrived and stands present. This is the same verb used in verse 2 (<hi type="italic">epistēthi</hi>, "stand ready, be at hand") where Timothy was commanded to be ready. Paul's departure stands at hand; Timothy must stand at hand for his work. The parallelism is structural: Paul's readiness to depart corresponds to Timothy's readiness to preach.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I have fought the good fight"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ton kalon agōna ēgōnismai</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">agōn</hi> ("contest, struggle, fight, conflict, athletic competition") describes the entire span of Paul's apostolic career under the image of an athletic or military contest. The adjective <hi type="italic">kalos</hi> ("good, noble, beautiful, excellent") — the same adjective used throughout the Pastoral Epistles for things worthy and honorable (the "good warfare" of 1 Timothy 1:18, the "good confession" of 1 Timothy 6:12-13, the "good deposit" of 2 Timothy 1:14, the "good soldier" of 2 Timothy 2:3) — marks this contest as noble. The verb <hi type="italic">agōnizesthai</hi> (<hi type="italic">ēgōnismai</hi>, perfect middle/passive, "I have competed, I have fought") in the perfect tense indicates a completed action with abiding results: the fight has been fought, and the result stands. In 1 Timothy 6:12, Paul commanded Timothy: "Fight the good fight of the faith" (<hi type="italic">agōnizou ton kalon agōna tēs pisteōs</hi>). What Paul commanded, Paul has done. He offers himself as the model: I fought the fight I told you to fight.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I have finished the race"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ton dromon teteleka</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">dromos</hi> ("course, race, career, running track") describes Paul's ministry under the image of a footrace. The same word appeared in Acts 13:25, where John the Baptist said, "What do you suppose that I am? I am not he. No, but behold, after me one is coming, the sandals of whose feet I am not worthy to untie," and this is introduced with "as John was finishing his course (<hi type="italic">ton dromon</hi>)." In Acts 20:24, Paul told the Ephesian elders: "I do not account my life of any value nor as precious to myself, if only I may finish my course (<hi type="italic">ton dromon mou</hi>) and the ministry that I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify to the gospel of the grace of God." The <hi type="italic">dromos</hi> is the assigned course — not a distance the runner chooses but a track laid out for him. Paul's commission determined his course; his faithfulness consisted in running it to the end.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">telein</hi> (<hi type="italic">teteleka</hi>, from <hi type="italic">telein/teleō</hi>, "to complete, to finish, to bring to an end, to accomplish") in the perfect tense indicates that the course has been completed — Paul has crossed the finish line. The word carries the sense of fulfillment, not merely cessation. He has not quit the race; he has finished it. The same verb's cognate appears in verse 5, where Timothy was told to <hi type="italic">plērophoresōn</hi> ("fulfill") his ministry. Paul has fulfilled his; Timothy must fulfill his.</p>
          <p>The athletic imagery connects directly to 2:5, where the athlete "is not crowned unless he competes according to the rules." Paul has competed according to the rules; therefore the crown awaits.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I have kept the faith"</hi> (<hi type="italic">tēn pistin tetērēka</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">tērein</hi> (<hi type="italic">tetērēka</hi>, "I have kept, I have guarded, I have maintained, I have observed") in the perfect tense describes the sustained guarding of something entrusted. The noun <hi type="italic">pistis</hi> ("faith, faithfulness, trust, the faith") is here best understood as "the faith" — the body of apostolic teaching, the deposit, the content of the gospel. This is the same <hi type="italic">pistin</hi> that Timothy was told to pursue (2:22), the same faith that Hymenaeus and Philetus upset in some (2:18), the same faith that was the goal of the "sincere faith" described in 1:5. Paul has kept what he was given. He has not altered, abandoned, diluted, or corrupted the deposit. The guard duty is complete.</p>
          <p>The three perfect tenses (<hi type="italic">ēgōnismai, teteleka, tetērēka</hi>) are cumulative: each describes a completed action with enduring results. The fight has been fought and remains won. The race has been finished and the course lies behind. The faith has been kept and stands guarded. Paul's life of service is presented as a finished work — not a partial effort or an interrupted project but a completed performance. The three statements correspond to the three metaphors of chapter 2: the soldier (fighting), the athlete (racing), and the farmer (whose labor produces results that endure). The soldier-athlete-farmer triad finds its autobiographical fulfillment in Paul's final testimony.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Henceforth there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness"</hi> (<hi type="italic">loipon apokeitai moi ho tēs dikaiosynēs stephanos</hi>). The adverb <hi type="italic">loipon</hi> ("henceforth, from now on, finally, for the remaining time") marks a transition from the completed past to the anticipated future. The verb <hi type="italic">apokeisthai</hi> ("to be laid up, to be stored away, to be reserved") describes something set aside in safekeeping — deposited and waiting for collection. In Colossians 1:5, Paul spoke of "the hope laid up (<hi type="italic">apokeimenen</hi>) for you in heaven." The crown is not uncertain; it is already stored and waiting. The certainty of the language matches the confidence of the statement "I know whom I have believed" (1:12). Paul knows his deposit is secure because he knows the one who holds it.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">stephanos</hi> ("crown, wreath, garland") is the victor's crown — the wreath awarded to the winner of an athletic competition. In 2:5, the athlete "is not crowned (<hi type="italic">stephanoutai</hi>) unless he competes according to the rules." Paul has competed according to the rules; therefore the crown is his. The <hi type="italic">stephanos</hi> was not a jeweled diadem (that would be <hi type="italic">diadēma</hi>) but a wreath of leaves — olive, laurel, celery, or pine, depending on the games. It was the visible mark of victory, worn proudly by the winner before the assembled spectators. The games of the Greek world were the most familiar competitive institution of the first-century Mediterranean, and the victor's crown was their most coveted prize.</p>
          <p>The genitive <hi type="italic">tēs dikaiosynēs</hi> ("of righteousness") describes the crown's nature or content. The phrase could mean "the crown that consists of righteousness" (a crown whose substance is righteousness), "the crown awarded for righteousness" (righteousness as the qualifying virtue), or "the crown that belongs to the righteous" (the crown reserved for those who lived rightly). All three meanings are compatible with the context. The crown is connected to righteousness — it is the righteous outcome that corresponds to the righteous life. The word <hi type="italic">dikaiosynē</hi> ("righteousness, justice, right standing") was one of the virtues Timothy was told to pursue (2:22: "pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace").</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me on that day"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hon apodōsei moi ho kyrios en ekeinē tē hēmera, ho dikaios kritēs</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">apodidonai</hi> ("to give back, to repay, to render, to award, to return what is owed") describes the crown as something earned and deserved — not a gift of arbitrary generosity but a just return for faithful service. The word carries a legal or commercial sense: payment rendered for work completed, a debt discharged. In Romans 2:6, God "will render to each one according to his works" (<hi type="italic">apodōsei hekastō kata ta erga autou</hi>). The judge renders the verdict and awards the prize.</p>
          <p>The title <hi type="italic">ho dikaios kritēs</hi> ("the righteous judge") identifies the Lord as a judge whose judgments are <hi type="italic">dikaios</hi> — "just, righteous, fair, in accordance with what is right." The assurance is not merely that Paul will receive the crown but that the judge who awards it is righteous. The system is fair. The judge cannot be bribed, deceived, or swayed. The outcome corresponds to the reality.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en ekeinē tē hēmera</hi> ("on that day") echoes the same expression in 1:12 (Paul's confidence that God can guard his deposit "until that day") and 1:18 (the prayer that Onesiphorus would find mercy "on that day"). "That day" is the day of Christ's return and judgment — the day when all hidden things are revealed, all faithful service is recognized, and all accounts are settled. The three occurrences of "that day" in the letter create a structural arc: Paul trusts God to guard his deposit until that day (1:12), prays for Onesiphorus to find mercy on that day (1:18), and expects to receive the crown on that day (4:8). The day is the horizon toward which the entire letter points.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And not only to me but also to all who have loved his appearing"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ou monon de emoi alla kai pasin tois ēgapēkosin tēn epiphaneian autou</hi>). The phrase <hi type="italic">ou monon... alla kai</hi> ("not only... but also") expands the promise beyond Paul to a universal scope. The crown is not Paul's private reward but the prize for every person who meets the stated condition. The condition is described with the perfect participle <hi type="italic">ēgapēkosin</hi> (from <hi type="italic">agapan</hi>, "to love") — "who have loved and continue to love" — applied to <hi type="italic">tēn epiphaneian autou</hi> ("his appearing"). The perfect tense indicates a love that began in the past and persists into the present: these are people whose love for Christ's appearing is a settled, enduring posture, not a momentary enthusiasm.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">epiphaneia</hi> ("appearing, manifestation") has now appeared three times in the chapter: in verse 1 (the charge is issued "by his appearing and his kingdom"), in verse 8 (the crown awaits those who love "his appearing"), and will appear implicitly in Paul's testimony of verse 17 (where the Lord's purpose is the full proclamation of the gospel). The first-century hearer would connect the love of Christ's appearing to the life of endurance and faithfulness the letter describes: to love his appearing is to live in orientation toward it, to conduct oneself as one who expects the judge to arrive, to value his kingdom above "this present world" (4:10). Those who love the appearing are those who endure (2:12), who are not ashamed (1:8, 12, 16), who do not deny (2:12), who keep the faith (4:7). Those who do not love the appearing are those who love "this present world" (4:10) — like Demas, whose story is about to follow.</p>
          <p>The expansion from "me" to "all" is structurally important. Paul's experience is not unique; it is paradigmatic. What is true for the apostle is true for every believer. The fight, the race, the faith, the crown, the righteous judge, that day — all are shared realities. Timothy, who is being charged to carry on Paul's work, stands among "all who have loved his appearing." The crown awaits him too, on the same terms.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.9-2Tim.4.13">
          <title>Verses 9-13: Personal Requests — Desertion, Companionship, and Practical Needs</title>
          <p>"Do your best to come to me soon. For Demas, in love with this present world, has deserted me and gone to Thessalonica. Crescens has gone to Galatia, Titus to Dalmatia. Only Luke is with me. Get Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for ministry. Tychicus I have sent to Ephesus. When you come, bring the cloak that I left with Carpus at Troas, also the books, and above all the parchments."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Do your best to come to me soon"</hi> (<hi type="italic">spoudason elthein pros me tacheōs</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">spoudazein</hi> ("to be diligent, to make every effort, to hasten, to be eager") is the same word used in 2:15, where Timothy was told to "do your best (<hi type="italic">spoudason</hi>) to present yourself to God as one approved." The urgency is personal: Paul wants Timothy to come quickly (<hi type="italic">tacheōs</hi>, "quickly, soon, without delay"). The request will be repeated in verse 21 with an added condition: "before winter." The double request frames the personal section and communicates both the urgency and the reason for it — Paul is alone, cold, and facing death.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For Demas, in love with this present world, has deserted me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Dēmas gar me egkatelipen agapēsas ton nyn aiōna</hi>). The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") explains why Timothy needs to come: Paul's companions have left. Demas is named first and with the most pointed description. The verb <hi type="italic">egkataleipein</hi> ("to abandon, to forsake, to desert, to leave behind") is a strong word. In the LXX, it describes God's potential abandonment of his people — Deuteronomy 31:6, "He will not leave you or forsake you (<hi type="italic">ou me se egkatalipē</hi>)"; Psalm 22:1 (LXX 21:2), "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me (<hi type="italic">hina ti egkatelipes me</hi>)?" — and the corresponding human abandonment of God. The word carries the weight of covenant betrayal: Demas has not merely relocated but has forsaken Paul.</p>
          <p>The reason is stated with devastating brevity: <hi type="italic">agapēsas ton nyn aiōna</hi> — "having loved this present age." The participle <hi type="italic">agapēsas</hi> (from <hi type="italic">agapan</hi>, "to love") uses the same verb just employed in verse 8 for those who "have loved his appearing" (<hi type="italic">ēgapēkosin tēn epiphaneian autou</hi>). The contrast is precise and deliberate. Those who love Christ's appearing receive the crown of righteousness. Demas loved the present age instead. The two loves are mutually exclusive: one cannot simultaneously love the appearing of the judge and love the world that the judge will judge. The noun <hi type="italic">aiōn</hi> ("age, era, world-order") with <hi type="italic">nyn</hi> ("now, present") describes the current world-order as opposed to the coming age. Demas chose the age that is passing over the age that is coming. His departure to Thessalonica is the physical expression of a spiritual decision.</p>
          <p>Demas appears in Colossians 4:14 alongside "Luke the beloved physician," and in Philemon 24 as one of Paul's "fellow workers." He was not a peripheral figure but a member of Paul's inner circle. His desertion is therefore not the departure of a casual acquaintance but the defection of a trusted co-worker. The pattern echoes 1:15, where "all who are in Asia turned away from me." The mass desertion described at the letter's beginning continues in its closing: Demas joins the long list of those who chose self-preservation over solidarity with the prisoner.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Crescens has gone to Galatia, Titus to Dalmatia"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Krēskēs eporethē eis Galatian, Titos eis Dalmatian</hi>). These departures are reported without the negative language used for Demas. The verb <hi type="italic">poreuein</hi> ("to go, to travel, to journey") is neutral — it describes travel without implying desertion. Crescens (mentioned only here in the New Testament) has gone to Galatia, and Titus (the recipient of the letter to Titus, a trusted co-worker) has gone to Dalmatia (the coastal region of the province of Illyricum, on the eastern shore of the Adriatic Sea). These may be legitimate ministry assignments rather than desertions; Paul does not accuse them as he accuses Demas. The effect, however, is the same: Paul is left with fewer and fewer companions.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Only Luke is with me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Loukas estin monos met' emou</hi>). The adverb <hi type="italic">monos</hi> ("only, alone") is placed for emphasis: Luke alone remains. Luke is identified in Colossians 4:14 as "the beloved physician" and appears as Paul's traveling companion throughout the latter portions of Acts (the "we" passages: Acts 16:10-17; 20:5-21:18; 27:1-28:16). His solitary presence at Paul's side in Rome, when all others have departed or been sent elsewhere, marks him as the one companion who did not leave.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Get Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for ministry"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Markon analabōn age meta seautou, estin gar moi euchrestos eis diakonian</hi>). The name <hi type="italic">Markos</hi> refers to John Mark, the figure who accompanied Paul and Barnabas on their first missionary journey but turned back at Pamphylia (Acts 13:13), causing a sharp disagreement between Paul and Barnabas over whether to take him on the second journey (Acts 15:37-39). The fact that Paul now wants Mark and describes him as <hi type="italic">euchrestos</hi> ("useful, serviceable, profitable") — composed of <hi type="italic">eu</hi> ("well") + <hi type="italic">chrēstos</hi> ("usable") — indicates a reconciliation and rehabilitation. The word <hi type="italic">euchrestos</hi> appeared in Philemon 11, where Paul described the formerly "useless" (<hi type="italic">achrestos</hi>) Onesimus as now "useful" (<hi type="italic">euchrestos</hi>). The adjective implies demonstrated competence. Mark has proven himself. The noun <hi type="italic">diakonia</hi> ("ministry, service") describes the practical gospel service for which Mark is needed.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Tychicus I have sent to Ephesus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Tychikon de apesteila eis Epheson</hi>). Tychicus was a trusted delegate. In Ephesians 6:21-22, he was described as "the beloved brother and faithful minister in the Lord" whom Paul sent to inform the Ephesians about his situation and "encourage your hearts." In Colossians 4:7-8, similar language describes his mission. In Titus 3:12, Paul planned to send either Artemas or Tychicus to Titus. The sending of Tychicus to Ephesus — where Timothy is stationed — may indicate that Tychicus is being sent as Timothy's replacement, freeing Timothy to come to Paul. Alternatively, he may be the carrier of this letter itself.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"When you come, bring the cloak that I left with Carpus at Troas"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ton phailonēn hon apelipon en Trōadi para Karpō erchomenos phere</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">phailonēs</hi> (Latin <hi type="italic">paenula</hi>, "cloak, heavy outer garment, traveling cloak") was a thick, circular garment designed for protection against cold and rain. Paul left it at Troas with a man named Carpus (mentioned only here). The request is achingly practical: the apostle who has just spoken of the crown of righteousness also needs his cloak. Winter is approaching (v. 21), and the prisoner is cold. The detail grounds the letter's lofty theology in physical reality. The man being poured out as a drink offering still has a body that feels cold.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Also the books, and above all the parchments"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ta biblia, malista tas membranas</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">biblion</hi> ("book, scroll, written document") in the plural (<hi type="italic">ta biblia</hi>) refers to papyrus scrolls — likely copies of the Scriptures of Israel, the "sacred writings" of 3:15. The noun <hi type="italic">membrana</hi> (a Latin loan word, "parchment, vellum") in the plural (<hi type="italic">tas membranas</hi>) refers to sheets or codices made of animal skin, which was more durable and expensive than papyrus. The adverb <hi type="italic">malista</hi> ("especially, above all, most of all") gives the parchments priority over the books. What the parchments contained is not specified, but the possibilities include: Paul's own writings or notes, copies of his letters, legal documents relevant to his trial, or portions of Scripture in parchment codex form. The request shows a mind still active, still engaged with texts, still needing intellectual and spiritual resources even as the body approaches death. The man who commanded Timothy to "preach the word" (v. 2) practices what he commands: even in prison, he wants his books.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.14-2Tim.4.15">
          <title>Verses 14-15: Alexander the Coppersmith — Harm and Warning</title>
          <p>"Alexander the coppersmith did me great harm; the Lord will repay him according to his deeds. Beware of him yourself, for he strongly opposed our message."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Alexander the coppersmith did me great harm"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Alexandros ho chalkeus polla moi kaka enedeixato</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">chalkeus</hi> ("coppersmith, metalworker, worker in bronze") identifies Alexander by his trade. The name Alexander is common, but this Alexander may be the same one mentioned in 1 Timothy 1:20, who along with Hymenaeus was "handed over to Satan that they may learn not to blaspheme." If so, the pedagogical discipline of 1 Timothy 1:20 has not produced reformation in Alexander's case, and he has actively harmed Paul.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">endeiknysthai</hi> ("to show, to demonstrate, to display, to inflict") with <hi type="italic">kaka</hi> ("evil things, harm, bad things") and the adverb <hi type="italic">polla</hi> ("many, much, great") describes extensive, demonstrated harm — not a single incident but a pattern of hostile action. The nature of the harm is not specified but may include testimony against Paul at his trial (connecting to the "first defense" of v. 16), active opposition to Paul's teaching, or deliberate undermining of Paul's work in the community.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The Lord will repay him according to his deeds"</hi> (<hi type="italic">apodōsei autō ho kyrios kata ta erga autou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">apodidonai</hi> ("to repay, to render, to give back what is owed") is the same verb used in verse 8 for the Lord's awarding of the crown — the righteous judge renders what is deserved. What the righteous judge awards to the faithful (the crown), the same righteous judge repays to the harmful (according to their deeds). The phrase <hi type="italic">kata ta erga autou</hi> ("according to his deeds") echoes the principle of Psalm 62:12 (LXX 61:13): "For you will render to each one according to his work" (<hi type="italic">sy apodōseis hekastō kata ta erga autou</hi>). In Proverbs 24:12 (LXX): "He who keeps your soul, does he not know it? And he will repay each according to his deeds." In Romans 2:6: "He will render to each one according to his works." The principle is consistent across the Scriptures: God judges according to deeds. Paul does not take vengeance himself; he commits the matter to the Lord. The language is neither vindictive nor detached — it is an expression of trust in the righteous judge's capacity to administer justice.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Beware of him yourself, for he strongly opposed our message"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hon kai sy phylassou, lian gar antestē tois hēmeterois logois</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">phylassein</hi> ("to guard, to watch, to be on guard against, to beware") in the middle voice (<hi type="italic">phylassou</hi>) commands Timothy to protect himself from Alexander. The adverb <hi type="italic">lian</hi> ("exceedingly, greatly, very much") intensifies the opposition: Alexander's resistance was not mild disagreement but vehement hostility. The verb <hi type="italic">anthistanai</hi> (<hi type="italic">antestē</hi>, "to resist, to oppose, to stand against, to withstand") is the same verb used in 3:8 for Jannes and Jambres, who "opposed (<hi type="italic">antestēsan</hi>) Moses." The parallel is telling: Alexander's opposition to the apostolic message is compared, through verbal echo, to the Egyptian magicians' opposition to God's appointed leader. The noun <hi type="italic">logois</hi> ("words, message, teaching") with the possessive <hi type="italic">hēmeterois</hi> ("our") refers to the apostolic gospel — "our message," the word Paul has been commanding Timothy to preach.</p>
          <p>The warning is practical: Alexander is dangerous, he is active, and Timothy must be aware of him. The advice is not to confront Alexander but to be on guard — the same defensive posture recommended throughout the letter for dealing with opponents who have proven resistant to correction.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.16-2Tim.4.18">
          <title>Verses 16-18: The First Defense — Desertion by All, Rescue by the Lord</title>
          <p>"At my first defense no one came to stand by me, but all deserted me. May it not be charged against them! But the Lord stood by me and strengthened me, so that through me the message might be fully proclaimed and all the Gentiles might hear it. So I was rescued from the lion's mouth. The Lord will rescue me from every evil deed and bring me safely into his heavenly kingdom. To him be the glory forever and ever. Amen."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"At my first defense no one came to stand by me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">en tē prōtē mou apologia oudeis moi paregeneto</hi>). The noun <hi type="italic">apologia</hi> ("defense, speech in one's own defense, legal defense") is the technical term for a defendant's speech at a Roman trial. The adjective <hi type="italic">prōtē</hi> ("first") implies that there may be a second defense yet to come — Roman legal procedure allowed for multiple hearings. The verb <hi type="italic">paraginesthai</hi> ("to come, to arrive, to be present, to come to the aid of") describes the failure of any supporter to appear. In Roman courts, the accused was expected to have advocates (<hi type="italic">patroni</hi>) and character witnesses (<hi type="italic">laudatores</hi>) who would speak in his favor, testify to his character, and demonstrate that he had social standing and support. The absence of any supporter (<hi type="italic">oudeis</hi>, "no one") was both legally and socially devastating. It meant Paul faced the Roman court entirely alone, without the network of relationships that constituted a person's social identity in the ancient world.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But all deserted me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">alla pantes me egkatelipon</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">egkataleipein</hi> ("to abandon, to forsake, to desert") is the same word used for Demas in verse 10. The universal scope — <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") — echoes the "all who are in Asia turned away from me" of 1:15. The theme of mass desertion forms an <hi type="italic">inclusio</hi> that brackets the entire letter: it opens with everyone in Asia turning away (1:15) and closes with everyone at the trial deserting (4:16). Paul's isolation is total.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"May it not be charged against them!"</hi> (<hi type="italic">mē autois logistheiē</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">logizesthai</hi> ("to reckon, to charge, to account, to count") in the optative (<hi type="italic">logistheiē</hi>) expresses a prayer or wish: may the desertion not be reckoned to their account, may it not be counted against them at the judgment. The negative <hi type="italic">mē</hi> ("not") with the optative creates a gentle, prayerful negative wish. The prayer echoes Stephen's dying words in Acts 7:60: "Lord, do not hold this sin against them" (<hi type="italic">kyrie, mē stēsēs autois tautēn tēn hamartian</hi>). It also echoes Jesus' prayer from the cross in Luke 23:34: "Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do." Paul, approaching his own death, follows the pattern of both Jesus and Stephen: he prays for mercy on those who failed him.</p>
          <p>The prayer is remarkable given the letter's stark treatment of desertion elsewhere. Demas is described without mercy: he "loved this present world" and deserted (v. 10). The faithful saying warned that those who deny Christ will be denied (2:12). Yet here Paul prays for those who deserted him at his trial. The distinction may be between deserting Paul (which is understandable human weakness under pressure) and denying Christ (which is a fundamental repudiation of allegiance). Paul himself drew this distinction in 1:15-16: those who turned away from Paul were described without invocation of divine wrath, while Onesiphorus was commended for not being ashamed. The desertion at the trial is a failure of courage, not necessarily a denial of Christ, and Paul's prayer asks that the failure not be held against them.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But the Lord stood by me and strengthened me"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ho de kyrios moi parestē kai enedynamōsen me</hi>). The adversative <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("but") introduces the contrast: no human stood by Paul, but the Lord did. The verb <hi type="italic">paristanai</hi> (<hi type="italic">parestē</hi>, "to stand beside, to come to one's aid, to be present") is the same verb that no human performed (<hi type="italic">paregeneto</hi>, v. 16) — but with the Lord as its subject. Where every person was absent, the Lord was present. The Lord did what the advocates and witnesses failed to do: he stood at Paul's side.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">endynamoun</hi> (<hi type="italic">enedynamōsen</hi>, "to strengthen, to empower, to fill with power") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:12, where Paul thanked Christ "who has given me strength" (<hi type="italic">tō endynamōsanti me Christō Iēsou</hi>), and in 2:1, where Timothy was told to "be strengthened in the grace that is in Christ Jesus" (<hi type="italic">endynamou en tē chariti</hi>). The verb runs from the opening of 1 Timothy to the closing of 2 Timothy, creating a thread of divine empowerment that spans the entire correspondence. The strength that Paul received at the beginning of his ministry (1 Timothy 1:12) is the same strength that sustained him at his trial (2 Timothy 4:17). The resource has not changed; the need has not diminished; the Lord who strengthens has not wavered.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that through me the message might be fully proclaimed and all the Gentiles might hear it"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hina di' emou to kērygma plērophorēthē kai akousōsin panta ta ethnē</hi>). The <hi type="italic">hina</hi> clause ("in order that, so that") states the purpose of the Lord's strengthening: not merely Paul's personal survival but the completion of his preaching mission. The noun <hi type="italic">kērygma</hi> ("proclamation, preaching, the thing preached, the message") is the content proclaimed by the <hi type="italic">keryx</hi> ("herald") — the same word family used in verse 2 (<hi type="italic">kēryxon</hi>, "preach") and 1:11 (<hi type="italic">kēryx</hi>, "preacher"). The verb <hi type="italic">plērophorein</hi> (<hi type="italic">plērophorēthē</hi>, "to be fulfilled completely, to be carried out fully") is the same verb used in verse 5, where Timothy was told to "fulfill" (<hi type="italic">plērophoresōn</hi>) his ministry. Paul's preaching was fulfilled — brought to completion, carried to its full measure — through the Lord's strengthening at the trial. The trial itself became an occasion for proclamation.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">panta ta ethnē</hi> ("all the Gentiles, all the nations") identifies the audience. Paul's commission was specifically to the nations — "I was appointed a preacher and apostle and teacher" (1:11); his gospel was for all peoples. The trial before the Roman court, attended by representatives of the empire's reach, became the venue through which "all the Gentiles" heard the gospel — not literally every individual Gentile, but the fullness of the Gentile mission that Paul's apostleship was commissioned to accomplish.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So I was rescued from the lion's mouth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai errysthēn ek stomatos leontos</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">rryesthai</hi> (<hi type="italic">errysthēn</hi>, "I was rescued, I was delivered") describes divine deliverance from mortal danger. In 3:11, Paul reminded Timothy: "the Lord rescued me from them all" (<hi type="italic">ek pantōn me errysato ho kyrios</hi>), referring to the persecutions at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra. The pattern of rescue is established across Paul's career: danger followed by divine deliverance.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ek stomatos leontos</hi> ("from the lion's mouth") draws on deep Scriptural imagery. In Psalm 22:21 (LXX 21:22): "Save me from the mouth of the lion" (<hi type="italic">sōson me ek stomatos leontos</hi>). The Psalm of the suffering righteous one — which Jesus himself quoted from the cross (Matthew 27:46; Mark 15:34, quoting Psalm 22:1) — contains the cry for deliverance from the lion's mouth. David's own testimony included rescue from lions (1 Samuel 17:34-37). Daniel was delivered from the lion's den (Daniel 6:22). The image of the lion as a figure for mortal danger, especially the danger of hostile power, is pervasive in Israel's literature. In the present context, the "lion's mouth" most naturally refers to the Roman court and its power to condemn Paul to death. Paul was rescued from that immediate threat — his first defense did not result in execution.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The Lord will rescue me from every evil deed"</hi> (<hi type="italic">rhysetai me ho kyrios apo pantos ergou ponērou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">rryesthai</hi> (<hi type="italic">rhysetai</hi>, future middle, "he will rescue") now shifts from the past (<hi type="italic">errysthēn</hi>, "I was rescued") to the future: the Lord will rescue. The shift from aorist to future is significant. The past rescue from the lion's mouth was a specific, historical deliverance. The future rescue is comprehensive: <hi type="italic">apo pantos ergou ponērou</hi> ("from every evil deed, from every wicked work"). The adjective <hi type="italic">ponēros</hi> ("evil, wicked, bad, harmful") describes works that are actively malicious. The Lord's rescue extends beyond the immediate danger to encompass every evil work that might threaten Paul.</p>
          <p>This future rescue must be understood in light of verse 6: Paul knows he is about to die ("I am already being poured out as a drink offering"). The rescue Paul anticipates is not rescue from death but rescue through death — deliverance not from physical danger but from evil itself. The Lord's rescue operates at a level deeper than physical survival. Paul may die, but the evil deeds of his opponents will not ultimately prevail. The Lord's faithfulness transcends the outcome of any trial.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And bring me safely into his heavenly kingdom"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai sōsei eis tēn basileian autou tēn epouranion</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">sōzein</hi> (<hi type="italic">sōsei</hi>, "he will save, he will bring safely, he will preserve") connects to the letter's opening, where God "saved us and called us to a holy calling" (1:9). The saving that began before the ages (1:9) will be consummated in the heavenly kingdom. The noun <hi type="italic">basileia</hi> ("kingdom") appeared in verse 1 as part of the charge: the charge was issued "by his appearing and his kingdom." The adjective <hi type="italic">epouranios</hi> ("heavenly, belonging to the heavens, celestial") locates this kingdom above — not the earthly kingdoms that imprison Paul but the heavenly kingdom that awaits him. The possessive <hi type="italic">autou</hi> ("his") identifies it as Christ's kingdom, the kingdom into which those who endure will be brought to reign (2:12).</p>
          <p>The movement from "rescue from every evil deed" to "bring safely into his heavenly kingdom" describes Paul's trajectory: through death, past evil, into glory. The death that approaches is not a defeat but a passage — from the present evil age (which Demas loved) to the heavenly kingdom (which Paul anticipates). The language fulfills the promise of the faithful saying: "if we have died with him, we will also live with him; if we endure, we will also reign with him" (2:11-12). Paul has endured; he will reign.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To him be the glory forever and ever. Amen."</hi> (<hi type="italic">hō hē doxa eis tous aiōnas tōn aiōnōn, amēn</hi>). The doxology — an ascription of glory to God — closes the theological section of the letter. The relative pronoun <hi type="italic">hō</hi> ("to him") refers to the Lord who stood by Paul, strengthened him, and will bring him safely into the heavenly kingdom. The noun <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory, splendor, honor, magnificence") ascribes supreme honor. The phrase <hi type="italic">eis tous aiōnas tōn aiōnōn</hi> ("unto the ages of the ages, forever and ever") is the strongest temporal expression available: ages upon ages, without end. The Hebrew-derived <hi type="italic">amēn</hi> ("truly, so be it, let it be confirmed") seals the doxology as a communal affirmation.</p>
          <p>The doxology is not decorative. It is the theological conclusion to Paul's final testimony. Having described his approaching death, his completed race, his awaiting crown, and the Lord's rescue, Paul directs all the resulting glory away from himself and toward the Lord. The glory belongs to God, not to Paul. The faith has been kept, but the keeper does not claim the honor; he ascribes it to the one who kept him.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4.19-2Tim.4.22">
          <title>Verses 19-22: Final Greetings, Last Instructions, and Closing</title>
          <p>"Greet Prisca and Aquila, and the household of Onesiphorus. Erastus remained at Corinth, and I left Trophimus, who was ill, at Miletus. Do your best to come before winter. Eubulus sends greetings to you, as do Pudens and Linus and Claudia and all the brothers. The Lord be with your spirit. Grace be with you."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Greet Prisca and Aquila"</hi> (<hi type="italic">aspasai Priskan kai Akylan</hi>). Prisca (also called Priscilla) and Aquila are well known from the narrative of Acts and from Paul's other letters. In Acts 18:2-3, Paul met them in Corinth; they were Jewish tentmakers who had been expelled from Rome by Claudius's edict. In Acts 18:18-19, they accompanied Paul to Ephesus. In Acts 18:26, they instructed Apollos "more accurately" in the Way of God. In Romans 16:3-5, Paul greeted them as "my fellow workers in Christ Jesus, who risked their necks for my life." In 1 Corinthians 16:19, they sent greetings from Asia along with "the church in their house." Their presence in Ephesus (if the greeting locates them there, which is likely since Timothy is in Ephesus) demonstrates their continued ministry in the region. Paul names Prisca first, as in Romans 16:3 — a detail that suggests her prominence in the partnership.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And the household of Onesiphorus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">kai ton Onēsiphorou oikon</hi>). Onesiphorus, whose faithfulness was celebrated in 1:16-18, is greeted here through his household (<hi type="italic">oikos</hi>). As noted in the analysis of 1:16-18, the greeting of the household rather than Onesiphorus personally may indicate that Onesiphorus is absent — still traveling, still in Rome with Paul, or possibly deceased. The text does not resolve the question, but the pattern of greeting "the household of Onesiphorus" rather than "Onesiphorus" is consistent with his absence from the household.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Erastus remained at Corinth"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Erastos emeinen en Korinthō</hi>). Erastus is likely the same person mentioned in Acts 19:22, where Paul "sent into Macedonia two of those who ministered to him, Timothy and Erastus." In Romans 16:23, "Erastus, the city treasurer (<hi type="italic">oikonomos tēs poleōs</hi>), greets you." An inscription found at Corinth reads "Erastus, in return for his aedileship, laid this pavement at his own expense" — possibly the same person. The verb <hi type="italic">menein</hi> (<hi type="italic">emeinen</hi>, "remained, stayed") is neutral: Erastus stayed at Corinth, where he apparently had responsibilities.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And I left Trophimus, who was ill, at Miletus"</hi> (<hi type="italic">Trophimon de apelipon en Milētō asthenonta</hi>). Trophimus was an Ephesian (Acts 21:29) and a companion of Paul (Acts 20:4). The participle <hi type="italic">asthenonta</hi> (from <hi type="italic">asthenein</hi>, "to be weak, to be ill, to be sick") explains why Trophimus was left behind: he was too ill to travel. The verb <hi type="italic">apoleipein</hi> (<hi type="italic">apelipon</hi>, "I left, I left behind") indicates Paul's decision to continue without him. The detail is striking in its ordinariness: a man who traveled with an apostle endowed with the gift of healing (Acts 19:11-12) was left behind sick. The text does not comment on why Trophimus was not healed; it simply reports the fact. Miletus was a port city about 30 miles south of Ephesus — the same city where Paul delivered his farewell address to the Ephesian elders (Acts 20:17-38).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Do your best to come before winter"</hi> (<hi type="italic">spoudason pro cheimōnos elthein</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">spoudazein</hi> ("to hasten, to be diligent") repeats the urgency of verse 9. The new element is the deadline: <hi type="italic">pro cheimōnos</hi> ("before winter"). The reason is practical. Sea travel in the Mediterranean essentially ceased during winter. The sailing season typically ended by mid-November, and the sea was considered closed until at least February or March. If Timothy does not come before winter, he will not be able to come until spring — and Paul may not survive until spring. The request for the cloak (v. 13) also reflects the approaching cold. The deadline sharpens every instruction in the letter: there is limited time, and the window is closing.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Eubulus sends greetings to you, as do Pudens and Linus and Claudia and all the brothers"</hi> (<hi type="italic">aspazetai se Euboulos kai Poudēs kai Linos kai Klaudia kai hoi adelphoi pantes</hi>). Four individuals and "all the brothers" send greetings to Timothy from Rome. Eubulus, Pudens, and Claudia appear nowhere else in the New Testament. Linus is identified by early church tradition (Irenaeus, <hi type="italic">Adversus Haereses</hi> 3.3.3) as a subsequent leader of the Roman community, though the text itself makes no such claim. The presence of "all the brothers" (<hi type="italic">hoi adelphoi pantes</hi>) indicates that Paul is not entirely without a community in Rome — despite the statement that "only Luke is with me" (v. 11), there are believers in the city who know Timothy and send greetings. The apparent tension between "only Luke is with me" and the greetings from multiple people may be resolved by understanding "only Luke is with me" to refer to Paul's regular companions and co-workers, while the greeters are members of the Roman community who are not Paul's personal attendants.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The Lord be with your spirit"</hi> (<hi type="italic">ho kyrios meta tou pneumatos sou</hi>). The closing benediction is addressed to Timothy personally. The noun <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> ("spirit") with the possessive <hi type="italic">sou</hi> ("your") refers to Timothy's inner person, his spirit. The prayer is that the Lord (<hi type="italic">ho kyrios</hi>) would be present with Timothy's spirit — accompanying, sustaining, and guiding him from within. The word <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> here corresponds to the "spirit not of cowardice but of power and love and self-discipline" that God gave (1:7). The Lord's presence with Timothy's spirit is the resource that makes everything else possible: the preaching, the suffering, the faithfulness, the completion of ministry.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Grace be with you"</hi> (<hi type="italic">hē charis meth' hymōn</hi>). The final word is <hi type="italic">charis</hi> — "grace," the free, unmerited favor of God. The pronoun <hi type="italic">hymōn</hi> ("you," plural) extends the benediction beyond Timothy to the community that will hear the letter read. The plural ending suggests that while the letter is addressed to Timothy, it was intended for a wider audience. The word <hi type="italic">charis</hi> frames the entire letter: it appeared in the greeting (1:2, "grace, mercy, and peace") and now closes the letter. Grace is the first word of God's engagement with his people and the last. The letter that began with the "promise of life that is in Christ Jesus" (1:1) ends with grace. The man who is about to be poured out as a drink offering commits his beloved child and the community to the grace of God.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="2Tim.4">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>2 Timothy 4 is the culmination of Paul's final letter — the chapter where the testamentary exhortation reaches its climax and the dying father delivers his last words to the son who must carry on alone.</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Verses 1-2</hi> issue the letter's most solemn charge. Under the witness of God and Christ Jesus — the one who will judge the living and the dead — and in view of Christ's appearing and kingdom, Timothy is commanded to preach the word. The charge encompasses the full range of pastoral speech: reproof, rebuke, exhortation, all conducted with complete patience and teaching. The charge gathers up every instruction in the letter and distills it into a single imperative: preach.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Verses 3-4</hi> provide the reason for the charge's urgency. A time is coming — and has already begun — when people will not tolerate sound teaching. They will accumulate teachers who scratch their itching ears, they will turn from the truth, and they will wander into myths. The progressive deterioration from rejection of health to embrace of falsehood describes the trajectory the letter has been tracking since the first mention of false teaching in 1 Timothy.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Verse 5</hi> counters with four commands directed specifically at Timothy: be sober-minded, endure suffering, do the work of an evangelist, fulfill your ministry. These commands correspond inversely to the four stages of deterioration in verses 3-4 and summarize the letter's entire vision of faithful service.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold">Verses 6-8</hi> contain Paul's final testimony — the emotional and theological climax of the letter. Paul describes his approaching death as a sacrificial offering (the drink offering being poured out) and a departure (the ship being untied from its moorings). Three perfect-tense verbs summarize his completed life: he fought the good fight, finished the race, kept the faith. The crown of righteousness awaits — awarded by the righteous judge on that day, not to Paul alone but to all who have loved Christ's appearing. The three completed actions correspond to the soldier, athlete, and farmer metaphors of chapter 2, now autobiographically fulfilled.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold">Verses 9-13</hi> turn to personal requests that ground the letter's theology in physical reality. Paul wants Timothy to come quickly because Demas has deserted, having loved this present world (the direct antithesis of loving Christ's appearing). Others have departed on legitimate missions. Only Luke remains. Paul wants Mark brought, Tychicus has been sent to Ephesus, and Paul needs his cloak, books, and parchments. The apostle who speaks of crowns and kingdoms also needs a coat for winter.</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold">Verses 14-15</hi> warn about Alexander the coppersmith, whose great harm to Paul will be repaid by the Lord according to his deeds. Timothy is warned to be on guard, because Alexander fiercely opposed the apostolic message.</p>
          <p>(7) <hi type="bold">Verses 16-18</hi> describe Paul's first defense in court, where no one stood with him and all deserted — but the Lord stood by him and strengthened him, so that the gospel might be fully proclaimed and all the Gentiles might hear it. Paul was rescued from the lion's mouth. He expresses confidence that the Lord will rescue him from every evil deed and bring him safely into the heavenly kingdom. The prayer that the deserters not be charged echoes Jesus' prayer from the cross and Stephen's dying prayer. The doxology — "To him be the glory forever and ever. Amen" — seals the testimony.</p>
          <p>(8) <hi type="bold">Verses 19-22</hi> close the letter with greetings, final travel notes, and the benediction. The greeting of Onesiphorus's household recalls the faithful man of 1:16-18. The request to come before winter adds the last note of urgency. The closing benedictions — "The Lord be with your spirit" and "Grace be with you" — commit Timothy and the community to the Lord's presence and God's grace.</p>
          <p>The chapter's controlling structure is the contrast between two orientations: love of the present age versus love of Christ's appearing. Demas loved the present age and deserted (v. 10). Paul and all the faithful love Christ's appearing and receive the crown (v. 8). The people who reject sound teaching choose teachers who suit their present passions (vv. 3-4). The Lord's servant preaches the word that prepares people for the coming judgment (vv. 1-2). Everything in the chapter — the charge, the warning, the testimony, the personal details, the benediction — is oriented toward "that day" (v. 8), the day when the righteous judge renders his verdict. The letter that began with "the promise of life that is in Christ Jesus" (1:1) ends with the confidence that the Lord "will bring me safely into his heavenly kingdom" (v. 18). The life promised at the beginning is the kingdom entered at the end.</p>
          <p>The chapter also completes the letter's pattern of exemplary pairs — faithfulness contrasted with desertion. Chapter 1 presented the desertion of all in Asia versus the faithfulness of Onesiphorus. Chapter 4 presents the desertion of Demas and all at the trial versus the faithfulness of the Lord who stood by Paul. The human examples fail progressively: in chapter 1, one man was faithful; in chapter 4, only the Lord himself stands by Paul. The trajectory of human desertion drives the reader toward the only reliable constant: the Lord who "remains faithful, for he cannot deny himself" (2:13). At the end of the letter, when every human support has been stripped away, the Lord alone stands — and that is sufficient.</p>
          <p>Paul's final testimony — "I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith" — is offered not as self-congratulation but as a model. Timothy is to fight the same fight (1 Timothy 6:12), run the same race (cf. the athlete of 2:5), and keep the same faith. The crown awaits Timothy on the same terms it awaits Paul: faithful endurance, legitimate competition, love of the appearing. The letter's last word to Timothy is not an instruction but a benediction: "The Lord be with your spirit. Grace be with you." The Lord who stood by Paul in the courtroom will stand by Timothy in the years ahead. The grace that sustained Paul through chains, desertion, and death will sustain Timothy through the ministry that lies before him. The deposit has been passed. The chain of transmission — Paul to Timothy to faithful people to others (2:2) — now depends on the next link.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
